WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.458
- So, welcome, everybody,

00:00:04.458 --> 00:00:07.571
and if I can call the public board meeting

00:00:07.571 --> 00:00:11.343
of NHS England to action.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.160
Welcome.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.080
We've got apologies for absence today

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.230
from Jeremy Townsend and Steve.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.317
Where are you, Steve?

00:00:20.317 --> 00:00:22.290
Steve, I know you've got to leave

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.140
in about just after five o'clock, I think,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.520
but thank you for being here.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.340
Can I just see if there are
any additional declarations

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.253
of interest that people
have since we last met?

00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:35.100
Mary?
- I've been appointed

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.550
to the special enquiry to preterm births.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.280
- Thank you. So, we'll note
that in your declarations.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.020
Thank you very much.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.730
Is the board happy to support,

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.365
and so I can of sign off the minutes

00:00:47.365 --> 00:00:49.110
of the meeting of 7th of December.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.810
- Yes. Yeah.
- Thank you.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:52.643
Thank you very much.

00:00:52.643 --> 00:00:57.440
So, let me just give a
few introductory remarks

00:00:58.920 --> 00:00:59.753
and I'll try and be brief,

00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:03.450
'cause actually, we've got
quite a packed agenda today

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.430
and I think each of these
topics are important

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.663
to deserve some real focus and discussion.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:11.867
One of the topics I'm
really looking forward to,

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:12.900
'cause we did this

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.080
at the Digital Data and
Technology Committee,

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:16.913
which of course actually

00:01:16.913 --> 00:01:18.700
has taken over the oversight challenge

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.030
that the NHS Digital
Board used to exercise.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.650
But actually, we had a
very good presentation.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.243
Where's Joe in the room here?

00:01:28.403 --> 00:01:31.320
So, we've got Joe and Helen here,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.940
Helen Clifton, Joe Harrison.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.607
And there is a presentation
later on the NHS app

00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:37.547
and I highlight that in particular,

00:01:37.547 --> 00:01:40.290
'cause I think it is transformational

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.240
and revolutionary actually

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.236
in terms of the way the NHS operates

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:47.403
and it has huge further potential.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:49.710
You'll know the numbers better than me,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.920
but it's 33 million or
so of the population,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.430
74% of the population,

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.570
or numbers like that now have the app,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.412
and clearly the interaction

00:01:59.412 --> 00:02:03.630
with citizens through the app into health.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.320
I think it's a brilliant new platform.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.610
So, we had a very good private
board meeting, I think,

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:16.610
and a series of, again very
good and really meaty topics.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.110
We looked as the board knows

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.330
that long-term infrastructure strategy,

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.070
long-term capital strategy,

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.600
very sensible to look long-term

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.300
as we did with the
long-term workforce plan.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.210
And within that long-term
infrastructure review,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.820
we also looked within that
as one of the components,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.500
which is the new hospitals programme

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.260
and had a very good update on that.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.770
And again, staying on
long-term looks forward.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.260
Again, Navina, you gave
us a very good update

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.313
on continued implementation progress

00:02:45.313 --> 00:02:48.300
with the long-term workforce plan.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.300
Again, we had a bit of a
think about primary care,

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.311
just looking at where
primary care is operating

00:02:55.311 --> 00:02:59.104
and potential future roles

00:02:59.104 --> 00:03:02.250
and ways of operating for primary care.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.630
And then, we had a very good conversation,

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.638
Amanda Doyle, led by
you again on dentistry

00:03:06.638 --> 00:03:10.200
and reform and recovery of dentistry.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.593
So, some very good and meaty topics there.

00:03:14.976 --> 00:03:17.483
Let me just then make some other comments.

00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:21.030
And not to take Amanda Pritchard's thunder

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.650
and for the team,

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.300
but I would quickly like to comment

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.040
on winter,
- Mm-hmm.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.040
- and just to say that

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:29.873
the performance this winter

00:03:29.873 --> 00:03:31.950
and is not over yet

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.710
was so markedly better than last winter.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:38.160
But in saying that, not at all complacent,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.950
there's still further, much further to go.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.290
But actually, as we now enter February,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.760
actually the performance in winter

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.094
and we'll have an update on it later on,

00:03:47.094 --> 00:03:52.094
I think is a credit to the
huge hard work of the workforce

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.740
and actually the fact that
early planning was put in place

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.870
and very targeted
interventions were put in place

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.130
and collaboration across ICBs
and systems into providers

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:05.550
and into primary operated really well.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.280
So, there's still much,
much further to go.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.110
But actually, the performance
this current winter

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.100
on average roughly twice,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.170
twice a better performance

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.753
than we saw in the previous year.

00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:24.644
I'd quite like to just make,
if I can, an announcement.

00:04:24.644 --> 00:04:26.790
It was actually announced
slightly early today

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.230
and the board probably

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.700
haven't had a chance yet to pick it up.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:30.600
But today, we launched

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.900
the national BRCA gene testing programme,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.600
which identifies cancer risk early.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:37.980
And there are tens of thousands of people

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.500
with Jewish ancestry who are more likely

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.280
to carry this genetic fault
that can increase the risk

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.530
of developing certain cancers.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.110
And they actually are going
to receive genetic testing

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.060
under this new programme.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.340
We have plans to identify
thousands more people

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:58.340
who carry faults in the BRCA
genes over the next two years,

00:04:58.496 --> 00:05:00.840
so that they can seek early access

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.110
to surveillance and monitoring
and prevention services.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:07.328
And that testing programme is
part of the NHS' major drive

00:05:07.328 --> 00:05:11.310
to catch tumours early when
they are much easier to treat.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.143
And there's an announcement

00:05:12.143 --> 00:05:13.740
I think has gone out earlier today,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.960
but I do want to just
make that announcement

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.143
here on the public board.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:25.190
In terms of other topics,
I attended yesterday

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.180
and I referenced an earlier conversation

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.790
we had in the private board
session on primary care.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.130
I had the great pleasure
yesterday of attending

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.680
an all day session

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.120
with a number of a significant
number of GP leaders.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.770
And it is the first GP leadership
event hosted by the NHS.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.980
And it was hosted in
person by Claire Fuller

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:50.733
and Amanda Doyle and Navina Evans.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.870
It really was a very good engagement

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:59.340
and we did four breakouts in the day

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.140
and everyone engaged on each of those.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:04.770
But it won't surprise you
that they are estates,

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.420
which is quite, I often described

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.470
as a sort of a Gordian knot
issue within primary care.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.041
We looked at primary, secondary interface,

00:06:13.041 --> 00:06:14.130
how that operates,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:18.060
how we might be able to
think about its improvement.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.430
We absolutely looked at GP retention,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.440
which is clearly a very
important conversation

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.770
to have that discussion with
the leadership in the room,

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.280
the quality of the leadership
in the room was excellent.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.280
And then, we also looked at
patient safety in primary care.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:35.874
The first event of its type

00:06:35.874 --> 00:06:40.171
for that GP work, that GP cohort.

00:06:40.171 --> 00:06:42.810
We are going to hold
that a couple of times,

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:44.670
two or three times a year.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.720
We'll probably also look

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.640
to do that again in pharmacy
and also in dentistry.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.040
But going back to the GP cohort,

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.050
the thing I would be most excited about

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.680
is then getting that GP
voice into the gatherings,

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.510
into the seminars and meetings

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.857
when we bring together providers

00:07:02.857 --> 00:07:06.693
or other groups across the NHS.

00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:09.750
Now, I know this happens
in the systems in the ICBs.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:10.620
That's very good.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.473
It also helps, I think, to
get that community of voices,

00:07:14.621 --> 00:07:18.183
getting after particular issues together.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.030
I'll only reference two or
three meetings that I've had.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.610
I visited Guy's and St. Thomas',

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.647
actually with the deputy
PM in mid-January.

00:07:30.647 --> 00:07:32.700
And we were looking at their genomics

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.540
and bio-security and
Vin, you were with me.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.839
It was a really interesting
and high-quality visit.

00:07:39.839 --> 00:07:42.120
It helped me, 'cause
on the 29th of January,

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.880
I attended my first Genomics England Board

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.890
where I've now I've joined that board.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.170
And again, we think about
whole genome sequencing,

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:50.670
the leadership we have internationally

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.380
or globally in whole genome.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.760
Actually, it's really good

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.430
and I'm delighted that
I've joined that board.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.660
I also had made the point

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.280
that whilst that's really important

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.638
and we must drive and
support it as hard as we can,

00:08:02.638 --> 00:08:05.220
we sometimes forget that actually,

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.530
the whole genome sequencing work

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.900
is less than 10% of the genomics activity

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.360
that's carried out across
the whole of the NHS.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.880
And so, actually,
thinking about going back

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.020
to the NHS app actually,

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.840
levels of innovation, transformation.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:19.950
We see it in the app,

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.690
we also see it in actually genomics

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:26.100
and in our NHS England
genomics activities.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.930
We now, I think, last year, Amanda,

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:31.427
we did what, I think we
tested over 700,000 patients.

00:08:31.427 --> 00:08:32.910
For the minute, someone should probably

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:34.827
square bracket that number and check it,

00:08:34.827 --> 00:08:35.850
but I think we tested

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:39.354
over 700,000 patients

00:08:39.354 --> 00:08:41.970
- and we scanned for 200 cancers

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.730
and over 3,000 rare
diseases is my memory of it.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:47.700
So, it's a remarkable transformation

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.683
in the offer of the NHS, of
services to the population.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.170
Coming up on the 28th of February,

00:08:55.170 --> 00:09:00.170
we've got an event in person
of ICB and trust chairs.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:02.970
Again, it's really good

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.580
to bring those two communities together.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.550
They work closer together
in their systems,

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:09.813
but actually bring them all together

00:09:09.813 --> 00:09:13.140
with NHS England, I think, is important.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.110
And then, lastly, we're doing some work

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.328
in looking at the leadership academy.

00:09:18.328 --> 00:09:21.270
We're just looking at
actually current offering,

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.190
how it engages with particular cohorts

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:26.580
of echelons of NHS staff,
NHS managers, and so on.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.530
So, we're just doing a refresh
on the leadership academy.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.450
I think that's really good.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.280
I had the pleasure of a meeting

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.380
with Henrietta Hughes, the
patient safety commissioner.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:35.370
Very good engagement.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.110
She's a huge advocate clearly

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.476
for patient safety and we
had a very good exchange.

00:09:39.476 --> 00:09:41.160
And also, Amanda, you and I met

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.090
with Sir John Simmons and Sir John Bell,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:45.923
which was really good.

00:09:45.923 --> 00:09:48.480
But they'd written a good punchy letter

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.840
on Office of Life Sciences'
strategy looking forward.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.100
And so, we had a very good engagement

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.670
with them around that strategy.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.950
So, I won't...

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.786
I'll pause if anyone wants
to ask me any questions,

00:10:01.786 --> 00:10:04.677
but I'm not trying to
encourage that on my report.

00:10:04.677 --> 00:10:07.162
But if you'd like to,
as always, please do.

00:10:07.162 --> 00:10:10.953
But let me hand over to
Amanda for the CEO report.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:14.940
- Great, thank you. Thank you
very much indeed, Richard.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:18.540
So, I've just got a
number of things to cover,

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.210
because as ever, there's a lot going on.

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:25.350
Just to start with a word if
I may, on industrial action,

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:29.520
because of course, we knew
that the industrial action plan

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.820
on either side of Christmas

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:32.853
and New Year was going to be tough,

00:10:32.853 --> 00:10:35.819
particularly coinciding with
that early January period,

00:10:35.819 --> 00:10:38.640
where there's often an increase in demand

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:39.870
and pressure on the NHS.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.528
So, I really do want to
start by thanking colleagues

00:10:42.528 --> 00:10:46.650
in EPR teams up and down the
country, including NHS England

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.690
and colleagues across the NHS,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.936
who really did pull out all the stops

00:10:50.936 --> 00:10:54.032
to try and maintain services and safety.

00:10:54.032 --> 00:10:57.630
As we know though, protecting
urgent emergency care services

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.571
does come at the expense of planned care,

00:11:00.571 --> 00:11:02.520
particularly at that time of the year.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:03.960
And since strikes began,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.730
the cumulative total of appointments

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:09.030
that have been rescheduled
is now over 1.3 million.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.801
As ever, that is 1.3 million people

00:11:11.801 --> 00:11:15.480
who have had to have
their care rescheduled

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.730
and an awful lot of work that
goes on in the background

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:20.070
to make sure that those
appointments are rebooked.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.980
So, whilst I think we would recognise

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:25.890
that the omens don't look
good, we would all hope,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:28.741
I think, that further
action can be avoided.

00:11:28.741 --> 00:11:31.500
I know we're going to come
on to talk about winter

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.100
in detail in a moment, but worth saying

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:36.600
and exactly as you've just said, Richard,

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.400
that despite the massive disruption

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.440
that industrial action has caused,

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.657
the work that we have been doing

00:11:42.657 --> 00:11:45.090
and the teams across the country
have been doing this year

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.940
as part of delivering
the urgent emergency care

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.585
recovery plan really
has made a difference.

00:11:50.585 --> 00:11:53.330
So, we have achieved good progress

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.020
on all of the major deliverables,

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:57.150
including reaching the milestone

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:01.110
of having 5,000 more core beds open.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:02.651
And that again,

00:12:02.651 --> 00:12:04.890
we can see has made

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:07.740
a real difference at this
point of peak pressure

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.170
in helping maintain
flow through the system.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.380
In A&amp;E, 1.5 million more
people were seen treated

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:14.790
and either discharged, transferred,

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:19.333
or admitted within four
hours in 2023 than in 2022.

00:12:19.333 --> 00:12:20.596
A four-hour performance

00:12:20.596 --> 00:12:23.820
has been better in every month of the year

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:25.050
that's just finished, the calendar year

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:27.180
that's just finished,
than in the previous year.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.610
The same is true for category
one and category two.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.860
Ambulance calls with
category two response times

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:35.930
in December, 50% lower
than in December, 2022,

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:40.920
and that is despite an
11% increase in incidents.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.930
To your point though,
Richard, it's really important

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:44.760
that we say this, whilst I think

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:48.007
we can take real encouragement
from some of those statistics

00:12:48.007 --> 00:12:50.861
and that is no doubt better for patients,

00:12:50.861 --> 00:12:52.950
that is still a long way to go.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:53.793
- Yeah.
- And I think the other thing

00:12:53.793 --> 00:12:55.050
that we would really acknowledge

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:56.640
is the pressure on our staff,

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.859
which people are feeling
up and down the country.

00:12:58.859 --> 00:13:01.650
And when I talk about
urgent emergency care,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.110
I always mean mental health
as well as physical health.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:06.060
But I think it's worth
making that point explicitly,

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:06.960
because we do know

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:10.200
that the pressures in inpatient
mental health services

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:14.822
and through our urgent
emergency care services

00:13:14.822 --> 00:13:17.790
are equally acute across mental health

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:20.490
and physical health care settings.

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.400
So, a huge thank you to
all of the staff in the NHS

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:25.710
who again, working under enormous pressure

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:29.340
to do the best they possibly
can for patients as ever.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:30.420
Pharmacy First.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:34.800
So, just on another really
important announcement

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.027
that we've just made,

00:13:36.027 --> 00:13:40.169
the launch of Pharmacy First was something

00:13:40.169 --> 00:13:43.542
that we talked about back
when the urgent emergency care

00:13:43.542 --> 00:13:48.542
recovery plans published a year
ago is now come into being,

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.850
which means that as of yesterday,

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.700
you can walk into a High Street Pharmacy

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:57.120
to get treatment and
prescription medicines

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:58.560
or medicines that you would

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.290
otherwise normally need to see a GP for

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:02.149
in order to get a prescription for.

00:14:02.149 --> 00:14:03.960
So, you can get those medicines directly

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.043
walking into a High Street Pharmacy

00:14:06.043 --> 00:14:08.940
and over 10,000 pharmacies in the country.

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.070
That's more than 9 in 10 pharmacies

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:12.651
that have signed up to the scheme.

00:14:12.651 --> 00:14:14.040
And what we hope

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:17.610
is that we'll both take
some pressure off GPs

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:19.438
and off GP teams,

00:14:19.438 --> 00:14:22.800
but also just provide
other options for patients

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.050
that are more convenient for some people

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.960
because 8 in 10 people
live within 20 minutes

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:29.070
of a High Street Pharmacy

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:31.410
and opening hours are
often up 'til midnight

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:33.000
and certainly weekends as well.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.118
So, it's an important step

00:14:35.118 --> 00:14:40.118
in what has been a continued
series of investments

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.600
and developments in community services.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:45.090
So, the expansion of
blood pressure checks,

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.090
the expansion of oral
contraceptive services

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:49.331
in community pharmacy,

00:14:49.331 --> 00:14:51.030
and we are looking forward to continuing

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:54.660
to do more of that as time goes on,

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:55.740
both in pharmacy,

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:58.623
but also community
services more generally.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.100
So, really wanted

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:03.810
to just draw the board's
attention to that.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.810
I suspect they did notice it yesterday.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:10.686
Just a few more things
for me, less positively.

00:15:10.686 --> 00:15:12.379
It is just worth the board

00:15:12.379 --> 00:15:14.700
being aware that since we last met,

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.341
a significant development has
been the measles outbreak,

00:15:18.341 --> 00:15:21.030
which has been declared
in the West Midlands

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:23.670
with Birmingham in particular affected.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:26.370
Steve may well want to
say more about this,

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.500
but Steve and his team have
been working really hard

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:30.704
to support the UKHSA-led response,

00:15:30.704 --> 00:15:33.270
including standing up a recall campaign

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:34.290
with millions of letters,

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:37.560
inviting parents to catch
up on their child's jabs,

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:42.560
but also lots of support for
local initiatives to make sure

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.395
that getting those catch-up jabs

00:15:44.395 --> 00:15:48.750
is as convenient and easy
as possible for parents.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.070
Other things of note.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.900
So, we've continued to make progress

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.060
on delivering the
long-term workforce plan.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.820
A couple of weeks ago, we
launched the Emeritus scheme

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:58.950
for retired consultants
to return to the NHS

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:00.807
to help deliver elective appointments

00:16:00.807 --> 00:16:03.060
and that includes remote appointments.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.600
So, again, trying to make work flexible

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:08.550
and keep people who want to continue

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:11.100
to offer their expertise to the NHS

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.020
to keep them working with us,

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.120
but in ways that work for them as well.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.830
We've also continued to grow the workforce

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:19.980
with a record number
of nurses and midwives

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:22.170
and health visitors in post in November.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.680
That's an increase of
over 20,000 in a year.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:27.750
So, a huge congratulations to Ruth

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:31.050
and her team who've worked
very hard to support staff

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:34.080
both entering the profession
and staying in it.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:35.820
We also saw, and again,

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.710
I know Emily will talk about this more,

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:39.510
but wanted to draw the
board's attention to the fact

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:41.850
that we also saw the
elective waiting list fall

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:44.831
for the second month in a row in November.

00:16:44.831 --> 00:16:46.560
Thanks particularly to a break

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.660
in industrial action over the autumn,

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:50.820
as well as those continued actions

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.530
that colleagues are taking
across the health service

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:55.500
with everything from the
increase in robotic surgery

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:58.110
to thinking really creatively about things

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:01.260
like high impact theatre
lists and other innovations

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:02.220
to make sure that patients

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.230
can be seen as quickly as possible.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.450
So, again, lots more to do,

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:09.240
but a real sense of I think,
encouragement that the actions

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.910
that colleagues are taking
are making a difference.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:14.418
Couple more things
before I finish, Richard.

00:17:14.418 --> 00:17:17.460
I know we're going to talk
about mental health later

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:21.900
and Claire and Liz are here
to lead us in that discussion.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:26.130
But just wanted to note the
importance of that conversation

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:28.680
and it would be important anyway.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.150
But particularly, in light

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.610
of the tragic events in Nottingham

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:36.990
and also the report into the
really shocking behaviour

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:41.990
and practise that we saw at
the Edenfield Centre last year,

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:44.640
that report's just come out.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.520
I think it really does
just sharpen the mind

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:48.584
and sharpen the focus

00:17:48.584 --> 00:17:51.690
on why in all the
conversation that we've had

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:53.306
and we'll continue to have about access,

00:17:53.306 --> 00:17:57.303
about funding, about prevention,
about all of the things

00:17:57.303 --> 00:18:00.120
that we know matter, actually
just reminding us all

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:04.560
that quality is the thing that
we also are here to make sure

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:05.670
that we are supporting people

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.440
to deliver the highest quality of care,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:08.670
experience, and safety.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:09.772
It is just as vital

00:18:09.772 --> 00:18:12.300
as all of the other things
that we've mentioned.

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:15.690
So, making sure that when
people need the support

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:18.480
and help of health services,
they get that in a way

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:20.520
that's patient-focused, respectful,

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.290
as close to home as possible,

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:24.193
that involves the family
in an appropriate way.

00:18:24.193 --> 00:18:26.697
And I know the team feel
incredibly passionate about that

00:18:26.697 --> 00:18:29.013
and we'll talk about that a bit later.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.243
Finally, just on our own organisation,

00:18:32.243 --> 00:18:36.000
so we did achieve our aim of 95%

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.850
of staff within NHS England having surety

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.700
of their roles in the new
organisation by December.

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:44.805
That was an important commitment
we made to our own staff

00:18:44.805 --> 00:18:47.040
and there is now a programme

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:50.760
of work going on to ensure
that this final stage

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:53.250
of restructure stage
three takes place again.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:55.380
And Steve may want to say more about that.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.256
My thanks as ever, not just
to my executive colleagues

00:18:58.256 --> 00:19:00.450
for their continued leadership

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:03.390
through what has been an
incredibly challenging

00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:06.300
and disruptive period
for the organisation,

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:08.700
but for all of our
colleagues in NHS England

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:11.850
who've had to live
through a year or more now

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:16.850
of just a really
challenging period in terms

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:21.990
of just personal futures and
their place in our organisation

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:25.058
and have continued to give 100% focus

00:19:25.058 --> 00:19:28.170
to their roles to supporting the NHS

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.360
and the professionalism
with which colleagues

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:31.320
have conducted themselves,

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:33.499
I think never ceases to impress me.

00:19:33.499 --> 00:19:35.820
So, there is now a
programme of work going on

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:40.110
to harmonise policies across
the three organisations.

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:41.730
And in light of that,

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.160
we have now communicated the decision

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.290
taken on hybrid working.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.900
So, we're developing resources
to support individuals,

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.910
line managers, and teams to discuss

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:54.330
how they will implement
hybrid working in their teams.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.930
And I think we recognise
that whilst there clearly

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.600
are positives to remote working,

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:03.750
there are also positives
to in-person working.

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:06.873
And the balance we have struck
is we think the right one.

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.337
- Amanda, thank you.

00:20:09.337 --> 00:20:10.650
I'm going to open up, so
if there are any comments

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:12.540
or questions to Amanda

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:16.920
on anything she has just commented upon.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:20.130
I did forget, just to mention
a couple of other things,

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.073
if I might just reverse
back into a chair's update

00:20:23.073 --> 00:20:26.040
and the first, actually, we
had a conversation this morning

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:28.890
and Wol started doing quite a lot of work

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:31.639
and thinking in this
space, a number of us also

00:20:31.639 --> 00:20:33.420
with Wol trying to do the same thing,

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:36.030
which actually is about how do we grow

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:40.560
and deepen the pipeline
of candidates for roles

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.510
as non-executives or chairs

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:44.940
on our trust,

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:46.440
foundation trust and so forth.

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:50.224
And we have a very high demand

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:54.660
for truly able chairs and
non-executive directors.

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:57.090
And I think we need to
reinvigorate that pipeline

00:20:57.090 --> 00:21:00.749
and see if we can get more
people to come forward

00:21:00.749 --> 00:21:03.540
and take on what are
really very demanding,

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:04.590
enjoyable at times,

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:07.560
but also very demanding roles.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:08.730
The second thing I should have said,

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:10.257
and I was trying to look behind me

00:21:10.257 --> 00:21:11.710
as Amanda was talking, to see

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:13.950
if there were any pictures of Norwich.

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:14.783
Actually, what I should
- Yes.

00:21:14.783 --> 00:21:15.900
- have said, of course, actually,

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.280
is clearly we are here

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:20.250
holding our board meeting in Norwich.

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:25.103
So, as guests actually,
Norfolk and Waveney ICB,

00:21:25.103 --> 00:21:27.360
and clearly we're joined by Nick Hulme

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:29.220
and Tracey Bleakley who
will be commenting later on,

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:30.620
I think, in the performance,

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:36.138
the performance update on
winter and our delivery there.

00:21:36.138 --> 00:21:38.250
So, we acknowledge as part of a programme

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:41.130
where the board does
not just meet in London,

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:42.360
which would be wholy inappropriate,

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:43.980
but actually we do whole board meetings

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:44.970
elsewhere around the country.

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:47.523
So, today, we are in Norwich.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:53.960
Any comments to Amanda on
anything she said? Any questions?

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:56.040
Should we get into the agenda?

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.000
I think we're heading for
the agenda. Okay, very good.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.100
Emily, I think we want
operational performance update.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:03.840
Remember of course that
there's a separate item

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:05.940
on winter planning and delivery,

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:08.970
but operational update
would be really helpful.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:10.113
- I'll do my best.

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:14.040
So, thank you to Amanda

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.140
having given some of
these headlines already.

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:17.190
So, I'll try

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:20.299
and give the appropriate
angle on them for the report.

00:22:20.299 --> 00:22:22.207
So, as always, the board paper

00:22:22.207 --> 00:22:25.302
presents the NHS' performance
in November and December,

00:22:25.302 --> 00:22:27.990
because of the timing
of publication of data.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:28.950
So, be aware of that.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.350
Compared to other statistics
that are out there.

00:22:32.351 --> 00:22:35.370
Our position would be that
the pressure has remained high

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:38.100
on urgent emergency care
services over the winter

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:39.262
and it's been exacerbated

00:22:39.262 --> 00:22:42.360
by the combination of not
only usual winter pressures,

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:43.260
but also three days

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:46.050
of junior doctors industrial
action in December.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:47.370
And obviously, there was more in January,

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.443
which we can look at the following board.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:52.890
December in particular, was
a challenging month for UEC

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:54.450
and we saw rising demand

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:57.270
with 5.7% more emergency admissions

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:59.307
compared to the same time last year.

00:22:59.307 --> 00:23:02.670
And I thought it was helpful
to give some initial dimensions

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.181
which are in the board paper.

00:23:04.181 --> 00:23:05.520
So, it gives you the headline

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:10.520
that ambulances answered 889,389
999 calls during December.

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:12.420
And if you work it out,

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.311
that averages out at just
under 20 calls a minute.

00:23:15.311 --> 00:23:17.010
And by the same calculation,

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.080
they're on average eight and
a half ambulance arrivals

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:20.640
at A&amp;E every minute similarly.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:24.663
So, you get a sense of just
that ongoing volume of activity.

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.796
Despite this, we had
four-hour performance at 69.4%

00:23:28.796 --> 00:23:30.810
and category two performances.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:34.230
Amanda and Rich I think have
both mentioned at 45 minutes,

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:37.230
57 seconds, which are
both substantively better

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:41.070
than December 22, where
A&amp;E performance was 65.2%

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.010
and category two was 1 hour, 32 minutes.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:46.710
So, this is thanks to actions the system

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:48.452
has taken from the UEC recovery plan,

00:23:48.452 --> 00:23:50.850
coupled with significant preparation

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:52.380
and management for industrial action

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:54.180
from colleagues in systems

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:57.183
and the whole healthcare
system across the country.

00:23:58.341 --> 00:24:01.020
Despite the continued challenges of IA,

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:02.690
we have made substantive progress

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:03.960
in some of our other targets,

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.210
particularly those to reduce long waits.

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:08.040
So, for example, the overall waiting list

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.110
has decreased in October and November,

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:13.890
so it reduced by 63,000
between September and October.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:18.113
And by 95,600 between
October 23 and November.

00:24:18.113 --> 00:24:21.698
And the total listed at
7.6 million in November.

00:24:21.698 --> 00:24:23.880
104-week waits increased marginally

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:28.880
while 78 weeks did increase
by 662, which is a level

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:32.100
that we are concerned
about in taking action on.

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:37.100
Whereas 65-week weights decreased
significantly by 12,870.

00:24:37.737 --> 00:24:42.737
We also saw increased
activity in other areas,

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:45.260
not just in UEC, so on cancer,

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:47.520
urgent suspected cancer referrals

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:51.474
first seen at almost 128%
above pre-pandemic levels.

00:24:51.474 --> 00:24:54.894
And it's helpful sometimes
to look behind the data.

00:24:54.894 --> 00:24:57.030
So, I thought the board might want to note

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:58.230
that in the two days following

00:24:58.230 --> 00:24:59.550
the Duchess of York's announcement

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:01.800
that she's been diagnosed
with malignant melanoma,

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:03.900
we saw more than eight
times the number of visits

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:05.880
to the melanoma skin information page

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:08.851
on the NHS website compared
to the previous week.

00:25:08.851 --> 00:25:10.770
So, obviously, specifically on this one,

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:12.870
but more broadly, we are all very aware

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:15.000
that diagnosing cancer early saves lives.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.454
So, we would always prefer

00:25:16.454 --> 00:25:19.110
that if anyone is worried
about their symptoms

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.615
that they do present
sooner rather than later,

00:25:21.615 --> 00:25:24.363
so that we can take the
right diagnostic action.

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:27.750
On diagnostics more broadly,
our published statistics

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:30.711
show we're delivering
record diagnostic activity

00:25:30.711 --> 00:25:33.754
115% above pre-pandemic levels.

00:25:33.754 --> 00:25:37.530
And each month in '23, '24
has seen higher activity

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:40.321
than the same month in any previous year.

00:25:40.321 --> 00:25:43.380
So, overall, we would say the
service has been coping well

00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:45.081
despite winter pressures in IA,

00:25:45.081 --> 00:25:48.650
but recent weeks' data show the
strain the service is under.

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:51.144
So, if we look at how
stretched capacity is,

00:25:51.144 --> 00:25:54.420
a latest weekly winter data
shows there was an average

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:59.070
of 2,226 patients with flu in
hospital every day last week,

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:00.900
which is up 70% in a month

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:03.800
and three quarters higher than
the same week last January.

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:07.080
A significant amount of work was done,

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:09.630
as we've discussed
previously at the board,

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.880
to ensure the NHS was in
the best possible shape

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:15.480
for the winter period this
year, following the UEC plan,

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:18.240
which was published a year
and two days ago today.

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:20.010
This included making sure capacity plans

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:21.180
to achieve our national commitments

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:23.190
for 5,000 additional core beds

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:25.620
and 10,000 new virtual
ward beds were delivered,

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:27.206
which they have been.

00:26:27.206 --> 00:26:28.950
The investment on capital schemes

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:30.600
has helped substantially this year,

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:34.260
which includes the work on
core beds and our ability

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:38.370
to deliver the 10,000 virtual
ward bed target ahead of time.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:41.940
We had 11,700 virtual ward
beds open in December,

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:43.769
which is tremendous progress.

00:26:43.769 --> 00:26:46.440
As we've mentioned,
we'll look in more detail

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:49.757
about what that means going
forward in another board item.

00:26:49.757 --> 00:26:53.700
But I think what we have is a whole system

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:56.070
that is pulled together
with brilliant engagement

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:58.080
and direction from top
to bottom of the system.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.480
And brilliant leadership from Sarah-Jane

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:03.436
and her team particularly
being able now to see

00:27:03.436 --> 00:27:06.750
and use the same metrics from end-to-end

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:09.420
of the process has made a
massive difference in our ability

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:13.050
to work together to solve
problems rapidly when they arise.

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.060
We also now know a lot about the impact

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:17.160
of the metric, of the interventions

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:19.803
that we put into the
system this time last year.

00:27:20.931 --> 00:27:23.340
I wanted to also bring up the point about,

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.650
that Amanda has mentioned,
but it deserves repeating,

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:27.240
which is the extraordinary work

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:29.250
that it took staff to respond to IA.

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:32.010
So, when we write out from NHS England

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:33.450
about preparation for strikes,

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:36.780
we ask that systems trusts spend 14,

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:38.490
at least 14 days before a strike

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:40.350
are starting their preparation work.

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:42.420
And this is, we really can't underestimate

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:44.340
how much management time
and attention it takes

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.260
to work through strikes effectively,

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:49.315
as well as the disruption post-strike

00:27:49.315 --> 00:27:51.390
and the way that staff need time

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:53.820
to recover both physical
and mental health.

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:55.260
And we're really conscious of the work

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:56.490
that that's had to go into that,

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:57.690
particularly over the last two weeks

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:00.780
after the longest ongoing
period of strike action

00:28:00.780 --> 00:28:03.580
that we've previously seen
between December and January.

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:09.060
We therefore have to think about

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:11.889
what the potential impact
of future action will be

00:28:11.889 --> 00:28:16.500
and making sure we work with
systems to prepare for that,

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:18.210
but also the impact that might have

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:19.993
on our planned performance

00:28:19.993 --> 00:28:21.870
and making sure we work with systems

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:23.910
to identify how we can best mitigate that.

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:24.960
Because what we want to do

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:26.550
is to keep on improving performance,

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:29.460
bearing in mind everything that
we've achieved collectively

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:31.710
and everything that we've
learned over the last year.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:32.543
Thank you.

00:28:34.018 --> 00:28:38.610
- I thank you very much for
that summary. Any comment?

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:41.310
Yeah, Wol, you wanted to, and Mark

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:42.990
- Yeah. Thanks, Emily.

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:45.060
And I'm taking Richard's
and Amanda's comments

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:46.770
about the ambition still to do more,

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:49.623
even though it seems to have
gone okay despite the IA.

00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:52.178
You mentioned the UEC plan,

00:28:52.178 --> 00:28:53.560
which was published a year
ago almost to the day.

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:54.630
- Yeah.
- And presuming

00:28:54.630 --> 00:28:58.170
that was quite helpful
in the performance then,

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:00.330
- Yes.
- subsequent follow through.

00:29:00.330 --> 00:29:02.267
What lessons from the period,

00:29:02.267 --> 00:29:04.410
I know we're still in
it, so it's not finished.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:05.339
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:29:05.339 --> 00:29:07.020
- Do we think we're
going to carry through?

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:09.540
So, again, we can build the base again,

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:14.540
make sure this winter is
similarly under control, in quote.

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.590
- [Emily] So, I wonder if
we want to touch on that now

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:17.423
or save it for the

00:29:17.423 --> 00:29:19.500
latest item.
- Well, I think we can.

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:22.560
So, why don't we actually,
yeah, it's a really good,

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:24.380
actually it's the fundamental
question, I think.

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:26.040
So, should we...

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:29.250
Let's hold it and we'll
hold it 'til Sarah-Jane

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:31.920
and Nick and Tracey come in, with Emily

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.077
on winter planning.
- Yeah.

00:29:33.077 --> 00:29:33.910
- But I think

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:34.825
it's the fundamental question.
- Yeah.

00:29:34.825 --> 00:29:35.700
- Mark?
- Yeah.

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:37.290
Emily, you mentioned in the report

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:39.180
the outbreaks of measles.
- Yes.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:40.013
- And I'm just wondering

00:29:40.013 --> 00:29:42.420
what the impact on paediatric beds is.

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:43.620
- Yeah.
- And also the question

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.328
about the distribution of vaccines.

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:49.320
We had very nasty pandemic
affecting older people.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.880
This is a nasty disease
that affects young people,

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:53.430
- Yeah.
- who haven't been vaccinated.

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.830
- So, now, I'll pick up
quickly the big question,

00:29:55.830 --> 00:29:59.010
but talk turn to Steve who's
leading the work on the rollout

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:00.090
of further vaccination,

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:03.720
but the occupancy of
paediatric intensive care beds

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:06.630
is as you know, always
tight during the winter.

00:30:06.630 --> 00:30:07.890
It has fluctuated up and down,

00:30:07.890 --> 00:30:09.750
but we haven't at the
moment been in a position

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:11.736
where that's been closer to the edge

00:30:11.736 --> 00:30:13.709
than we would've been anyway.

00:30:13.709 --> 00:30:16.350
But obviously, the critical
thing is to keep children

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:17.700
from getting measles in the first place

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:19.350
and from becoming that seriously ill,

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:21.742
which is the work that Steve
and his team have been doing.

00:30:21.742 --> 00:30:23.070
- Thanks, Emily.

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:24.870
So, just, Mark, to just add

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:27.270
to the response point
that you raised there.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:30.360
So, starting next week,

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:32.580
and then running over
about a three-week period,

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:37.293
we'll be inviting all
children, through their parents

00:30:37.293 --> 00:30:39.600
who are 6 to 11 years old

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:43.795
to come forward for a
vaccine, measles, MMR vaccine.

00:30:43.795 --> 00:30:47.400
And in three areas in England

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:49.940
where there are more
concentration of cases,

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:54.940
so London, the Midlands, and Manchester.

00:30:55.176 --> 00:30:57.150
That will be extended up to 25 year olds.

00:30:57.150 --> 00:31:00.030
So, it's about 4 million people

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.650
who will be invited over
that period of time.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:04.560
And to your question on supply,

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.110
we're aware that there's sufficient supply

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:09.603
to manage that scale of vaccination.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:13.500
- Mary?
- I just wanted to pick up

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:17.370
the issue that you
illustrated diagnostically

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:20.640
with people concerned
about malignant melanoma.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:24.300
Presumably, the fast track
diagnosis also gives reassurance

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:25.740
to a high proportion,

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:28.440
and therefore they come off
the waiting list quite quickly.

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:30.450
- Yeah. Yes, that's exactly right.

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:33.660
And so, the additional capacity
has been helpful at that,

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:35.580
but with the increased demand,

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:37.230
we really need to mesh
the two do together.

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:38.063
What we've seen is,

00:31:38.063 --> 00:31:40.849
as we've provided substantially
more access to diagnostics

00:31:40.849 --> 00:31:43.320
in the emergency room,

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:46.680
clinicians are now using
more diagnostic work there

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:48.930
rather than admitting people
or putting them on a separate

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:50.280
outpatient diagnostic pathway.

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:52.440
So, we've moved capacity

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:54.419
between different parts of the system.

00:31:54.419 --> 00:31:56.310
But yes, you're right,
our goal is to make sure

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:59.020
people get that reassurance
sooner rather than later.

00:31:59.020 --> 00:32:00.123
- I think the,

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:06.900
we do risk confetting,
lots of stats around,

00:32:06.900 --> 00:32:08.613
but I like stats, actually.

00:32:10.474 --> 00:32:12.690
And when you look at this and
when you look at the deck,

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:15.360
- Yeah.
- I do think we sometimes,

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.300
the challenges are huge.
- Mm.

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:20.836
- The workforce is engaged
with the challenges

00:32:20.836 --> 00:32:24.690
as is really impressive and remarkable.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:27.690
But actually what comes to
the papers is this steady,

00:32:27.690 --> 00:32:29.310
more than steady increase

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:33.000
in the demand that's coming at the NHS.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:36.390
So, I was just looking at,
there's a very interesting chart,

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:38.640
difficult for people
watching in the world.

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:42.090
We look at the mental
health presentations,

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:44.670
so number of nought to 17
year olds, so young children,

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:47.010
young people receiving a first contact

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:48.060
with mental health services.

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:49.740
You look at the rate of growth

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:53.775
and it's up virtually 50% in four years

00:32:53.775 --> 00:32:57.990
and it's over 750,000 young people now

00:32:57.990 --> 00:32:59.970
receiving that first intervention.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:03.510
There is a touch over
500,000 four years ago.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:05.875
So, that rate of demand
that's coming at us,

00:33:05.875 --> 00:33:07.980
I think in how we have to deal with it.

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:12.980
And I think we deal with it
and clearly we do deal with it.

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:16.759
And then, you look at
is it numbers of people.

00:33:16.759 --> 00:33:20.250
So, I was struck again, look
at the number of patients

00:33:20.250 --> 00:33:23.485
starting treatment in
November of last year.

00:33:23.485 --> 00:33:27.180
It was up almost 5% on
October of last year.

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:28.260
And actually, if you look at the number

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:29.730
of people starting treatment in a year,

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:31.830
it's over 1.6 million people.

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:34.230
Population of England's 57 million.

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:37.680
So, 1.6 million a starting treatment

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:40.093
in November of last year.

00:33:40.093 --> 00:33:44.040
And that's more than 10% greater
than it was pre-pandemic.

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:46.260
So, there's more and more
interventions of treatment

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.270
being provided as we meet the demand

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:49.680
coming from the population.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:51.690
And I often think that scale of provision

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:54.472
and the growth in demand
is sometimes missed

00:33:54.472 --> 00:33:56.820
in the narrative and
the external narrative,

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:00.090
which isn't to say ever that we can

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:01.415
and we can't be complacent.

00:34:01.415 --> 00:34:04.167
There's still much, much, much more to do

00:34:04.167 --> 00:34:05.909
and demand continues to rise,

00:34:05.909 --> 00:34:10.909
but the challenges are
noteworthy really, I think.

00:34:11.492 --> 00:34:13.440
Any other comments

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:14.273
before we bring in...

00:34:14.273 --> 00:34:17.490
Sorry, Andrew, before we
bring in Julian on finance.

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:18.323
- [Andrew] Thanks, Richard.

00:34:18.323 --> 00:34:19.860
Can I just bring up a point on cancer?

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:21.510
- Yes.
- So, the referrals

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:26.100
are up by a significant number, 126%,

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:26.933
and we've still got

00:34:26.933 --> 00:34:28.920
this backlog of patients
- Yeah.

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:29.978
- that are waiting

00:34:29.978 --> 00:34:31.257
more than 62 days.
- Yeah.

00:34:31.257 --> 00:34:34.920
- So, just looking forward as
some of that additional work

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:37.380
that we've seen starts to come through,

00:34:37.380 --> 00:34:38.657
it's going to put further pressure

00:34:38.657 --> 00:34:39.717
- It is.
- on us.

00:34:39.717 --> 00:34:43.480
And I'm just thinking what can we do to

00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:47.700
get that back under control,

00:34:47.700 --> 00:34:51.120
because all the clinical
evidence is that your outcome

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:56.120
improves if you're seen
within the 62-day pathway.

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:57.660
So, that's an issue

00:34:57.660 --> 00:35:00.298
for us going forward, I think,
that we need to address.

00:35:00.298 --> 00:35:01.393
- Yeah.

00:35:01.393 --> 00:35:05.340
So, we are very focused on
our public targets on cancer,

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:08.937
which we think start to
tackle that challenge.

00:35:08.937 --> 00:35:10.200
Obviously there's a
longer term capacity point

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:12.660
to your point, Andrew,
and the opportunities

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:15.630
with new technologies to
improve treatment as well.

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:18.480
But our, at the moment, the 62-day backlog

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:21.310
and the faster diagnostic
standard are our focus

00:35:21.310 --> 00:35:22.320
- Yeah.
- to make sure

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:23.639
we are doing everything we can

00:35:23.639 --> 00:35:27.570
to get those waiting down
to give early reassurance

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:29.130
to people who are waiting
without an answer.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.290
But their answer will be
that they don't have cancer

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:33.300
and that we can then focus the treatment

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:34.920
on the patients who need it.

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:38.100
But as you say, as with everything else,

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:40.860
we have a growth in demand that we need

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:43.140
to keep on innovating and
keep on improving pathways

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:45.060
and improving capacity
in order to deal with it.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:47.433
- And we're searching and
screening much more, aren't we?

00:35:47.433 --> 00:35:49.757
I think we take lung trucks,
a really good initiative

00:35:49.757 --> 00:35:51.750
and actually we've also
dropped the age range,

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:54.300
lowered the age range at
which bowel screening,

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:56.420
I think is now done.
- Yes.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:58.500
- So, I think we're continuously looking

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:00.290
to increase our screening capability.

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:01.500
- To increase screening capability

00:36:01.500 --> 00:36:04.350
and also where the lung tracks
is the brilliant example,

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:07.080
make sure we improve
the equality of access.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.780
So, on lung cancer, it is now more likely

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:12.494
to be diagnosed with lung cancer early

00:36:12.494 --> 00:36:16.020
if you are in a lower
decile of deprivation

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.180
than if you are at the privileged end,

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:20.220
because of where we've put the lung trunks

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:21.690
and who they've managed to reach out to.

00:36:21.690 --> 00:36:24.330
- Very good. Thank you.

