through November of

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:45.060
having put funding in to cover the cost

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:48.600
of industrial action to agree

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:51.360
with system by system
provider by by provider

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:55.350
where we would outturn
for the end of the year.

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:59.174
Clearly, the background remains
that inflation this year

00:36:59.174 --> 00:37:02.400
much higher than the forecast at the time

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:04.620
we agreed the funding with government

00:37:04.620 --> 00:37:06.693
at autumn statement '22.

00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:12.780
Roughly, it is on a GDP deflator basis,

00:37:12.780 --> 00:37:14.940
almost 4% higher in terms of the way

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:16.410
we actually experience it in health.

00:37:16.410 --> 00:37:21.038
It's probably more like 5 or 6% higher

00:37:21.038 --> 00:37:22.650
as I've discussed previously.

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:25.290
We see that in particular in, for example,

00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:28.830
the cost of continuing
healthcare, the cost of medicines,

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:30.030
all of which are up on average

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:33.600
around 8 to 9% over the
course of this year.

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:35.220
And you will see that also

00:37:35.220 --> 00:37:37.220
as people have renewed energy contracts,

00:37:38.504 --> 00:37:42.663
the vast array of services
that people are having to buy.

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:45.370
The net result of all of that

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:48.236
is that we are forecasting

00:37:48.236 --> 00:37:50.850
with systems and providers

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:54.870
that we will end up about a billion pounds

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:57.663
off plan by the end of the year.

00:37:58.571 --> 00:38:03.571
But in aggregate, we will
live within the NHS mandate

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:08.610
as provided to us by government.

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:10.380
Clearly, there are still
ongoing discussions

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:12.390
with the government

00:38:12.390 --> 00:38:14.760
and with the department
about total final funding

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:19.230
for the Department of Health,
as will then be reflected

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:21.540
through the supplementary
estimate process.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:22.380
But we will live

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:26.670
within the NHS England
mandate as given to us.

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:29.961
And conversations remain
ongoing about '24, '25.

00:38:29.961 --> 00:38:34.045
And with the funding
that has been provided,

00:38:34.045 --> 00:38:35.790
you know what the expectations are

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:37.383
for what we will deliver.

00:38:38.550 --> 00:38:43.500
I wrote out to the NHS with
some guidelines therefore,

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:46.050
for how they should
continue to plan for '25,

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:48.900
for '24, '25, I wrote out in December,

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.400
which was very much a sort
of a steady-as-she-goes

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:57.660
proposition, including in
particular, the need for people

00:38:57.660 --> 00:39:01.530
to maintain and keep open the
extra capacity we have put in

00:39:01.530 --> 00:39:03.604
over the course of '24, '25,

00:39:03.604 --> 00:39:08.310
both in our acute hospitals,
ambulance services,

00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:10.680
and indeed maintain the
extra capacity investment

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:15.302
we have put into mental health
GPs and community services.

00:39:15.302 --> 00:39:18.720
But we will continue those
discussions with government,

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:21.390
hope to conclude them
soon, and therefore be able

00:39:21.390 --> 00:39:25.953
to give clear direction
to the NHS for next year.

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:30.930
- Can I ask a quick question?

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:32.976
When we were looking at earlier data,

00:39:32.976 --> 00:39:37.424
there is a clear variance in performance

00:39:37.424 --> 00:39:40.460
across the 42 systems,

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:42.270
42 ICBs.

00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:44.737
Any reflections on that?

00:39:44.737 --> 00:39:45.570
Clearly, they all start

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:46.920
in different spaces and different places.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:50.370
There's lots of reason why
they would have variance.

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:51.813
But any reflections?

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:54.353
- As you say,

00:39:55.700 --> 00:39:57.270
there is variation.

00:39:57.270 --> 00:39:59.670
Some of that is due to,

00:39:59.670 --> 00:40:02.280
actually even the position we saw,

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:06.750
even as we entered the
pandemic back in 2019, '20.

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:08.580
And as we have recovered,

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:12.092
you see some of the
historic problems emerge.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:15.133
We have seen very different patterns,

00:40:15.133 --> 00:40:18.330
as to how the pressures emerge

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:20.005
and things like continuing healthcare,

00:40:20.005 --> 00:40:22.650
which partly reflects differences

00:40:22.650 --> 00:40:26.577
in practise even on sort of
local authority, social care,

00:40:26.577 --> 00:40:27.912
and how some of the changes

00:40:27.912 --> 00:40:29.280
in fair cost of care

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:32.580
have kind of worked their
way through social care,

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:35.523
and then into the NHS
over a period of time.

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:38.940
Clearly, there have also been differences

00:40:38.940 --> 00:40:41.257
in the way people have
responded with their workforce.

00:40:41.257 --> 00:40:44.200
As we went through the pandemic

00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:49.890
and differences in the pace at which some,

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:54.240
in particular, providers
have responded to,

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:57.313
as we have come out of the pandemic.

00:40:57.313 --> 00:41:01.406
In terms of really making sure

00:41:01.406 --> 00:41:03.690
we have appropriate management

00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:08.670
of total workforce, agency
spending, bank spending.

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:10.500
And I will flag

00:41:10.500 --> 00:41:14.340
that this year, we are
seeing cash reductions

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:19.340
across the NHS in spending
on temporary staffing.

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:22.260
Year on year for the last quarter,

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:25.470
we're about 12% down in real
terms in terms of spending.

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:28.140
That's an actual cash reduction

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:32.930
and reflects really how
people are making sure

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:35.760
that as we've come through the pandemic,

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:39.090
we get back some sort of
operational management

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:41.170
and a real focus on productivity

00:41:42.162 --> 00:41:45.690
off the back of this sort of
increase in permanent staffing

00:41:45.690 --> 00:41:48.990
that Amanda was talking
about at the start.

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:53.280
So, I think it does reflect
some variable practise,

00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:57.178
as well as genuinely some
differences in the circumstances

00:41:57.178 --> 00:42:02.130
that different systems and
different providers face.

00:42:02.130 --> 00:42:03.780
But look, we are clearly working

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:07.770
with some of our systems and providers,

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:10.127
particularly those who we have put

00:42:10.127 --> 00:42:15.120
into enhanced supervisory arrangements

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:16.260
- Yeah.
- to make sure

00:42:16.260 --> 00:42:19.110
that appropriate recovery
plans are in place

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:21.609
and appropriate action is being taken

00:42:21.609 --> 00:42:23.550
to make sure we are managing money,

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:26.790
performance, and quality as a whole.

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:31.790
- And can you remind me the
benefit in cashflow terms

00:42:34.166 --> 00:42:39.166
of actually, this very
effectively managed merger

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:43.260
of NHS England with Digital, with HE,

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.540
with improvement, with NHSX,

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:49.080
and actually then the headcount reduction

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:50.190
and the cost savings.

00:42:50.190 --> 00:42:52.440
It was done not for cost savings,

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:54.180
but there were cost savings that arose.

00:42:54.180 --> 00:42:55.530
It was done for different reasons

00:42:55.530 --> 00:43:00.530
of reducing head office burden
upon systems appropriately.

00:43:00.703 --> 00:43:03.300
But can you remind me of the,

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.790
there is a significant cash savings

00:43:05.790 --> 00:43:08.940
from that exercise which
has moved to frontline.

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:09.773
Can you remind me

00:43:09.773 --> 00:43:13.920
of the order of magnitude?
- So, this year, '23, '24,

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:15.840
I do not have the absolutely
- Yeah.

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:17.760
- precise number by the end,
- Yeah.

00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:19.650
- which we will firm up.

00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:21.666
But this year in '23, '24,

00:43:21.666 --> 00:43:25.350
we will have saved on a recurrent basis

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:27.843
around 300 million pounds
by the end of the year.

00:43:30.300 --> 00:43:33.660
We are targeting that by the
time we work our way through

00:43:33.660 --> 00:43:37.020
to a whole full year effect
in the end of next year,

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:39.780
I think the number will be
over 600 million pounds,

00:43:39.780 --> 00:43:41.190
all of which is going

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:45.210
basically into supporting
frontline services,

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:48.000
both the maintenance of the capacity

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:49.740
that I've just described,
as well as our ability

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:52.892
to keep investing in mental health, cancer

00:43:52.892 --> 00:43:56.700
and various other programmes
where maternity is,

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:57.963
Ruth is reminding me,

00:43:59.439 --> 00:44:02.760
just to make sure we
can continue to invest.

00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:07.380
And I'd flag that integrated
care boards are also well on,

00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:09.390
not just with the planning,
but in many cases,

00:44:09.390 --> 00:44:14.390
the execution of the
consolidation of resources also,

00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:18.506
which is basically freeing up funding

00:44:18.506 --> 00:44:21.150
to go into frontline services,

00:44:21.150 --> 00:44:22.980
which I think will add
about another 200 million

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:25.110
to the number that I just described.

00:44:25.110 --> 00:44:27.900
- So, over that period,
that's of 800 million or so.

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:29.820
It's an order of magnitude,

00:44:29.820 --> 00:44:32.190
about 800 million moved out of layers

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:33.180
into the front line.
- On the current basis

00:44:33.180 --> 00:44:35.310
as we finish '24, '25.

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:36.270
- That's very good. Thank you.

00:44:36.270 --> 00:44:38.110
Wol?
- Thanks, Richard.

00:44:39.249 --> 00:44:42.060
So, Julian, one of the things
you very quickly mentioned

00:44:42.060 --> 00:44:44.410
is productivity and how
key that's going to be.

00:44:45.990 --> 00:44:48.540
Is it worth just expanding
on how you are seeing that?

00:44:48.540 --> 00:44:51.137
It's a very tricky, difficult subject,

00:44:51.137 --> 00:44:54.180
and certainly in anecdotal conversations

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:57.060
with people outside, I
don't think they appreciate

00:44:57.060 --> 00:44:59.370
looking back as we look at the numbers,

00:44:59.370 --> 00:45:02.043
how productive the NHS has been,

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:05.700
and then looking forward
how you are thinking

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:08.580
about that in terms of
what we have to achieve.

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:10.530
And we haven't got the numbers
for next year, don't worry.

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:11.613
But yeah-
- Sure.

00:45:12.780 --> 00:45:15.130
Historically, the NHS

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:19.290
has achieved high levels of productivity

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:21.746
in the 20 years up to the pandemic.

00:45:21.746 --> 00:45:25.440
I think it was York
University did some analysis

00:45:25.440 --> 00:45:29.280
which suggested we outstripped
total economy productivity.

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:33.000
Clearly, the pandemic was a
massive shock to the system

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:38.000
and you can look at similar
shocks to other services

00:45:38.580 --> 00:45:39.413
in the economy

00:45:39.413 --> 00:45:44.413
that it does actually
take some time to recover.

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:48.570
And actually, you recover
faster where the infrastructure

00:45:48.570 --> 00:45:51.003
and technology's actually in a good state.

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:53.850
And clearly, it's highly variable

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:56.190
and we know across the NHS,

00:45:56.190 --> 00:46:00.280
nonetheless, we have seen a hit to output.

00:46:00.280 --> 00:46:05.280
Some of the numbers, I think,
underestimate the impact

00:46:05.700 --> 00:46:09.630
of increased complexity of cases, frailty,

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:14.250
the impact of COVID
actually both on patients,

00:46:14.250 --> 00:46:15.960
and clearly, you have a hit to staffing,

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:20.960
because we have seen higher
structural sickness, absence.

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:24.073
And the one thing we
really struggle to capture

00:46:24.073 --> 00:46:27.599
in a measurable way is the
impact on discretionary effort.

00:46:27.599 --> 00:46:30.510
It's always often said that the NHS

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:32.163
relies on discretionary effort,

00:46:34.950 --> 00:46:37.050
but if your staff have been striking

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:40.890
for the best part of well
over a year, you are clearly

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:44.793
going to see less discretionary
effort in the round.

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:48.660
We also need to get better at counting

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:49.560
some of the things we do

00:46:49.560 --> 00:46:50.880
which are not currently captured

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:52.230
in some of the official statistics,

00:46:52.230 --> 00:46:53.280
because we have changed the way

00:46:53.280 --> 00:46:56.990
that we work in particular,
in emergency care,

00:46:56.990 --> 00:47:00.270
where we are using same day emergency care

00:47:00.270 --> 00:47:01.200
much more than we ever were

00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:02.760
and we don't really yet

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:06.483
capture that well in
some of our statistics.

00:47:08.430 --> 00:47:12.240
And then, finally, we
also don't really capture,

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:15.870
which is not an ideal effect

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:17.700
that effectively rather
than admitting people,

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:20.550
you're holding people in
the emergency department.

00:47:20.550 --> 00:47:22.020
Now you are actually caring for them,

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:23.523
we'd be better admitting them,

00:47:24.600 --> 00:47:26.520
but you don't weight that the same way.

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:27.930
So, some of the headline stats, I think,

00:47:27.930 --> 00:47:30.810
don't capture everything
that's actually gone on.

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:34.150
Nonetheless, there has
been a productivity hit

00:47:35.310 --> 00:47:38.103
and clearly,

00:47:39.780 --> 00:47:41.220
we are trying to make sure

00:47:41.220 --> 00:47:43.200
we are improving some
of the benchmarking data

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:45.500
that is available so
that in fact, Nick's here

00:47:47.310 --> 00:47:49.890
from Norwich and has worked elsewhere.

00:47:49.890 --> 00:47:52.083
So, people can just keep working on,

00:47:53.400 --> 00:47:56.880
how do we keep a focus on recovery.

00:47:56.880 --> 00:47:58.400
And that does take some time.

00:47:58.400 --> 00:47:59.853
In the long run,

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:05.580
the way we will sustainably
improve productivity

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:07.230
will come from

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:10.578
and will only really come
from serious investment,

00:48:10.578 --> 00:48:13.050
a sustained investment in technology.

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:16.680
And we will have to address the problems

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:19.170
with our physical infrastructure

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:21.931
in particular the critical
backlog maintenance,

00:48:21.931 --> 00:48:25.028
because we can see over the last 10 years

00:48:25.028 --> 00:48:27.730
a substantial rise in

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:32.700
incidents in the estate,
which mean you lose-

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:34.800
- Capability. Yeah.
- You lose capacity.

00:48:35.649 --> 00:48:36.750
And we can see that increasing.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.427
We have done some headline estimates,

00:48:39.427 --> 00:48:41.520
which I don't think
are incredibly precise,

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:45.433
but suggest we could be losing 0.1,

00:48:45.433 --> 00:48:50.433
0.2% a year at the moment
of productivity simply,

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:55.110
NHS by productivity simply because we lose

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:57.000
an increasing amount of estate each year.

00:48:57.000 --> 00:49:02.000
So, that's alongside the
sort of management focus,

00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:07.500
the recovery of really good practise,

00:49:07.500 --> 00:49:08.790
which has been really hit as we just

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:12.003
had to move everything
around within our services,

00:49:15.324 --> 00:49:18.360
is going to require sustained
investment in those areas

00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:21.090
if we're going to see
a substantial change.

00:49:21.090 --> 00:49:21.923
- Thank you.

00:49:21.923 --> 00:49:22.756
Ruth, you wanted to

00:49:22.756 --> 00:49:24.750
- Yes.
- ask a question?

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:27.450
- It's more of a comment
really in that Amanda

00:49:27.450 --> 00:49:30.570
talked earlier about 20,000
more registered nurses

00:49:30.570 --> 00:49:33.360
going into this winter than last year,

00:49:33.360 --> 00:49:36.750
over a thousand more midwives
than this time last year,

00:49:36.750 --> 00:49:39.210
over 8,000 more healthcare support workers

00:49:39.210 --> 00:49:40.620
this time last year.

00:49:40.620 --> 00:49:42.854
All of those going into
substantive positions

00:49:42.854 --> 00:49:46.143
will no doubt I'd expect
agency to come down.

00:49:47.130 --> 00:49:48.248
Absolutely right,

00:49:48.248 --> 00:49:50.370
'cause that's the right
thing for patients,

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:52.590
right things for quality safety.

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:55.590
I think now, we'll really
need to work on getting people

00:49:55.590 --> 00:49:59.163
that are on the bank into
substantive positions,

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.007
offering them flexible approaches.

00:50:02.007 --> 00:50:04.470
And then, of course our retention work

00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:07.410
that we are all doing is
going to be really important,

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:09.699
because the best quality of care

00:50:09.699 --> 00:50:12.030
will only be provided, I'm sure,

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:14.277
by the permanent substantive members

00:50:14.277 --> 00:50:15.750
of staff all of the time.

00:50:15.750 --> 00:50:16.980
So, let's not waste the work

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:18.730
that we've done to recruit these people.

00:50:18.730 --> 00:50:20.910
We've really now got to go further

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:23.880
and get them from bank
onto substantive contracts.

00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:25.890
- Thank you. Let's move on.

00:50:25.890 --> 00:50:26.723
Julian, thank you.

00:50:26.723 --> 00:50:30.633
As always, really clear
and very, very helpful.

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:32.790
So, Sarah-Jane,

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:35.220
I think you're going to
lead on the next item

00:50:35.220 --> 00:50:38.880
and you've got Nick Hulme
and Tracey Bleakley.

00:50:38.880 --> 00:50:40.110
We've got you with us,

00:50:40.110 --> 00:50:41.127
- which is brilliant.

00:50:41.127 --> 00:50:42.913
- Tracey and Nick are
the stars of this show.

00:50:42.913 --> 00:50:43.770
So, I'm just going to say
- There you go.

00:50:43.770 --> 00:50:44.970
- a few words of introduction,

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:47.130
I'm going to hand over to my colleagues

00:50:47.130 --> 00:50:49.080
who are going to bring it
to life, what it's meant

00:50:49.080 --> 00:50:50.993
at system and trust-
- And can you pick up

00:50:50.993 --> 00:50:53.370
Wol's fundamental question?
- I will, of course.

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:54.780
Of course, chair. Thank you.

00:50:54.780 --> 00:50:57.540
So, just, this has come
up a few times already,

00:50:57.540 --> 00:50:59.160
but just to remind people,

00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:02.430
we have been preparing for
this winter for 12 months.

00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:04.480
The UEC recovery plan
- Yeah.

00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:05.670
- was published in January.

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:07.950
It was branded NHS England in DHSC,

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:10.515
but it was the work of
everybody across the NHS.

00:51:10.515 --> 00:51:13.140
It was based on what
everybody in our trust,

00:51:13.140 --> 00:51:15.210
in our systems were
telling us were the things

00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:18.870
that would make a biggest
impact going into this winter.

00:51:18.870 --> 00:51:19.730
It was end-to-end,

00:51:19.730 --> 00:51:23.460
so it was the first time
that a UEC focus plan

00:51:23.460 --> 00:51:25.208
hadn't just looked at ambulances,

00:51:25.208 --> 00:51:28.140
A&amp;E departments, or what
was happening in hospitals.

00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:29.970
It had looked end-to-end

00:51:29.970 --> 00:51:32.430
at all the things that we
can do in the community.

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:36.300
And it had a really big
focus as well on discharge,

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:38.700
working with our
colleagues in social care.

00:51:38.700 --> 00:51:41.280
It was all based around system planning,

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:43.320
not just doing things in isolation

00:51:43.320 --> 00:51:45.870
in individual organisations.

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:48.480
And the money for the delivery of it

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:51.180
was released at the beginning of the year,

00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:54.960
rather than coming out in
a sort of ad hoc funding,

00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:56.550
as we went into the winter period,

00:51:56.550 --> 00:51:58.290
which had characterised previous years

00:51:58.290 --> 00:51:59.220
and was something that people

00:51:59.220 --> 00:52:01.290
told us they didn't want to see.

00:52:01.290 --> 00:52:04.590
And that was true for both
the NHS part of the funding

00:52:04.590 --> 00:52:06.600
and also the social care
part of the funding.

00:52:06.600 --> 00:52:10.080
So, we were able to spend
more time preparing.

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:11.880
And then, just the other feature of it

00:52:11.880 --> 00:52:15.750
was that we went into
it absolutely determined

00:52:15.750 --> 00:52:18.120
to be more data-driven in our responses,

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:19.620
so that we were really clear

00:52:19.620 --> 00:52:23.340
at each part of the
system where people were.

00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:24.960
And our new Opal framework

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:27.514
actually allows us to
see on a 15-minute basis

00:52:27.514 --> 00:52:30.360
how pressure is building in our systems

00:52:30.360 --> 00:52:32.400
and allowing people to be able to respond

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:34.710
in the most appropriate way.

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:38.379
So, we spent a lot of
time preparing for this,

00:52:38.379 --> 00:52:40.470
a 12-month run in.

00:52:40.470 --> 00:52:41.790
A lot of the resilience

00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:44.610
that we built has been related
to the additional capacity.

00:52:44.610 --> 00:52:45.870
Other colleagues have mentioned that,

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:48.270
so I won't list it out again.

00:52:48.270 --> 00:52:50.280
But we have had those additional beds,

00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:52.410
ambulances, virtual wards, and so on.

00:52:52.410 --> 00:52:54.420
Probably the area that hasn't come up,

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:57.330
I just want to reinforce,
is all the excellent work

00:52:57.330 --> 00:52:59.490
that has been done around discharge.

00:52:59.490 --> 00:53:00.810
We often talk about

00:53:00.810 --> 00:53:04.339
discharge being one of
our real constraints

00:53:04.339 --> 00:53:06.333
and it continues to be,

00:53:06.333 --> 00:53:08.850
but it has held up really well

00:53:08.850 --> 00:53:11.340
in the face of the demands this year.

00:53:11.340 --> 00:53:12.600
So, we have been able

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:15.600
to maintain a pretty
consistent discharge profile

00:53:15.600 --> 00:53:18.780
across all of our pathways
throughout the year.

00:53:18.780 --> 00:53:21.090
Sometimes, we need to flex
and go a little higher

00:53:21.090 --> 00:53:22.800
and that can be problematic,

00:53:22.800 --> 00:53:25.140
but we have managed to
hold onto that steady flow

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:26.490
of people going out of hospital

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:28.890
and that has no doubt helped us

00:53:28.890 --> 00:53:30.870
as we've gone into the period.

00:53:30.870 --> 00:53:34.263
Other colleagues have shared statistics.

00:53:34.263 --> 00:53:37.650
We obviously focus on the
ambulance response times.

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:39.630
We know why it's really
important to the public

00:53:39.630 --> 00:53:43.200
and the A&amp;E four hours as well.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:44.610
I just want to share one of my own,

00:53:44.610 --> 00:53:46.920
which I only read about last week

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:49.350
and just was so struck by
the significance of this.

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:51.870
And this is just actually
about our 111 services,

00:53:51.870 --> 00:53:54.180
which is the front door often to patients

00:53:54.180 --> 00:53:57.720
trying to access urgent care out of hours.

00:53:57.720 --> 00:53:59.610
We answered in the Christmas week,

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:01.710
which is our busiest week ever for 111.

00:54:01.710 --> 00:54:03.570
We answered the phone 16 minutes quicker

00:54:03.570 --> 00:54:08.460
than we had in the week, for
the Christmas period before.

00:54:08.460 --> 00:54:10.770
And we were able to
provide clinical advice

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:12.950
to 13,000 more patients

00:54:12.950 --> 00:54:15.210
than we had done in the previous year.

00:54:15.210 --> 00:54:16.380
So, all the work that we did

00:54:16.380 --> 00:54:18.150
to get clinicians into call centres,

00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:19.808
to get our patients phone back,

00:54:19.808 --> 00:54:22.694
all of those things had a huge impact.

00:54:22.694 --> 00:54:26.250
And it's those, I think,
I point is everything

00:54:26.250 --> 00:54:27.480
contributes to what we see

00:54:27.480 --> 00:54:28.980
in some of those headline statistics.

00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:30.990
So, whilst it's important
that we look at those,

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:34.066
it's important that we look at
the drivers of them as well.

00:54:34.066 --> 00:54:36.870
Nevertheless, it's come at a huge cost.

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:39.900
This has some of the
things that we've achieved

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:42.540
are because we have moved
pressures around in the system,

00:54:42.540 --> 00:54:43.917
we've talked a lot about risk management

00:54:43.917 --> 00:54:47.315
and where we put risk at
different parts of the pathway.

00:54:47.315 --> 00:54:48.720
And I'm really conscious,

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:50.903
and we're going to hear this
from colleagues in a moment,

00:54:50.903 --> 00:54:54.000
that we have moved risk
out of people's houses

00:54:54.000 --> 00:54:55.470
and from the back of ambulances.

00:54:55.470 --> 00:54:56.460
And in some cases,

00:54:56.460 --> 00:54:58.407
we've moved that into
emergency departments,

00:54:58.407 --> 00:55:01.200
we've moved that into wards

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:03.240
that have had to take the pressure

00:55:03.240 --> 00:55:06.303
of taking additional patients on there.

00:55:06.303 --> 00:55:08.130
And I think that's one of the areas

00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:11.610
as we move into next year
is one of our learnings here

00:55:11.610 --> 00:55:13.470
that we need to have a really big focus

00:55:13.470 --> 00:55:16.650
on what is happening inside our hospitals,

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:18.720
so that we continue with the performance,

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:20.428
that levels that we're seeing.

00:55:20.428 --> 00:55:23.880
But we decongest some
very, very crowded areas.

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:27.060
So, just in terms of our
learning for taking forwards,

00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:29.010
we've got four big areas of work.

00:55:29.010 --> 00:55:31.680
The first thing is we need
to maintain the capacity.

00:55:31.680 --> 00:55:33.260
That's easy to say and challenging to do

00:55:33.260 --> 00:55:35.010
in the face of what Julian talked about,

00:55:35.010 --> 00:55:36.660
the financial position.

00:55:36.660 --> 00:55:38.880
So, we know it's going to be
challenging for organisations,

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:40.980
but particularly the core bed base,

00:55:40.980 --> 00:55:43.590
we have to sustain through
the course of the year.

00:55:43.590 --> 00:55:46.740
We then do need to genuinely
improve productivity

00:55:46.740 --> 00:55:48.472
and we know how to do this.

00:55:48.472 --> 00:55:51.210
So, we know the services that have got

00:55:51.210 --> 00:55:53.670
the same-day emergency
care working effectively.

00:55:53.670 --> 00:55:56.610
They've got their medical
admission, 72-hour pathways,

00:55:56.610 --> 00:55:58.200
they're looking to
reduce the length of stay

00:55:58.200 --> 00:56:01.380
of every patient, they
discharge before lunchtime.

00:56:01.380 --> 00:56:04.950
These are the organisations
that manage flow better,

00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:06.630
are able to offload ambulances

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.400
that are in a better financial position

00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:09.990
that get better quality and outcomes.

00:56:09.990 --> 00:56:11.950
So, it's all the same work

00:56:12.900 --> 00:56:15.540
and it will be the second area of focus.

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:17.700
And then, the third is the coordination.

00:56:17.700 --> 00:56:19.890
And you'll hear this
again from colleagues.

00:56:19.890 --> 00:56:22.350
It's the coordination of some
of the community services.

00:56:22.350 --> 00:56:24.540
We've got some brilliant work happening,

00:56:24.540 --> 00:56:26.880
but we need the same
rigour in our approach

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:28.920
and coordination in
our community services.

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:30.840
And the same challenges to ourselves

00:56:30.840 --> 00:56:34.320
around productivity as we
have in the acute sector.

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:36.210
And then, finally, it's
looking more broadly

00:56:36.210 --> 00:56:37.860
at some of the data I talked about before.

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:40.080
So, primary care, mental health,

00:56:40.080 --> 00:56:42.480
and the broader community services

00:56:42.480 --> 00:56:44.400
is all part of our escalation data.

00:56:44.400 --> 00:56:46.050
At the moment, it is too focused

00:56:46.050 --> 00:56:47.610
on what is happening in hospitals,

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:48.840
which is still a massive advance

00:56:48.840 --> 00:56:50.100
from what we had previously,

00:56:50.100 --> 00:56:53.190
but we need to be looking at
the whole healthcare system.

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:55.320
So, I think we could characterise this

00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:57.960
as we've done an awful lot,

00:56:57.960 --> 00:57:00.480
but we've still got an
awful lot more to do

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:04.350
and it can be true that
we have prepared better,

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:06.510
that we've delivered better.

00:57:06.510 --> 00:57:10.033
And that by and large
it's, although challenging,

00:57:10.033 --> 00:57:11.716
we've got through it together,

00:57:11.716 --> 00:57:16.290
and there is still a lot more
that we could and need to do,

00:57:16.290 --> 00:57:18.390
and that for many
patients their experience

00:57:18.390 --> 00:57:19.823
hasn't been what we would want it to be.

00:57:19.823 --> 00:57:22.347
And that has had a huge
impact on our staff.

00:57:22.347 --> 00:57:24.210
And both of those things are true

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:25.890
and I think that makes us all determined

00:57:25.890 --> 00:57:28.050
to take the lessons, take the learning,

00:57:28.050 --> 00:57:30.180
and really make sure it's time

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:32.163
to start planning again, isn't it?

00:57:32.163 --> 00:57:33.510
It's absolutely.

00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:35.190
But now my colleagues, Tracey and Nick,

00:57:35.190 --> 00:57:36.150
will bring this to life

00:57:36.150 --> 00:57:37.860
from the Norfolk and Waveney system.

00:57:37.860 --> 00:57:39.300
And specific-
- So, I dunno

00:57:39.300 --> 00:57:40.200
who's going to go first,

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:41.100
but, Tracey, welcome,

00:57:41.100 --> 00:57:42.869
is Chief Executive of-
- Always Tracey, Tracey

00:57:42.869 --> 00:57:43.857
goes first, yeah.

00:57:43.857 --> 00:57:44.690
- Yeah, Chief Executive

00:57:44.690 --> 00:57:46.680
of Norfolk and Waveney ICB,

00:57:46.680 --> 00:57:49.110
and Nuck Hulme, Chief Executive

00:57:49.110 --> 00:57:51.360
of Norfolk and Norwich Hospital.

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:53.250
So, Tracey.

00:57:53.250 --> 00:57:54.690
- Thank you, chair,
and welcome, everybody,

00:57:54.690 --> 00:57:56.670
to Norwich and Norfolk and Waveney.

00:57:56.670 --> 00:58:00.750
Our first year as an ICB, '22, '23,

00:58:00.750 --> 00:58:04.470
was awful from a UEC
perspective, quite frankly.

00:58:04.470 --> 00:58:05.790
All of the underlying issues

00:58:05.790 --> 00:58:08.520
that Julian alluded to

00:58:08.520 --> 00:58:09.450
factored into this.

00:58:09.450 --> 00:58:12.240
And we'd had many years
of poor performance.

00:58:12.240 --> 00:58:14.880
So, we've got two rack hospitals,

00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:19.440
massive backlog on our maintenance,
a huge deficit as well.

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:21.450
We don't have an EPR system in our acute,

00:58:21.450 --> 00:58:24.090
so all the things that
will be working against us,

00:58:24.090 --> 00:58:24.923
plus an ageing

00:58:24.923 --> 00:58:26.340
in older demographic as well,

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:28.440
and very much siloed working.

00:58:28.440 --> 00:58:32.190
So, incredibly polite people
here in Norfolk and Waveney,

00:58:32.190 --> 00:58:34.170
but not an awful lot of system working,

00:58:34.170 --> 00:58:36.960
a lot of understanding
about what happens outside

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:40.494
of the boundaries of the
particular organisations.

00:58:40.494 --> 00:58:42.240
A year ago, over Christmas,

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:44.040
well, we went into critical incident

00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:47.849
in '22 on the 1st of October
and came out in February 23.

00:58:47.849 --> 00:58:50.700
We had an ambulance delay
on a Sunday afternoon

00:58:50.700 --> 00:58:53.070
at one point that was
about to hit 24 hours.

00:58:53.070 --> 00:58:55.353
And I still can't believe
I'm saying that out loud.

00:58:55.353 --> 00:58:57.360
And we had 40 ambulances

00:58:57.360 --> 00:58:59.010
outside of the Norfolk
and Norwich on one day

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:00.240
between Christmas and New Year.

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:03.783
And I nearly had to declare a
major incident. It was awful.

00:59:04.740 --> 00:59:07.710
Amazing support actually
from the tier one group,

00:59:07.710 --> 00:59:11.970
so around UEC and around
discharge in the ECIST teams.

00:59:11.970 --> 00:59:14.160
And we really did focus
on those two things.

00:59:14.160 --> 00:59:17.430
So, discharge and also
long length of stay.

00:59:17.430 --> 00:59:19.290
We stopped calling it discharge here.

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:20.670
We call it RightCare now,

00:59:20.670 --> 00:59:23.280
because we want to actually
actively pull people

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:24.480
into the right services.

00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:27.810
It's not about pushing people
out and focusing on that.

00:59:27.810 --> 00:59:29.160
An awful lot of empathy

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:31.200
and joint working and joint teams.

00:59:31.200 --> 00:59:32.730
So, as an example,

00:59:32.730 --> 00:59:34.950
the Home First team from
Norfolk County Council

00:59:34.950 --> 00:59:38.040
went to work within the
community care organisation.

00:59:38.040 --> 00:59:40.260
Our scheduled care hub
is doing incredibly well,

00:59:40.260 --> 00:59:42.750
because it's co-located with a 111 service

00:59:42.750 --> 00:59:44.490
and we've got that multidisciplinary team,

00:59:44.490 --> 00:59:47.490
so it's able to really
pitch above its weight.

00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:49.650
So, we managed to reduce our non-criteria

00:59:49.650 --> 00:59:52.980
to reside number by around
200 working together

00:59:52.980 --> 00:59:56.220
and also pull back our
long length of stay.

00:59:56.220 --> 00:59:58.320
We were looking into this
winter thinking, right,

00:59:58.320 --> 01:00:01.560
we're going to target that 45-minute C2,

01:00:01.560 --> 01:00:03.660
and then, sorry,
ambulance handover rather.

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:06.270
And then, try and get
down to the half hour.

01:00:06.270 --> 01:00:08.970
Nick and I attended a national
meeting before Christmas

01:00:08.970 --> 01:00:11.250
and we were one of several trusts

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:14.255
who had a presentation
from Sarah-Jane and Julian.

01:00:14.255 --> 01:00:16.650
And we came out to that and said, oh,

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:18.330
could we just say

01:00:18.330 --> 01:00:20.130
we're not going to
tolerate ambulance waits?

01:00:20.130 --> 01:00:22.620
Could we do that? Is that
going to be possible?

01:00:22.620 --> 01:00:25.230
And we thought, well, we can't
just do that as one trust,

01:00:25.230 --> 01:00:27.300
because if we do, all of the ambulances

01:00:27.300 --> 01:00:29.310
are going to go there with
the best will in the world.

01:00:29.310 --> 01:00:31.230
That's where you're going to go as a crew.

01:00:31.230 --> 01:00:33.420
So, could we do this as a system?

01:00:33.420 --> 01:00:35.760
Came back to Norfolk and
Waveney, spoke to all the chairs,

01:00:35.760 --> 01:00:37.920
they were fantastic and behind this.

01:00:37.920 --> 01:00:39.673
And although they're not here,

01:00:39.673 --> 01:00:41.700
I really do have to mention specifically

01:00:41.700 --> 01:00:43.350
Jo Segasby and Alice Webster,

01:00:43.350 --> 01:00:45.420
the two chief execs of
the district generals,

01:00:45.420 --> 01:00:48.663
because this is not easy to
pull off in a district general.

01:00:49.560 --> 01:00:51.000
Massive culture change.

01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:52.830
And we did it incredibly quickly.

01:00:52.830 --> 01:00:55.290
So, Nick always has to go first,

01:00:55.290 --> 01:00:58.200
decided that he'd go first
on the 17th of November

01:00:58.200 --> 01:01:02.190
and the two district generals
on the 1st of December.

01:01:02.190 --> 01:01:03.930
It has not been easy,

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:07.320
but it has been an incredible
success in terms of looking

01:01:07.320 --> 01:01:08.580
at what has been achieved

01:01:08.580 --> 01:01:11.730
and the difference between
last winter and this winter.

01:01:11.730 --> 01:01:12.870
We've had our challenges.

01:01:12.870 --> 01:01:15.390
Of course we had the
last probably 10 days,

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:16.980
two weeks, we're proper in winter,

01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:20.010
and it's going to be for the
next two, three weeks as well.

01:01:20.010 --> 01:01:22.110
But it's nothing like it was a year ago.

01:01:22.110 --> 01:01:26.340
And when we do struggle, the
whole system helps each other.

01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:27.750
You can see on the gold calls

01:01:27.750 --> 01:01:29.820
that everybody really cares about this

01:01:29.820 --> 01:01:32.550
and wants to make sure people
are not on the kitchen floor.

01:01:32.550 --> 01:01:34.384
And we do recover much quickly.

01:01:34.384 --> 01:01:36.630
We know that we are putting in place

01:01:36.630 --> 01:01:39.390
the long-term transformation
to make this work.

01:01:39.390 --> 01:01:41.700
And we have exciting plans coming up.

01:01:41.700 --> 01:01:43.140
We're putting in the OPTICA system,

01:01:43.140 --> 01:01:45.780
so the transfer of care
documents will all be online

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:47.907
and that's going to
happen around March time.

01:01:47.907 --> 01:01:51.450
And new intermediate care model,
new facilities opening up,

01:01:51.450 --> 01:01:52.770
doing things very differently

01:01:52.770 --> 01:01:54.090
as we work through.

01:01:54.090 --> 01:01:55.350
And what's been really interesting,

01:01:55.350 --> 01:01:56.550
and I won't steal Nick's thunder,

01:01:56.550 --> 01:01:58.350
but we haven't done
the old fashioned thing

01:01:58.350 --> 01:02:01.530
of jumping on one problem and
letting something else go off.

01:02:01.530 --> 01:02:03.660
Actually, Norfolk and
Norwich's performance

01:02:03.660 --> 01:02:07.470
around FDS, 65-week cancer, 78-week waits,

01:02:07.470 --> 01:02:09.000
they are still tackling that.

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:11.637
We will build on this, we will improve.

01:02:11.637 --> 01:02:14.670
We have pretty much kept
to our financial targets.

01:02:14.670 --> 01:02:17.760
So, for the last period
of industrial action,

01:02:17.760 --> 01:02:19.830
we are absolutely determined here

01:02:19.830 --> 01:02:20.910
that Norfolk and Waveney

01:02:20.910 --> 01:02:22.800
will work together well as a system.

01:02:22.800 --> 01:02:25.680
We'll tackle the really
difficult problems one by one,

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:27.627
but make sure they're sustainable as well.

01:02:27.627 --> 01:02:29.760
And it's an incredible privilege actually

01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:31.860
to be leading this system.

01:02:31.860 --> 01:02:33.180
- Thank you.

01:02:33.180 --> 01:02:35.971
Nick, I think the microphone
goes to you, doesn't it?

01:02:35.971 --> 01:02:37.407
- Yeah, yeah. Good afternoon, colleagues.

01:02:37.407 --> 01:02:39.675
And thank you for inviting
me, privileged to be here.

01:02:39.675 --> 01:02:41.670
So, I'm the interim chief executive

01:02:41.670 --> 01:02:42.503
at Norfolk and Norwich.

01:02:42.503 --> 01:02:44.310
I started in the middle of September

01:02:44.310 --> 01:02:46.410
and I have another four weeks in post

01:02:46.410 --> 01:02:49.650
and I return to Suffolk
at Northeast Essex,

01:02:49.650 --> 01:02:52.620
hopefully not with my
tail between my legs.

01:02:52.620 --> 01:02:55.260
So, I suppose the first approach

01:02:55.260 --> 01:02:57.120
that I took when I came in in September

01:02:57.120 --> 01:02:59.100
was to look at the data

01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:01.058
and start to do a bit of myth-busting.

01:03:01.058 --> 01:03:05.010
The organisational mantra
was all of these delays

01:03:05.010 --> 01:03:07.500
are caused by delays of social care,

01:03:07.500 --> 01:03:10.770
delays of community services,
and stranded patients,

01:03:10.770 --> 01:03:12.930
with patients without
a criteria to reside.

01:03:12.930 --> 01:03:14.411
As Tracey described,

01:03:14.411 --> 01:03:17.070
the system had done so
well to reduce those,

01:03:17.070 --> 01:03:19.497
but that information
hadn't filtered through.

01:03:19.497 --> 01:03:23.190
And so, that myth-busting
based on the data

01:03:23.190 --> 01:03:25.170
and then a conversation
with senior colleagues,

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:27.741
including clinicians about
where's the greatest risk,

01:03:27.741 --> 01:03:30.220
and the greatest risk in the community

01:03:30.220 --> 01:03:32.700
is the undifferentiated patient

01:03:32.700 --> 01:03:34.800
being at home without an ambulance.

01:03:34.800 --> 01:03:37.219
And that was a difficult
message to keep pushing,

01:03:37.219 --> 01:03:38.760
but that was agreed.

01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:40.830
And so, just to give some data,

01:03:40.830 --> 01:03:42.960
'cause I was told to bring some numbers,

01:03:42.960 --> 01:03:45.360
Sarah-Jane said, "Bring some numbers."

01:03:45.360 --> 01:03:48.430
So, in October, the average offload time

01:03:48.430 --> 01:03:52.290
at the Norfolk and
Norwich was 142 minutes.

01:03:52.290 --> 01:03:54.330
That's the average offload time.

01:03:54.330 --> 01:03:57.870
And 59% of patients
waited more than an hour

01:03:57.870 --> 01:04:00.420
on an ambulance outside the hospital.

01:04:00.420 --> 01:04:02.850
In November, that dropped to 63 minutes

01:04:02.850 --> 01:04:05.700
for an average offload with 28 patients,

01:04:05.700 --> 01:04:08.907
28% of waits, patient's waiting an hour.

01:04:08.907 --> 01:04:11.070
And in December, it reduced even further

01:04:11.070 --> 01:04:14.040
to 24 minutes average offload delay,

01:04:14.040 --> 01:04:16.860
that's taking two hours off
the average offload delay

01:04:16.860 --> 01:04:19.830
and 8% waiting more than an hour.

01:04:19.830 --> 01:04:22.380
It has increased a little
bit through January

01:04:22.380 --> 01:04:26.340
with 31 minutes average offload

01:04:26.340 --> 01:04:29.190
and 13% waiting more than an hour.

01:04:29.190 --> 01:04:33.990
An extraordinary turnaround led
by the extraordinary people.

01:04:33.990 --> 01:04:36.621
However, it has come at a cost.

01:04:36.621 --> 01:04:39.477
And what we have done is quite simply

01:04:39.477 --> 01:04:43.329
move the risk from people's
homes into the corridors,

01:04:43.329 --> 01:04:45.000
into the additional patients

01:04:45.000 --> 01:04:47.220
that we're caring for on the wards.

01:04:47.220 --> 01:04:49.980
And that is really
difficult for the staff,

01:04:49.980 --> 01:04:51.720
but it is better for the community.

01:04:51.720 --> 01:04:56.097
And I think that challenge
of understanding,

01:04:56.097 --> 01:04:58.440
the understanding that our clinicians

01:04:58.440 --> 01:05:00.633
and our nurses and all
of our staff have now

01:05:00.633 --> 01:05:02.490
that this is making a difference

01:05:02.490 --> 01:05:04.470
to the communities that we serve,

01:05:04.470 --> 01:05:07.020
although it is putting
significant pressure

01:05:07.020 --> 01:05:08.790
on your working days.

01:05:08.790 --> 01:05:10.680
And as I often say to the staff,

01:05:10.680 --> 01:05:13.290
you will never know the names of the lives

01:05:13.290 --> 01:05:14.910
of the people that you have saved.

01:05:14.910 --> 01:05:17.520
You will never know
the names of the people

01:05:17.520 --> 01:05:20.190
who will have a better
recovery after a stroke

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:21.060
or a heart attack,

01:05:21.060 --> 01:05:24.180
because they've had a quicker
response in the ambulance.

01:05:24.180 --> 01:05:27.469
And that's really difficult,
because they can see the pain,

01:05:27.469 --> 01:05:30.480
but they can't necessarily
see the benefit.

01:05:30.480 --> 01:05:32.986
So, I think one of the
challenges we have as leaders

01:05:32.986 --> 01:05:36.030
is to always get the
narrative exactly clear

01:05:36.030 --> 01:05:39.750
about why people are doing
it and why we're doing it.

01:05:39.750 --> 01:05:41.463
My goodness, is it fragile?

01:05:42.540 --> 01:05:44.250
I have not relaxed and thought,

01:05:44.250 --> 01:05:46.770
well, that's done, move
on, go back to Suffolk.

01:05:46.770 --> 01:05:50.040
It is absolutely minute by minute.

01:05:50.040 --> 01:05:51.750
So, what we're attempting

01:05:51.750 --> 01:05:53.550
to do is to create a space

01:05:53.550 --> 01:05:56.730
for the next 10 ambulances every hour,

01:05:56.730 --> 01:05:57.900
the next 10 ambulances

01:05:57.900 --> 01:05:59.790
every hour, 24/7,

01:05:59.790 --> 01:06:01.080
7 days a week.

01:06:01.080 --> 01:06:03.750
And that does mean looking after patients

01:06:03.750 --> 01:06:06.510
in areas that are not designed
to look after patients.

01:06:06.510 --> 01:06:09.992
It does mean asking staff
to do things differently.

01:06:09.992 --> 01:06:13.860
It is building the plane
whilst trying to fly it.

01:06:13.860 --> 01:06:16.110
So, all the transformation
work we need to do

01:06:16.110 --> 01:06:17.610
around seven-day working,

01:06:17.610 --> 01:06:20.190
around working with people's job plans

01:06:20.190 --> 01:06:21.030
to ensure that we've got

01:06:21.030 --> 01:06:23.430
more senior decision-makers
at the front door.

01:06:23.430 --> 01:06:24.480
All the work we need to do

01:06:24.480 --> 01:06:27.120
with our colleagues in
the community is the work

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:30.270
that we've now started
with a very detailed

01:06:30.270 --> 01:06:32.400
and managed transformation programme

01:06:32.400 --> 01:06:33.870
to take the lessons learned,

01:06:33.870 --> 01:06:36.180
and then to move into that transformation.

01:06:36.180 --> 01:06:37.710
We're not out of the woods.

01:06:37.710 --> 01:06:39.420
It is incredibly fragile,

01:06:39.420 --> 01:06:41.130
but it's been a privilege
to be part of it.

01:06:41.130 --> 01:06:42.060
Thank you, chair.

01:06:42.060 --> 01:06:42.893
- Thank you.

01:06:42.893 --> 01:06:46.290
So, I'm going to open up
for comments or questions.

01:06:46.290 --> 01:06:50.803
If I throw one first at all three of you,

01:06:51.667 --> 01:06:54.090
how confident are you, you said fragile,

01:06:54.090 --> 01:06:56.398
how confident are you that we can lock in

01:06:56.398 --> 01:06:59.160
as sort of a firm base
and now improve again,

01:06:59.160 --> 01:07:02.130
getting ready for next winter?

01:07:02.130 --> 01:07:03.540
That would be my question.

01:07:03.540 --> 01:07:04.530
- We're absolutely committed

01:07:04.530 --> 01:07:05.790
to doing that, aren't we?

01:07:05.790 --> 01:07:06.690
- But confident...

01:07:06.690 --> 01:07:08.070
Commitment, I get.

01:07:08.070 --> 01:07:13.070
I get commitment. Confidence.

01:07:13.125 --> 01:07:16.401
- I think from my perspective,

01:07:16.401 --> 01:07:20.940
the one shift that I have
noticed within the hospital side,

01:07:20.940 --> 01:07:22.290
in the acute side,

01:07:22.290 --> 01:07:24.960
is the recognition of
why people are doing it.

01:07:24.960 --> 01:07:28.200
So, the hearts and minds
of people recognising.

01:07:28.200 --> 01:07:29.107
And there's one nurse said to me,

01:07:29.107 --> 01:07:30.480
"Nick, if you'd asked me to do this

01:07:30.480 --> 01:07:33.650
to improve ambulance turnaround,
I wouldn't have done it."

01:07:33.650 --> 01:07:34.630
- Yeah.
- What you've done about

01:07:34.630 --> 01:07:37.999
is about saving lives,
it's about improving lives.

01:07:37.999 --> 01:07:39.720
And there is something very different

01:07:39.720 --> 01:07:41.627
that I've learned working outside London

01:07:41.627 --> 01:07:43.890
is that almost all the patient,

01:07:43.890 --> 01:07:46.350
all the staff who use the hospital,

01:07:46.350 --> 01:07:48.060
it's their community,
- Yeah.

01:07:48.060 --> 01:07:50.310
- and therefore, we're
talking about treating people

01:07:50.310 --> 01:07:53.580
from your village, from your
city, from your community.

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:56.130
And linking into that hearts

01:07:56.130 --> 01:07:58.380
and minds has been really important.

01:07:58.380 --> 01:07:59.703
My degree of confidence,

01:08:01.114 --> 01:08:03.210
I think it's a really difficult question,

01:08:03.210 --> 01:08:04.193
which is probably why you asked it.

01:08:05.098 --> 01:08:07.320
But I think that...

01:08:07.320 --> 01:08:08.153
So, I'm confident

01:08:08.153 --> 01:08:09.900
that there's been a shift
in hearts and minds,

01:08:09.900 --> 01:08:11.370
that I'm confident there's been a shift

01:08:11.370 --> 01:08:15.065
in attitude and some shift in behaviours.

01:08:15.065 --> 01:08:17.730
But I think in a sense,
the heavy work starts now,

01:08:17.730 --> 01:08:20.010
because it does require talking

01:08:20.010 --> 01:08:22.544
to people who've worked
traditionally for 20 years

01:08:22.544 --> 01:08:25.176
about a different way of
delivering their job plan.

01:08:25.176 --> 01:08:27.240
It does require asking people

01:08:27.240 --> 01:08:29.626
to take a different approach to risk

01:08:29.626 --> 01:08:31.620
around sending people home

01:08:31.620 --> 01:08:33.960
when they're well enough and not well.

01:08:33.960 --> 01:08:35.230
So, I think that's the heavy work.

01:08:35.230 --> 01:08:36.480
- Yeah.
- So, I'm confident

01:08:36.480 --> 01:08:38.918
of the change of attitude,

01:08:38.918 --> 01:08:41.040
some change in behaviour,

01:08:41.040 --> 01:08:44.220
but I think the delivery is,
as I say, incredibly fragile.

01:08:44.220 --> 01:08:46.243
- Thank you. Amanda, I
know you want to ask it.

01:08:46.243 --> 01:08:48.810
- Well, it's a build actually,
if that's all right, Richard,

01:08:48.810 --> 01:08:50.790
on the question you just
asked about confidence

01:08:50.790 --> 01:08:53.268
and it's what...

01:08:53.268 --> 01:08:56.640
I'm interested in
understanding a little bit

01:08:56.640 --> 01:08:59.175
from both of your perspectives
about where the system

01:08:59.175 --> 01:09:01.487
adds value

01:09:01.487 --> 01:09:03.090
by being able to do things

01:09:03.090 --> 01:09:05.850
that an organisation on its
own wouldn't be able to do.

01:09:05.850 --> 01:09:09.660
So, in terms of sustaining
that kind of, well, Nick,

01:09:09.660 --> 01:09:10.800
you've described it brilliantly,

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:15.147
transforming at the same
time as actually doing,

01:09:15.147 --> 01:09:18.341
doing the hard yards operational delivery.

01:09:18.341 --> 01:09:21.330
You've touched on
community, keeping people,

01:09:21.330 --> 01:09:23.520
patients out of hospital
who don't need to be there.

01:09:23.520 --> 01:09:26.640
You've touched on discharge,
you've touched on capacity,

01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:29.160
you've touched on changing pathways.

01:09:29.160 --> 01:09:30.330
So, I've just be really interested

01:09:30.330 --> 01:09:32.070
from both of you in the sort of,

01:09:32.070 --> 01:09:33.390
so what's your reflection

01:09:33.390 --> 01:09:36.900
on what's made both it possible now,

01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:38.940
because of system rather
than just organisation

01:09:38.940 --> 01:09:41.577
and what would then be the next year plan?

01:09:41.577 --> 01:09:44.700
And by the way, because
we've been talking about data

01:09:44.700 --> 01:09:46.320
and how important it is, as I'm talking,

01:09:46.320 --> 01:09:49.170
I've called up what your
Opal position is right now

01:09:49.170 --> 01:09:50.130
and how many ambulances

01:09:50.130 --> 01:09:52.650
there are outside the Norfolk
and Norwich right now.

01:09:52.650 --> 01:09:54.030
So, your point about the pressure

01:09:54.030 --> 01:09:56.940
and the fragility is clearly really real.

01:09:56.940 --> 01:09:57.773
And that's why I think
- Yeah.

01:09:57.773 --> 01:09:59.160
- this is such an important

01:09:59.160 --> 01:10:00.450
kind of conversation.
- She does this to me

01:10:00.450 --> 01:10:01.658
all the time as well.
- Yeah.

01:10:05.160 --> 01:10:06.840
- And if you told me three months ago

01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:08.340
that this is the day
you were going to visit,

01:10:08.340 --> 01:10:09.490
I could have predicted.

01:10:11.160 --> 01:10:12.027
Our Opal score would've been,

01:10:12.027 --> 01:10:13.713
and exactly how many ambulances.

01:10:16.664 --> 01:10:18.214
I suppose from our perspective,

01:10:18.214 --> 01:10:20.970
the system work has been really important.

01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:24.270
And I think the response for example

01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:26.760
that we've had when things
have got really difficult,

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:29.070
so there's the kind of the
building and the system

01:10:29.070 --> 01:10:32.580
and if you like, what
we embed into system.

01:10:32.580 --> 01:10:34.440
But then there's the the balloon goes up,

01:10:34.440 --> 01:10:35.940
we need your help now.
- Mm.

01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:37.020
- And for both of those,

01:10:37.020 --> 01:10:39.960
I think it's a really
good system to work in.

01:10:39.960 --> 01:10:43.050
For me, I think where I to be staying,

01:10:43.050 --> 01:10:44.700
I would absolutely concentrate,

01:10:44.700 --> 01:10:47.397
and I think it's, it's not
so much at the back end.

01:10:47.397 --> 01:10:51.060
And I think what we learned,
if there was anything positive

01:10:51.060 --> 01:10:52.470
to take from the industrial action,

01:10:52.470 --> 01:10:53.970
we do know that if we have

01:10:53.970 --> 01:10:55.230
senior decision-makers

01:10:55.230 --> 01:10:57.750
at the front door 24/7
- Yeah.

01:10:57.750 --> 01:10:59.790
- with the ability to refer patients

01:10:59.790 --> 01:11:02.040
to pathways that are not
acute hospital-based,

01:11:02.040 --> 01:11:04.371
and that's the key, and
that's where the community,

01:11:04.371 --> 01:11:06.060
and that's where hot clinics

01:11:06.060 --> 01:11:08.196
and that's where quicker
diagnostics come in.

01:11:08.196 --> 01:11:12.660
And it is admitting people to assess them,

01:11:12.660 --> 01:11:13.620
assessing them to admit them

01:11:13.620 --> 01:11:14.970
rather than the other way around.

01:11:14.970 --> 01:11:17.730
So, that's where I concentrate
future system working

01:11:17.730 --> 01:11:20.430
is absolutely about why is it

01:11:20.430 --> 01:11:21.660
that after seven o'clock at night,

01:11:21.660 --> 01:11:25.410
the most significant criteria
for admission is living alone.

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:27.540
What can we do about people

01:11:27.540 --> 01:11:28.890
where we can wrap round something?

01:11:28.890 --> 01:11:30.480
So, that doesn't mean they get admitted

01:11:30.480 --> 01:11:33.180
just for no clinical reason
except that they live alone.

01:11:33.180 --> 01:11:35.790
So, that's where I'd be
looking for the system

01:11:35.790 --> 01:11:38.190
to really step up is alternatives

01:11:38.190 --> 01:11:39.990
to admission rather than...

01:11:39.990 --> 01:11:43.693
We will have delayed discharges sadly for,

01:11:43.693 --> 01:11:46.677
that will always in the
sense, be part of our systems.

01:11:46.677 --> 01:11:48.450
I think where we can really get the gain

01:11:48.450 --> 01:11:49.410
is to avoid admissions.

01:11:49.410 --> 01:11:50.850
- Thank you. So, Wol's got a...

01:11:50.850 --> 01:11:52.260
I'm going to move us on quite quickly,

01:11:52.260 --> 01:11:53.805
but Wol's got a question-
- Very good question.

01:11:53.805 --> 01:11:54.937
- Tracey's answer to that.

01:11:54.937 --> 01:11:55.770
- Oh sorry, of course.

01:11:55.770 --> 01:11:57.480
Sorry, Tracey, your answer

01:11:57.480 --> 01:11:59.070
and there are two more questions coming,

01:11:59.070 --> 01:12:01.327
and then I'm going to start
trenching the time I think.

01:12:01.327 --> 01:12:02.160
But anyway.

01:12:02.160 --> 01:12:03.330
- So, from a system perspective,

01:12:03.330 --> 01:12:05.520
Amanda, I can tell you
this afternoon that NCH&amp;C

01:12:05.520 --> 01:12:07.650
have opened seven more
surge beds in the community

01:12:07.650 --> 01:12:09.060
and we're having a four o'clock meeting

01:12:09.060 --> 01:12:09.893
to look at discharge,

01:12:09.893 --> 01:12:11.010
specifically to Norfolk

01:12:11.010 --> 01:12:12.300
and Norwich, so that whole system

01:12:12.300 --> 01:12:14.845
is pulling together this
afternoon, not just for you.

01:12:17.100 --> 01:12:18.450
In terms of sustainability,

01:12:18.450 --> 01:12:21.390
we know that we have made big inroads

01:12:21.390 --> 01:12:23.787
into our non-CTR number
and improving right hand

01:12:23.787 --> 01:12:26.460
out the back door as we
call it, with discharge.

01:12:26.460 --> 01:12:28.967
That will be sustained and
we'll keep improving on that.

01:12:28.967 --> 01:12:31.860
We are doing some great
work actually in social care

01:12:31.860 --> 01:12:34.470
about downgrading people from P2 to P1,

01:12:34.470 --> 01:12:36.252
making sure they retain
their independence.

01:12:36.252 --> 01:12:37.710
But one of the big things

01:12:37.710 --> 01:12:39.420
that struck me over the past few weeks

01:12:39.420 --> 01:12:41.190
and we are now able to really hone down

01:12:41.190 --> 01:12:42.315
to some of these issues,

01:12:42.315 --> 01:12:45.150
as a number of people aged over 75 over,

01:12:45.150 --> 01:12:47.100
going in with respiratory conditions

01:12:47.100 --> 01:12:48.000
and they're being admitted

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:49.740
'cause they're knocked off their feet.

01:12:49.740 --> 01:12:52.350
We need a completely different
model for looking at people,

01:12:52.350 --> 01:12:53.550
keeping people in their own home,

01:12:53.550 --> 01:12:55.230
admission avoidance and being able

01:12:55.230 --> 01:12:58.230
to look after them with
respiratory illness over Christmas.

01:12:58.230 --> 01:12:59.790
And we'll be focusing on that.

01:12:59.790 --> 01:13:00.685
- Thank you.

01:13:00.685 --> 01:13:02.040
So, I'm going to ask-

01:13:02.040 --> 01:13:03.360
- Just very quickly.
- Wol, you ask your question,

01:13:03.360 --> 01:13:05.850
then Steve, and then take
them both as it were.

01:13:05.850 --> 01:13:07.080
- Very, very quickly.

01:13:07.080 --> 01:13:10.650
We've been talking about
risk, but lumping it into one.

01:13:10.650 --> 01:13:12.360
I'm just going to just
know how you break it down,

01:13:12.360 --> 01:13:15.270
because I'm going to characterise
this completely unfairly,

01:13:15.270 --> 01:13:18.150
but clinical risk has trumped everything

01:13:18.150 --> 01:13:21.821
in the past, clearly
done quite extraordinary

01:13:21.821 --> 01:13:24.420
performance improvement
in the space of months.

01:13:24.420 --> 01:13:27.150
That must have involved
taking other kinds of risk

01:13:27.150 --> 01:13:28.808
and putting them up to the same level,

01:13:28.808 --> 01:13:31.500
and then working together
with clinicians and managers

01:13:31.500 --> 01:13:34.740
and what have you, to say,
actually, this is okay.

01:13:34.740 --> 01:13:35.573
Has that been part of it?

01:13:35.573 --> 01:13:37.620
And the real question is going forward,

01:13:37.620 --> 01:13:40.200
where else can we start to unpack risk,

01:13:40.200 --> 01:13:41.760
really understand it,

01:13:41.760 --> 01:13:44.640
using data to say actually,
we can do this in this way.

01:13:44.640 --> 01:13:45.690
- So, hold...

01:13:45.690 --> 01:13:47.580
You're going to get
three questions to hold,

01:13:47.580 --> 01:13:48.750
so you might want to scribble,

01:13:48.750 --> 01:13:49.647
'cause David was also coming in.

01:13:49.647 --> 01:13:50.480
So, Steve?

01:13:50.480 --> 01:13:51.660
- Well, a comment and a question,

01:13:51.660 --> 01:13:53.340
because I think the story that you've told

01:13:53.340 --> 01:13:55.530
about that understanding of risk

01:13:55.530 --> 01:13:58.800
and the risk that is occurring
in a group of the population

01:13:58.800 --> 01:14:00.240
that is not in front of your eyes

01:14:00.240 --> 01:14:01.230
and you're not dealing with

01:14:01.230 --> 01:14:04.650
is a really important cultural change.

01:14:04.650 --> 01:14:07.410
And actually, I've seen
that in many places

01:14:07.410 --> 01:14:08.757
I visited over the course of this year.

01:14:08.757 --> 01:14:10.500
And the difference between last winter

01:14:10.500 --> 01:14:12.990
where people were talking
about it, to this year,

01:14:12.990 --> 01:14:14.940
where I think increasingly,
people have understood

01:14:14.940 --> 01:14:17.970
and acted on it, I think is
one of the real differences.

01:14:17.970 --> 01:14:19.440
But of course, it's not the only thing

01:14:19.440 --> 01:14:21.120
that's made a difference this winter.

01:14:21.120 --> 01:14:23.321
And you've touched on some of the things.

01:14:23.321 --> 01:14:26.730
And there are other areas
such as virtual wards.

01:14:26.730 --> 01:14:29.150
And I just wondered from your perspective,

01:14:29.150 --> 01:14:31.980
how far have you got with
some of those other things

01:14:31.980 --> 01:14:35.425
and what is the opportunity
to go further for next winter?

01:14:35.425 --> 01:14:39.630
- And then, the last
question in the batch. David?

01:14:39.630 --> 01:14:42.930
- It's more of a point really
that I just want to draw out.

01:14:42.930 --> 01:14:46.020
I think in a private meeting,
we actually acknowledge

01:14:46.020 --> 01:14:47.640
just the progress that has been made

01:14:47.640 --> 01:14:51.120
to build improvements
on comparative months,

01:14:51.120 --> 01:14:54.420
previous years, and congratulated Emily

01:14:54.420 --> 01:14:56.103
and Sarah-Jane for the work

01:14:56.103 --> 01:14:58.050
with the teams that have gone on.

01:14:58.050 --> 01:14:59.100
But I just want to pull out

01:14:59.100 --> 01:15:00.990
what Tracey and Nick have talked about,

01:15:00.990 --> 01:15:03.630
because I think there's a
lesson in leadership here

01:15:03.630 --> 01:15:05.730
about how to lead.

01:15:05.730 --> 01:15:08.100
I think complex problems require teams

01:15:08.100 --> 01:15:11.010
to solve that made up of
different groups of people.

01:15:11.010 --> 01:15:12.570
But unless they collaborate

01:15:12.570 --> 01:15:14.730
and work compassionately with each other,

01:15:14.730 --> 01:15:17.940
that's Nick's point about
how do you harness the hearts

01:15:17.940 --> 01:15:19.650
and minds of people rather than say,

01:15:19.650 --> 01:15:23.250
we've got a target, please
deliver on this, is critical.

01:15:23.250 --> 01:15:26.580
And I just don't think
as a national board,

01:15:26.580 --> 01:15:27.924
we should let that point go,

01:15:27.924 --> 01:15:30.350
because it's absolutely critical, I think,

01:15:30.350 --> 01:15:33.603
to how you motivate
people and give them hope

01:15:33.603 --> 01:15:35.520
that the reason they went into this

01:15:35.520 --> 01:15:38.070
in the first place is
still relevant today.

01:15:38.070 --> 01:15:41.400
So, well done. Congratulations.

01:15:41.400 --> 01:15:43.080
- Thank you.
- So, it's a very good

01:15:43.080 --> 01:15:45.390
reflection, which I think
we would all agree with.

01:15:45.390 --> 01:15:46.223
- Yeah.

01:15:47.070 --> 01:15:51.990
- And actually, thank
you for, at which point,

01:15:51.990 --> 01:15:53.947
you've got two questions, I think,

01:15:53.947 --> 01:15:56.400
to have a go at, if you
can, between you and Nick.

01:15:56.400 --> 01:15:57.233
- Thank you.

01:15:57.233 --> 01:15:58.500
So, very quickly on virtual ward,

01:15:58.500 --> 01:16:01.740
we leaned into very early
on, step up as well.

01:16:01.740 --> 01:16:04.200
Step down actually and being
able to flex those models

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:07.500
and we are absolutely looking
at how we can both learn from

01:16:07.500 --> 01:16:09.150
and improve those models and extend them.

01:16:09.150 --> 01:16:11.370
So, they are part of our planning.

01:16:11.370 --> 01:16:14.700
I will hand over to
Nick to talk about risk,

01:16:14.700 --> 01:16:16.470
but I do want to mention
that when we have looked

01:16:16.470 --> 01:16:19.020
from an ICB perspective
and looking at system risk,

01:16:19.020 --> 01:16:21.780
we have introduced patient
experience and moral injury,

01:16:21.780 --> 01:16:23.303
because I think it's been really
important to capture those,

01:16:23.303 --> 01:16:25.530
so they don't get conflated in wider risk.

01:16:25.530 --> 01:16:27.450
But I'll just bring Nick in.

01:16:27.450 --> 01:16:28.500
- Thank you.

01:16:28.500 --> 01:16:29.790
What we attempted to do,

01:16:29.790 --> 01:16:32.700
and I'm delighted that we'll
be hosting some colleagues

01:16:32.700 --> 01:16:34.530
tomorrow at the hospital

01:16:34.530 --> 01:16:36.689
and you'll see some more detail of this,

01:16:36.689 --> 01:16:40.590
and I'll share it around with
the papers if that's okay,

01:16:40.590 --> 01:16:43.680
is that we have a minimum
risk escalation matrix,

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:46.680
so that we say that we
agree that as a trust,

01:16:46.680 --> 01:16:48.390
that the trust will not accept delays

01:16:48.390 --> 01:16:49.530
to patients requiring

01:16:49.530 --> 01:16:51.510
an ambulance in their place of residence

01:16:51.510 --> 01:16:53.610
is the highest risk to our system,

01:16:53.610 --> 01:16:56.310
not necessarily to our
organisation, for our system.

01:16:56.310 --> 01:16:57.570
And then, it works down

01:16:57.570 --> 01:17:00.060
with the lowest risk
being business as usual.

01:17:00.060 --> 01:17:01.860
So, we go from risk of being the highest

01:17:01.860 --> 01:17:03.780
is that people in their own homes.

01:17:03.780 --> 01:17:06.900
The next is people in ambulance,
the next is people in ED.

01:17:06.900 --> 01:17:08.820
And then, finally, we're going
to get into the real detail

01:17:08.820 --> 01:17:12.540
around the escalation plan that
we have within the hospital.

01:17:12.540 --> 01:17:14.670
We're very clear about, for example,

01:17:14.670 --> 01:17:16.290
how many additional patients on a ward,

01:17:16.290 --> 01:17:18.690
and then working through the lowest risk

01:17:18.690 --> 01:17:22.170
is probably corridor care
in ED, which is not great

01:17:22.170 --> 01:17:25.170
and nobody wants that,
right through to caring

01:17:25.170 --> 01:17:27.873
for additional patients
in areas on the ward

01:17:27.873 --> 01:17:29.160
that are not designed for those patients.

01:17:29.160 --> 01:17:31.620
So, we've got a very clear risk matrix

01:17:31.620 --> 01:17:33.670
and we have various escalation figures.

01:17:33.670 --> 01:17:36.945
Have we got complete
buy-in from everybody? No.

01:17:36.945 --> 01:17:39.955
It's absolutely a work in progress

01:17:39.955 --> 01:17:42.060
and I'm sure for those of you

01:17:42.060 --> 01:17:43.650
who I'll be delighted to host tomorrow,

01:17:43.650 --> 01:17:45.720
you'll hear a range of views

01:17:45.720 --> 01:17:47.390
about the approach that we're taking.

01:17:47.390 --> 01:17:51.138
But I think in a sense, the simplicity,

01:17:51.138 --> 01:17:53.130
and I do have the confidence

01:17:53.130 --> 01:17:55.530
that what we have done
has made a difference

01:17:55.530 --> 01:17:56.847
to lives that that we'll never know

01:17:56.847 --> 01:17:59.050
and that's really, really
difficult, I think.

01:17:59.050 --> 01:18:00.840
But I also remember,

01:18:00.840 --> 01:18:03.360
it's a credit to the
national team, really.

01:18:03.360 --> 01:18:08.360
I do remember that webinar
probably a year ago,

01:18:09.030 --> 01:18:10.470
Steve, maybe slightly longer,

01:18:10.470 --> 01:18:12.313
where you were absolutely clear

01:18:12.313 --> 01:18:15.510
about the shared risk of a system.

01:18:15.510 --> 01:18:17.312
And I would agree with you

01:18:17.312 --> 01:18:22.312
that they were words and
expectation a year ago.

01:18:22.710 --> 01:18:25.410
I think right across and
certainly my experience,

01:18:25.410 --> 01:18:27.360
which has only mainly in east of England,

01:18:27.360 --> 01:18:28.920
we've seen it actually delivered.

01:18:28.920 --> 01:18:30.180
And maybe there's a lesson there

01:18:30.180 --> 01:18:32.207
about recognising that sometimes,

01:18:32.207 --> 01:18:36.300
it does take time for
people to get to that place.

01:18:36.300 --> 01:18:37.133
- Thank you.

01:18:37.133 --> 01:18:40.560
And, Tracey, thank you.
And, Sarah-Jane, thank you.

01:18:40.560 --> 01:18:43.290
It's been really good
conversation actually.

01:18:43.290 --> 01:18:44.880
So, let's move on if we might.

01:18:44.880 --> 01:18:49.880
And the next item is Amanda
Doyle and David Webb,

01:18:50.070 --> 01:18:51.780
our Chief Pharmaceutical Officer,

01:18:51.780 --> 01:18:55.320
and it's an update for us
on Pharmacy First service.

01:18:55.320 --> 01:18:56.648
- Thank you.

01:18:56.648 --> 01:18:59.580
So, you may remember

01:18:59.580 --> 01:19:02.070
that when we published the delivery plan

01:19:02.070 --> 01:19:06.150
for recovering access to primary
care back in May last year,

01:19:06.150 --> 01:19:08.050
we had some commitments around

01:19:09.180 --> 01:19:12.120
how we might better
use the clinical skills

01:19:12.120 --> 01:19:15.275
and capacity within community pharmacy

01:19:15.275 --> 01:19:17.760
to move further in the direction

01:19:17.760 --> 01:19:20.103
of delivering greater clinical services.

01:19:21.540 --> 01:19:23.280
And one of the commitments

01:19:23.280 --> 01:19:25.454
was to launch the Pharmacy First service,

01:19:25.454 --> 01:19:30.450
which was an additional
range of clinical pathways

01:19:30.450 --> 01:19:32.640
for high volume common,

01:19:32.640 --> 01:19:34.706
but not necessarily minor conditions,

01:19:34.706 --> 01:19:38.460
for which although
pharmacists could recognise

01:19:38.460 --> 01:19:41.520
and advise patients, these
conditions often needed

01:19:41.520 --> 01:19:44.550
prescription only medications,
sometimes antibiotics.

01:19:44.550 --> 01:19:47.820
And so, pharmacies were unable
to treat those conditions.

01:19:47.820 --> 01:19:50.250
So, in addition to the existing

01:19:50.250 --> 01:19:53.310
community pharmacy consultation
service for minor illness,

01:19:53.310 --> 01:19:57.374
we have developed pathways
for seven common conditions.

01:19:57.374 --> 01:20:00.060
And we also committed to increase the role

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:01.860
of community pharmacy in the provision

01:20:01.860 --> 01:20:05.819
of contraceptive services
by expanding the existing

01:20:05.819 --> 01:20:07.410
prescription service

01:20:07.410 --> 01:20:09.360
for the oral contraceptive pill,

01:20:09.360 --> 01:20:11.850
but also move to allow patients

01:20:11.850 --> 01:20:15.540
to obtain first prescriptions
for oral contraceptive pill

01:20:15.540 --> 01:20:17.730
through their local community pharmacy,

01:20:17.730 --> 01:20:19.950
and also to significantly expand

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:22.740
and improve the role of community pharmacy

01:20:22.740 --> 01:20:25.743
in the diagnosis and management
of high blood pressure.

01:20:28.551 --> 01:20:30.900
As part of this, there was a commitment

01:20:30.900 --> 01:20:32.730
to a significant investment

01:20:32.730 --> 01:20:37.730
in the underpinning IT infrastructure,

01:20:38.055 --> 01:20:41.100
largely in around the connection
between general practise

01:20:41.100 --> 01:20:44.943
and community pharmacy to
enable shared access to records,

01:20:46.290 --> 01:20:49.110
smoother referral of patients

01:20:49.110 --> 01:20:51.465
into appointments in community pharmacies

01:20:51.465 --> 01:20:56.280
and information back
directly into GP records,

01:20:56.280 --> 01:20:58.290
so that the clinical information

01:20:58.290 --> 01:20:59.806
and particularly the prescriptions

01:20:59.806 --> 01:21:02.017
issued via community pharmacy

01:21:02.017 --> 01:21:05.103
become part of the
patient's electronic record.

01:21:06.000 --> 01:21:08.010
Really pleased to say that in December,

01:21:08.010 --> 01:21:10.410
we launched the expanded
contraception service

01:21:10.410 --> 01:21:11.607
and blood pressure service.

01:21:11.607 --> 01:21:12.900
And as Amanda said,

01:21:12.900 --> 01:21:16.050
we launched the new Pharmacy First service

01:21:16.050 --> 01:21:18.930
yesterday as planned.

01:21:18.930 --> 01:21:22.950
We went live in 10,200 pharmacies,

01:21:22.950 --> 01:21:25.811
which 95% of all the pharmacies in England

01:21:25.811 --> 01:21:29.550
across 16 and a half thousand sites.

01:21:29.550 --> 01:21:32.610
We had more than 9,800

01:21:32.610 --> 01:21:35.493
Pharmacy First consultations
on the first day.

01:21:37.461 --> 01:21:42.461
And this has been really,
really positively received,

01:21:43.440 --> 01:21:44.610
not only from patients,

01:21:44.610 --> 01:21:49.413
but from the pharmacy
sector who are really keen,

01:21:49.413 --> 01:21:51.030
as you can see by the numbers of signup,

01:21:51.030 --> 01:21:54.780
but really positive about the
ability to widen the range

01:21:54.780 --> 01:21:57.090
of clinical services that are delivered

01:21:57.090 --> 01:21:59.943
alongside the traditional
dispensing services.

01:22:02.732 --> 01:22:07.320
But is a game-changer around
getting us into a position

01:22:07.320 --> 01:22:09.450
to continue to develop this,

01:22:09.450 --> 01:22:13.800
and the IT infrastructure
that I cannot overstate

01:22:13.800 --> 01:22:16.687
how much hard work's being carried out

01:22:16.687 --> 01:22:21.150
by the transformation
team to get this in place.

01:22:21.150 --> 01:22:24.900
Six clinical system
suppliers made huge changes

01:22:24.900 --> 01:22:27.510
to the way their systems operate.

01:22:27.510 --> 01:22:31.560
More than 30,000 updates
to the director of services

01:22:31.560 --> 01:22:33.990
were put in place to enable 111s

01:22:33.990 --> 01:22:36.810
refer directly into these services.

01:22:36.810 --> 01:22:39.690
So, it's a lot of the
work under the surface

01:22:39.690 --> 01:22:41.160
has been absolutely huge.

01:22:41.160 --> 01:22:45.420
So, really well done to the
teams that have delivered this.

01:22:45.420 --> 01:22:46.710
David, I don't know if you wanted

01:22:46.710 --> 01:22:49.050
to comment on the clinical aspects?

01:22:49.050 --> 01:22:50.831
- Do give some time for,
- Yeah.

01:22:50.831 --> 01:22:53.010
- I'm sure there're going to
be some comments or questions.

01:22:53.010 --> 01:22:55.230
So, if you want to come
in relatively succinctly

01:22:55.230 --> 01:22:58.874
on clinical aspects.
- Yes. Thank you, chair.

01:22:58.874 --> 01:23:01.320
So, I think there are
three sort of dimensions

01:23:01.320 --> 01:23:03.780
that are really important
around this development.

01:23:03.780 --> 01:23:08.220
First one is integration.
Second one I think is inclusion.

01:23:08.220 --> 01:23:09.660
And Amanda pointed to the fact

01:23:09.660 --> 01:23:11.970
that we have more pharmacies in areas

01:23:11.970 --> 01:23:13.260
of higher deprivation

01:23:13.260 --> 01:23:15.120
than we do in areas of lower deprivation,

01:23:15.120 --> 01:23:16.890
which I think is a very important point.

01:23:16.890 --> 01:23:18.750
And then, the third dimension, I think,

01:23:18.750 --> 01:23:21.450
is really relevant here is impact

01:23:21.450 --> 01:23:23.994
and that's access to appropriate care

01:23:23.994 --> 01:23:26.580
at the right time when people need it.

01:23:26.580 --> 01:23:29.970
And just swinging back to my colleague,

01:23:29.970 --> 01:23:34.970
Amanda's point, is we are
entering a new era, I think.

01:23:35.070 --> 01:23:37.020
This is about recognising

01:23:37.020 --> 01:23:40.682
and developing the
clinical pharmacy services

01:23:40.682 --> 01:23:42.720
within community pharmacy,

01:23:42.720 --> 01:23:45.600
and it's a start on this journey, I think.

01:23:45.600 --> 01:23:47.600
This is a really impressive moment.

01:23:47.600 --> 01:23:51.000
I would like to say
thank you to everybody.

01:23:51.000 --> 01:23:55.410
It's been a team of teams
effort across NHS England

01:23:55.410 --> 01:23:57.720
with our colleagues in DHSC,

01:23:57.720 --> 01:23:59.880
and importantly, the
frontline professionals

01:23:59.880 --> 01:24:03.960
who are now delivering
these services to patients.

01:24:03.960 --> 01:24:07.530
Initial feedback is that
ability to close an episode

01:24:07.530 --> 01:24:11.010
of care appropriately,
to have somebody present,

01:24:11.010 --> 01:24:12.450
be able to recognise what's wrong,

01:24:12.450 --> 01:24:15.693
and then provide the treatment
that the person needs then,

01:24:15.693 --> 01:24:17.640
that's already coming through

01:24:17.640 --> 01:24:21.900
as a positive professional
aspect of the service too.

01:24:21.900 --> 01:24:25.080
So, I think in the
interest of time, chair,

01:24:25.080 --> 01:24:27.891
if I sort of hold it there, is that okay?

01:24:27.891 --> 01:24:28.724
And I take some question.
- That's fine.

01:24:28.724 --> 01:24:29.940
Let me just able it up to comment.

01:24:29.940 --> 01:24:32.820
So, Steve first. Steve, yeah.

01:24:32.820 --> 01:24:33.900
- Just an additional point.

01:24:33.900 --> 01:24:36.420
So, everybody around the board table

01:24:36.420 --> 01:24:40.260
understands the importance
of antimicrobial resistance

01:24:40.260 --> 01:24:42.690
and ensuring that we guard

01:24:42.690 --> 01:24:46.860
against increased resistance
of antimicrobials.

01:24:46.860 --> 01:24:49.616
And clearly in extending the prescription

01:24:49.616 --> 01:24:52.590
of antibiotics into an additional setting,

01:24:52.590 --> 01:24:54.930
in this case pharmacies,
is really important

01:24:54.930 --> 01:24:57.360
that we bear that in mind
and manage that risk.

01:24:57.360 --> 01:24:58.620
So, I just want to assure the board,

01:24:58.620 --> 01:24:59.730
it's in paragraph 9

01:24:59.730 --> 01:25:02.163
and paragraph 18,
- Yeah.

01:25:02.163 --> 01:25:04.530
- that we did engage clinical expert,

01:25:04.530 --> 01:25:07.023
clinical reference groups
in designing these pathways

01:25:07.023 --> 01:25:09.810
to ensure that any risk

01:25:09.810 --> 01:25:14.400
of increasing antimicrobial
resistance was addressed

01:25:14.400 --> 01:25:15.720
and the pathways are optimised

01:25:15.720 --> 01:25:17.790
for the appropriate use of antibiotics.

01:25:17.790 --> 01:25:21.420
That has been scrutinised by
our AMR programme board here,

01:25:21.420 --> 01:25:22.440
including by myself,

01:25:22.440 --> 01:25:24.300
with input from the chief medical officer.

01:25:24.300 --> 01:25:27.810
But it's really important
that we evaluate this service.

01:25:27.810 --> 01:25:31.287
And I draw your attention to paragraph 18.

01:25:31.287 --> 01:25:33.900
And I think one of the important things

01:25:33.900 --> 01:25:35.520
that we're doing is engaging

01:25:35.520 --> 01:25:37.819
the National Institute of
Health and Care Research

01:25:37.819 --> 01:25:40.323
in an evaluation programme of the service.

01:25:41.342 --> 01:25:42.175
- Thank you.

01:25:43.815 --> 01:25:45.150
- Yes.
- Mary? Yes, sorry.

01:25:45.150 --> 01:25:45.983
- Thank you.

01:25:45.983 --> 01:25:48.990
I just wanted to ask
whether you anticipated

01:25:48.990 --> 01:25:53.990
it reducing calls to 111 and
also whether in some areas,

01:25:54.125 --> 01:25:57.150
because pharmacies are open later,

01:25:57.150 --> 01:25:59.070
that it would make it
much easier for people

01:25:59.070 --> 01:26:01.773
who work to get some things
resolved very quickly.

01:26:02.850 --> 01:26:04.590
- Hold these, so it's the same system.

01:26:04.590 --> 01:26:07.078
Hold the questions,
and then we'll back to-

01:26:07.078 --> 01:26:08.553
- Extending Mary's point.

01:26:08.553 --> 01:26:09.420
From perspective

01:26:09.420 --> 01:26:13.830
of how are we going to measure
success of the initiative?

01:26:13.830 --> 01:26:16.590
Because on one dimension,
yes, of course it's access

01:26:16.590 --> 01:26:19.350
for patients out of hours and whatever,

01:26:19.350 --> 01:26:20.940
but does it reduce incidence

01:26:20.940 --> 01:26:23.280
of people turning up A&amp;E for...

01:26:23.280 --> 01:26:25.110
I didn't see much of that in here

01:26:25.110 --> 01:26:26.160
and you've got it I'm sure,

01:26:26.160 --> 01:26:29.610
but just like a little bit
more on that to be helpful.

01:26:29.610 --> 01:26:32.090
- And then, my only question is,

01:26:32.090 --> 01:26:34.290
reading the IT system connectivity

01:26:34.290 --> 01:26:36.870
and clearly there's been
a huge amount of work,

01:26:36.870 --> 01:26:38.580
I couldn't quite work out from the paper

01:26:38.580 --> 01:26:42.420
to what extent we've completed
a seamless interaction

01:26:42.420 --> 01:26:44.580
between the pharmacy sector

01:26:44.580 --> 01:26:47.805
and the patient's record in primary care.

01:26:47.805 --> 01:26:50.340
And it'd be quite helpful
just to get us some,

01:26:50.340 --> 01:26:52.440
an assurance and understanding
of where that work is

01:26:52.440 --> 01:26:54.930
and how much more needs to be done.

01:26:54.930 --> 01:26:57.090
So, do you want to have a go
at those, David and Amanda?

01:26:57.090 --> 01:27:02.090
- So, the 111 and A&amp;E,
which are both related.

01:27:02.291 --> 01:27:04.350
When we initially did this work,

01:27:04.350 --> 01:27:07.350
this was about freeing up capacity

01:27:07.350 --> 01:27:10.470
or taking some of the
pressure off general practise.

01:27:10.470 --> 01:27:11.910
These are largely services

01:27:11.910 --> 01:27:14.310
currently provided in general practise.

01:27:14.310 --> 01:27:18.990
But of course 111 tends
to be the front door

01:27:18.990 --> 01:27:22.350
to out of hours primary care,
urgent primary care services.

01:27:22.350 --> 01:27:23.790
And so, there will be an impact,

01:27:23.790 --> 01:27:26.640
because pharmacies tend
to be open at weekends

01:27:26.640 --> 01:27:29.440
and sometimes later in the
evenings in general practise.

01:27:31.191 --> 01:27:36.180
Large numbers of 111 calls
often relate to medicine

01:27:36.180 --> 01:27:38.070
and particularly access
to urgent medicine.

01:27:38.070 --> 01:27:41.100
People who've not realised
they've run out of their inhalers

01:27:41.100 --> 01:27:42.960
or their repeat prescriptions.

01:27:42.960 --> 01:27:44.610
Part of the Pharmacy First service

01:27:44.610 --> 01:27:47.670
we've rolled in the urgent
repeat medication service.

01:27:47.670 --> 01:27:50.130
And so, that's the reason
why it's so important

01:27:50.130 --> 01:27:52.848
to do those proper underpinning updates,

01:27:52.848 --> 01:27:54.960
so that patients from 111

01:27:54.960 --> 01:27:58.650
can be immediately flowed
into those services.

01:27:58.650 --> 01:27:59.483
So,

01:28:01.650 --> 01:28:03.660
of course initially, it's really important

01:28:03.660 --> 01:28:05.580
that 111 can signpost the patients

01:28:05.580 --> 01:28:07.203
to the appropriate service.

01:28:08.220 --> 01:28:11.190
As people get used to the
availability of these services

01:28:11.190 --> 01:28:12.454
and where they can go,

01:28:12.454 --> 01:28:15.360
we would hope that
people will go directly.

01:28:15.360 --> 01:28:19.110
We have got planned a big
public communications campaign,

01:28:19.110 --> 01:28:21.861
which is due to launch second week

01:28:21.861 --> 01:28:25.650
of February to talk to people about this.

01:28:25.650 --> 01:28:26.520
- Well, I'm going to hold you.

01:28:26.520 --> 01:28:27.750
There's one more question coming in,

01:28:27.750 --> 01:28:30.750
then I'm going to give you
about, with huge apologies,

01:28:30.750 --> 01:28:32.970
about three minutes to deal with them all.

01:28:32.970 --> 01:28:34.080
But, Mark, you want to...

01:28:34.080 --> 01:28:35.580
- This question's probably
directed at Steve,

01:28:35.580 --> 01:28:36.960
which is good clinical practise

01:28:36.960 --> 01:28:39.333
quite often requires
culture for infections.

01:28:40.349 --> 01:28:42.513
That will be part of the pathways?

01:28:44.910 --> 01:28:49.230
I'm saying good clinical
practise often requires culture

01:28:49.230 --> 01:28:50.063
- Yes.
- before you prescribe

01:28:50.063 --> 01:28:50.896
antibiotics.

01:28:50.896 --> 01:28:52.020
That's part of the pathway, is it?

01:28:52.020 --> 01:28:54.150
- Well, part of the
pathways is to ensure that,

01:28:54.150 --> 01:28:56.667
well, firstly, there are a
set of standardised pathways

01:28:56.667 --> 01:28:57.540
- Yeah.
- for people to follow,

01:28:57.540 --> 01:28:59.770
but it is also the appropriate training

01:29:01.050 --> 01:29:03.870
to ensure that those pathways are followed

01:29:03.870 --> 01:29:05.209
and any diagnostics

01:29:05.209 --> 01:29:07.710
as part of those pathways are understood.

01:29:07.710 --> 01:29:09.150
And I think all of that then

01:29:09.150 --> 01:29:12.434
is part of the cultural response as well.

01:29:12.434 --> 01:29:16.380
- Okay. Good.
- So, I think

01:29:16.380 --> 01:29:17.460
a couple more questions

01:29:17.460 --> 01:29:19.950
you've still got to pick up.
- Mm-hmm.

01:29:19.950 --> 01:29:21.450
- You want to just finish those for me?

01:29:21.450 --> 01:29:23.160
- So, the A&amp;E question

01:29:23.160 --> 01:29:25.470
really sits with the
111 question, I think.

01:29:25.470 --> 01:29:26.310
- Yeah.
- And that's about demand,

01:29:26.310 --> 01:29:27.710
another parts of the system.

01:29:28.740 --> 01:29:33.740
What's really important about
the IT infrastructure aspects

01:29:33.870 --> 01:29:36.180
of this service is this
is the first setting

01:29:36.180 --> 01:29:40.110
whereby the post-event, the
post-consultation information

01:29:40.110 --> 01:29:42.720
will flow directly into the workflow

01:29:42.720 --> 01:29:44.680
in the GP clinical system

01:29:45.840 --> 01:29:48.540
rather than coming as a letter

01:29:48.540 --> 01:29:50.880
that comes through the NHS mail,

01:29:50.880 --> 01:29:52.830
and then has to be dealt with manually.

01:29:52.830 --> 01:29:54.933
So, this is actually a huge step forward

01:29:54.933 --> 01:29:58.980
and that will roll out completely,

01:29:58.980 --> 01:30:02.133
and correct me if I'm wrong,
over about the next two weeks.

01:30:03.060 --> 01:30:04.620
It's happening in some places already,

01:30:04.620 --> 01:30:07.320
depending on which different
systems are in place,

01:30:07.320 --> 01:30:11.850
but will happen for all
pharmacy and GP IT systems.

01:30:11.850 --> 01:30:14.670
And that's really been the big part

01:30:14.670 --> 01:30:17.070
of the work we've asked
system suppliers to do

01:30:17.070 --> 01:30:19.080
to which they've responded really well.

01:30:19.080 --> 01:30:20.454
- So, thank you. That's-

01:30:20.454 --> 01:30:21.480
- [Steve] I think I might have answered

01:30:21.480 --> 01:30:22.320
a different questions.

01:30:22.320 --> 01:30:24.210
The one that Mark asked.
- I think you did. Yes.

01:30:24.210 --> 01:30:25.440
- [Steve] Which was around the culture

01:30:25.440 --> 01:30:26.970
of asking for antibiotics.

01:30:26.970 --> 01:30:28.500
- [Mark] Yes, it's culturing the microbe,

01:30:28.500 --> 01:30:30.210
not the-
- Oh, culturing the microbe.

01:30:30.210 --> 01:30:31.256
Yeah.
- Oh goodness.

01:30:34.320 --> 01:30:35.160
- [Steve] Even different question.

01:30:35.160 --> 01:30:38.843
It's a culturing the microbe.

01:30:38.843 --> 01:30:41.750
So, part, well, I did partly answer it,

01:30:41.750 --> 01:30:44.760
but the expert reference groups

01:30:44.760 --> 01:30:47.070
look very carefully at the diagnostics

01:30:47.070 --> 01:30:48.930
that would be required.

01:30:48.930 --> 01:30:50.400
So, culture is not part of that,

01:30:50.400 --> 01:30:52.020
but there are other checks and balances.

01:30:52.020 --> 01:30:53.010
- So, can I...

01:30:53.010 --> 01:30:56.020
I'm going to pause that debate
between the cognoscente.

01:30:57.535 --> 01:30:59.357
If I might and just say
thank you very much.

01:30:59.357 --> 01:31:03.664
It is, I utterly agree, a
really brilliant initiative.

01:31:03.664 --> 01:31:06.240
And partly, we haven't said it,

01:31:06.240 --> 01:31:10.770
because it allows us to
engage with a highly skilled

01:31:10.770 --> 01:31:11.790
workforce,
- Yeah.

01:31:11.790 --> 01:31:13.890
- a highly skilled pharmacists.

01:31:13.890 --> 01:31:15.480
And their then ability to come

01:31:15.480 --> 01:31:16.650
and contribute to the provision

01:31:16.650 --> 01:31:19.080
of health care into primary care.

01:31:19.080 --> 01:31:19.913
I think it's brilliant.

01:31:19.913 --> 01:31:21.570
It will give 10, I think,

01:31:21.570 --> 01:31:23.960
estimated 10 million more
potential appointments

01:31:23.960 --> 01:31:28.680
or it takes pressure off
primary care GP's practises

01:31:28.680 --> 01:31:30.660
for about 10 million appointments or so.

01:31:30.660 --> 01:31:32.310
But actually, it's also for patients,

01:31:32.310 --> 01:31:34.872
the ease of access, the breadth of access.

01:31:34.872 --> 01:31:36.150
- Exactly.
- Everyone's within the range

01:31:36.150 --> 01:31:38.850
of a community pharmacy.

01:31:38.850 --> 01:31:40.825
So, congratulations on it.

01:31:40.825 --> 01:31:41.880
I think it's a really brilliant initiative

01:31:41.880 --> 01:31:42.713
and thank you very much.

01:31:42.713 --> 01:31:44.340
- We often talk about
culture around this state,

01:31:44.340 --> 01:31:45.240
but I think that's the first time

01:31:45.240 --> 01:31:47.255
I've been asked about culture of microbes.

01:31:49.080 --> 01:31:51.990
- Yeah, be careful what you
wish for is all I can say.

01:31:51.990 --> 01:31:53.073
So, let's move this-

01:31:54.210 --> 01:31:55.320
- Yeah.
- Mind shift.

01:31:55.320 --> 01:31:56.257
- Yeah.
- So-

01:31:56.257 --> 01:31:59.400
- Genomics.
- So, Amanda and David,

01:31:59.400 --> 01:32:00.233
thank you.

01:32:00.233 --> 01:32:02.130
Let's go on to a really
important topic, actually.

01:32:02.130 --> 01:32:06.120
Claire Murdoch and Liz Durrant,
so it's the mental health

01:32:06.120 --> 01:32:07.593
- Yeah.
- paper.

01:32:08.430 --> 01:32:10.290
- Thank you very much.
- Do you want to introduce it

01:32:10.290 --> 01:32:11.123
relatively briefly?

01:32:11.123 --> 01:32:12.289
I'm sure there are going to be comments

01:32:12.289 --> 01:32:13.147
and questions on this.

01:32:13.147 --> 01:32:14.625
It's a very important topic.

01:32:14.625 --> 01:32:16.884
- Thank you, chair.

01:32:16.884 --> 01:32:19.530
Just to pick up on something
that our chief executive

01:32:19.530 --> 01:32:22.590
and yourself said earlier,
we must all acknowledge

01:32:22.590 --> 01:32:23.790
that mental health services

01:32:23.790 --> 01:32:25.740
are operating under extreme pressure.

01:32:25.740 --> 01:32:27.240
We're seeing 1.2 million

01:32:27.240 --> 01:32:31.340
more people than we saw back in 2016.

01:32:31.340 --> 01:32:34.290
Our inpatient services
are running red hot,

01:32:34.290 --> 01:32:37.680
our urgent emergency
care pathways similarly,

01:32:37.680 --> 01:32:39.930
whether that's discharge at the back end

01:32:39.930 --> 01:32:41.220
or what happens in A&amp;E

01:32:41.220 --> 01:32:44.977
or the community or
out of area placements.

01:32:44.977 --> 01:32:46.800
So, I think it's really important

01:32:46.800 --> 01:32:48.663
that we do all acknowledge that.

01:32:49.980 --> 01:32:52.620
And often, during periods of crisis,

01:32:52.620 --> 01:32:54.030
we know it's when patients

01:32:54.030 --> 01:32:56.190
are at their very most vulnerable

01:32:56.190 --> 01:32:58.600
and they need a timely response

01:32:58.600 --> 01:33:01.200
in the most appropriate setting.

01:33:01.200 --> 01:33:02.547
And we need both the workforce

01:33:02.547 --> 01:33:05.520
and the clinical models
to ensure we deliver that.

01:33:05.520 --> 01:33:07.165
When they come into hospital,

01:33:07.165 --> 01:33:10.140
it's absolutely vital
that the culture of care,

01:33:10.140 --> 01:33:11.820
the effectiveness of care,

01:33:11.820 --> 01:33:14.086
and the outcomes are the right ones.

01:33:14.086 --> 01:33:17.850
So, our workforce have been leaning into,

01:33:17.850 --> 01:33:19.290
as you said earlier,

01:33:19.290 --> 01:33:22.800
they've been leaning into
the mental health challenge.

01:33:22.800 --> 01:33:27.210
And I think overwhelmingly
come to work to do a good job.

01:33:27.210 --> 01:33:28.950
But it would be true to say

01:33:28.950 --> 01:33:31.080
that we don't always have the right skills

01:33:31.080 --> 01:33:33.060
in the right place at the right time.

01:33:33.060 --> 01:33:34.650
And part of what this programme,

01:33:34.650 --> 01:33:37.410
which Liz is going to take
us through in a moment,

01:33:37.410 --> 01:33:42.410
will focus on, is people working
in both the inpatient area

01:33:42.600 --> 01:33:44.406
and the alternative services,

01:33:44.406 --> 01:33:47.400
really having a good
fulfilling and rewarding jobs

01:33:47.400 --> 01:33:49.380
and the skill sets to conduct the care

01:33:49.380 --> 01:33:52.873
that they give to a high standard.

01:33:52.873 --> 01:33:55.830
As part of formulating this programme,

01:33:55.830 --> 01:33:57.990
we've had huge engagement

01:33:57.990 --> 01:34:02.040
with people with lived
experience, ICB chiefs,

01:34:02.040 --> 01:34:06.990
their teams, trust, third
sector partners, and others.

01:34:06.990 --> 01:34:10.830
And I'm pleased to say that
there is overwhelming appetite

01:34:10.830 --> 01:34:13.680
for improving the quality of care

01:34:13.680 --> 01:34:15.600
that we give and also changing it.

01:34:15.600 --> 01:34:17.700
I think your point, Amanda, earlier,

01:34:17.700 --> 01:34:19.920
about it's not just about more care,

01:34:19.920 --> 01:34:21.720
it's about the right care,

01:34:21.720 --> 01:34:24.184
and this program's all about that.

01:34:24.184 --> 01:34:26.580
Such is the level of need

01:34:26.580 --> 01:34:28.620
and the level of appetite
that I'm really pleased

01:34:28.620 --> 01:34:29.580
to announce today

01:34:29.580 --> 01:34:34.418
that we are investing 75
million pounds a year currently

01:34:34.418 --> 01:34:36.330
to support this programme.

01:34:36.330 --> 01:34:41.330
We are asking ICBs to
create three-year plans

01:34:41.684 --> 01:34:44.160
to both localise care

01:34:44.160 --> 01:34:47.777
and improve the quality
of care that we provide,

01:34:47.777 --> 01:34:49.950
and change the model of care.

01:34:49.950 --> 01:34:51.540
But this is recurrent funding,

01:34:51.540 --> 01:34:54.750
obviously subject to negotiations.

01:34:54.750 --> 01:34:57.300
But we are delighted to announce this

01:34:57.300 --> 01:34:59.640
to support this really
important programme.

01:34:59.640 --> 01:35:02.340
And my colleague, Liz, will
tell you just a little bit more

01:35:02.340 --> 01:35:07.340
about, so what's the content
of this, of this programme?

01:35:07.346 --> 01:35:08.494
- Thank you, Claire.
Thank you, colleagues.

01:35:08.494 --> 01:35:11.510
Claire and colleagues
attended just over a year ago

01:35:11.510 --> 01:35:14.700
in the immediate aftermath
of the horrendous events

01:35:14.700 --> 01:35:15.540
that took place

01:35:15.540 --> 01:35:18.450
in the Edenfield Centre
in Greater Manchester.

01:35:18.450 --> 01:35:21.720
And the board asked us to
focus in this programme

01:35:21.720 --> 01:35:24.174
on how we can change the model of care

01:35:24.174 --> 01:35:26.750
within mental health inpatient services,

01:35:26.750 --> 01:35:31.547
how we can focus on culture,
not in the microbe sense,

01:35:31.547 --> 01:35:33.840
but in the changing culture

01:35:33.840 --> 01:35:35.370
and practise sense,

01:35:35.370 --> 01:35:36.780
and to keep patients and families

01:35:36.780 --> 01:35:39.420
at the heart of everything that we do.

01:35:39.420 --> 01:35:43.110
Yesterday, we, NHS England,
published the independent review

01:35:43.110 --> 01:35:45.390
of the events that took
place in Manchester,

01:35:45.390 --> 01:35:47.960
and Professor Oliver
Shanley who led the review

01:35:47.960 --> 01:35:51.960
concluded that at the heart
of the problems in Manchester,

01:35:51.960 --> 01:35:54.660
a lack of value placed on patients

01:35:54.660 --> 01:35:57.873
and their families was critical
to all that went wrong.

01:35:59.820 --> 01:36:04.560
Our programme is based on
the fundamental risk factors

01:36:04.560 --> 01:36:08.520
of closed 24/7 psychiatric institutions,

01:36:08.520 --> 01:36:11.130
as well as all the great
engagement that we've had.

01:36:11.130 --> 01:36:13.620
And there are three
particular areas of focus

01:36:13.620 --> 01:36:16.213
that I wanted to draw the
board's attention to today.

01:36:16.213 --> 01:36:19.530
The first is that we've asked
every ICB across the country

01:36:19.530 --> 01:36:21.723
to reconsider the inpatient services

01:36:21.723 --> 01:36:24.150
that they commissioned
for their population.

01:36:24.150 --> 01:36:25.920
We've asked them to first of all,

01:36:25.920 --> 01:36:27.690
know where all their people are,

01:36:27.690 --> 01:36:29.580
as many people are placed very far away

01:36:29.580 --> 01:36:31.320
from those who know and love them.

01:36:31.320 --> 01:36:33.330
We've asked them to find
out what it's going to take

01:36:33.330 --> 01:36:35.813
to bring those people home and to do so.

01:36:35.813 --> 01:36:38.850
And we've asked them to
think quite differently

01:36:38.850 --> 01:36:40.680
about the models of care
that they're buying,

01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:43.530
so that they are in line
with both the evidence base

01:36:43.530 --> 01:36:45.990
and what people and families tell us.

01:36:45.990 --> 01:36:47.280
Secondly, we've launched

01:36:47.280 --> 01:36:50.340
a culture change programme this month,

01:36:50.340 --> 01:36:53.010
which is available to every NHS trust

01:36:53.010 --> 01:36:54.870
and provider of NHS-commissioned

01:36:54.870 --> 01:36:56.700
mental health inpatient services,

01:36:56.700 --> 01:36:58.650
which really focuses ward to board

01:36:58.650 --> 01:37:00.750
on how do we achieve a culture of care

01:37:00.750 --> 01:37:02.430
for all of our people

01:37:02.430 --> 01:37:05.370
that's our patients, their
families, and our staff.

01:37:05.370 --> 01:37:06.870
And finally, we're in the process

01:37:06.870 --> 01:37:08.580
of developing early warning signs

01:37:08.580 --> 01:37:11.970
as we know that the way in
which we measure quality of care

01:37:11.970 --> 01:37:14.214
isn't working for us in
the mental health sector.

01:37:14.214 --> 01:37:17.010
And it means that we are
not intervening early

01:37:17.010 --> 01:37:19.047
when we know that things
aren't going well.

01:37:19.047 --> 01:37:21.690
And we plan to test
those suite of measures

01:37:21.690 --> 01:37:23.760
against events that took
place in Manchester,

01:37:23.760 --> 01:37:25.620
so that we can tell whether or not

01:37:25.620 --> 01:37:27.030
if we'd have had those measures in place,

01:37:27.030 --> 01:37:29.160
we would've been able
to intervene earlier.

01:37:29.160 --> 01:37:30.127
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:37:30.127 --> 01:37:30.960
- So, I'm going to open it up

01:37:30.960 --> 01:37:31.793
for any comments and questions.

01:37:31.793 --> 01:37:33.523
There is a particular paragraph

01:37:33.523 --> 01:37:36.960
which we have sort of
delved into earlier on today

01:37:36.960 --> 01:37:40.219
actually in a really

01:37:40.219 --> 01:37:43.398
quite deep conversation on
the challenges in this space,

01:37:43.398 --> 01:37:44.267
and it's in this one paragraph, 25,

01:37:44.267 --> 01:37:46.833
around the two rate limiting factors.

01:37:48.210 --> 01:37:49.773
And they are here,

01:37:50.700 --> 01:37:53.522
workforce, growth,
retention, and recruitment.

01:37:53.522 --> 01:37:54.355
And then, actually,

01:37:54.355 --> 01:37:56.880
the second is addressing
the quality issues

01:37:56.880 --> 01:37:58.470
that exist with the physical estate.

01:37:58.470 --> 01:38:00.060
Do you have comments on...

01:38:00.060 --> 01:38:05.060
How would you reflect on
those two rate limiting areas?

01:38:05.700 --> 01:38:06.747
- Yes, thank you.

01:38:06.747 --> 01:38:08.010
Liz will add there,

01:38:08.010 --> 01:38:12.207
but workforce are central
to our ability as the NHS

01:38:12.207 --> 01:38:14.340
and it's no different in
mental health services

01:38:14.340 --> 01:38:16.530
to deliver overwhelmingly what we do.

01:38:16.530 --> 01:38:17.760
If we look at the patients

01:38:17.760 --> 01:38:20.070
coming into hospital in particular,

01:38:20.070 --> 01:38:23.460
they're often our most
poorly, our most vulnerable,

01:38:23.460 --> 01:38:24.810
and our most ill.

01:38:24.810 --> 01:38:26.340
And it's absolutely critical

01:38:26.340 --> 01:38:29.370
that we really take this opportunity

01:38:29.370 --> 01:38:32.700
to revisit not only the grade skills

01:38:32.700 --> 01:38:34.620
and experience of the workforce

01:38:34.620 --> 01:38:37.530
that we have in the inpatient area,

01:38:37.530 --> 01:38:40.350
but also the mix of staff that we have.

01:38:40.350 --> 01:38:42.570
So, doctors and nurses are fundamental

01:38:42.570 --> 01:38:44.250
and we are paying particular attention

01:38:44.250 --> 01:38:47.850
to mental health nursing
within the inpatient area,

01:38:47.850 --> 01:38:48.780
because we do worry

01:38:48.780 --> 01:38:50.670
about the vacancy levels.
- Yeah.

01:38:50.670 --> 01:38:52.800
- But it's also about other therapists,

01:38:52.800 --> 01:38:55.620
occupational therapists,
peer support workers,

01:38:55.620 --> 01:38:58.470
families being more
welcome into this space.

01:38:58.470 --> 01:39:00.393
So, it's absolutely vital.

01:39:01.830 --> 01:39:04.380
We've doubled the nurse training pipeline

01:39:04.380 --> 01:39:05.940
from about two and a half thousand

01:39:05.940 --> 01:39:08.250
to five and a half thousand a year now.

01:39:08.250 --> 01:39:10.320
So, more than doubled.

01:39:10.320 --> 01:39:11.820
And that that's a really

01:39:11.820 --> 01:39:13.440
important step,
- Yeah.

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:17.700
- but it still won't be enough
to meet demand and need now.

01:39:17.700 --> 01:39:19.162
So, the retention piece

01:39:19.162 --> 01:39:22.230
and making this work rewarding work,

01:39:22.230 --> 01:39:25.295
work that they can feel,
staff can feel proud of,

01:39:25.295 --> 01:39:28.657
and creating safe and therapeutic spaces

01:39:28.657 --> 01:39:30.811
actually will be as good for our staff,

01:39:30.811 --> 01:39:33.960
better for working in, as
it will for our patients.

01:39:33.960 --> 01:39:35.310
So, workforce is key

01:39:35.310 --> 01:39:36.660
and we do need

01:39:36.660 --> 01:39:38.400
to take the opportunity to review it.

01:39:38.400 --> 01:39:39.897
In terms of the physical estate, look,

01:39:39.897 --> 01:39:41.850
there's some good things we're doing.

01:39:41.850 --> 01:39:43.762
We're eradicating dormitories

01:39:43.762 --> 01:39:45.450
and that seems to me something

01:39:45.450 --> 01:39:48.690
we should have done a long time
ago in mental health units.

01:39:48.690 --> 01:39:50.520
But we are far down that journey now.

01:39:50.520 --> 01:39:53.700
However, even when we've done that,

01:39:53.700 --> 01:39:57.240
we've still got some very
old, very dilapidated estate

01:39:57.240 --> 01:40:02.240
that's not conducive to
dignified care, therapeutic care,

01:40:02.430 --> 01:40:07.200
modern care, including
actually the integration

01:40:07.200 --> 01:40:10.776
of digital services and care.
- Yeah.

01:40:10.776 --> 01:40:13.500
- So, we absolutely do think

01:40:13.500 --> 01:40:17.310
that the quality of the
environment's fundamental.

01:40:17.310 --> 01:40:18.600
- I'm not going to be losing this,

01:40:18.600 --> 01:40:20.993
but I know, Ruth, you
want to make a comment,

01:40:20.993 --> 01:40:22.505
ask question.
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:40:22.505 --> 01:40:23.580
- And then, so we'll take Ruth,

01:40:23.580 --> 01:40:26.220
and then we'll take David and then Amanda.

01:40:26.220 --> 01:40:27.390
And then, if you can deal with those,

01:40:27.390 --> 01:40:29.190
that batch of three, that would be great.

01:40:29.190 --> 01:40:30.023
Ruth?

01:40:30.023 --> 01:40:31.380
- So, Claire's absolutely right.

01:40:31.380 --> 01:40:33.900
Mental health nursing has been,

01:40:33.900 --> 01:40:36.810
is much more of a challenge
in terms of the vacancies

01:40:36.810 --> 01:40:41.454
and the numbers of stuff
we've got increasing.

01:40:41.454 --> 01:40:44.730
We have two and a half thousand
more mental health nurses

01:40:44.730 --> 01:40:47.580
this now compared to this time last year.

01:40:47.580 --> 01:40:50.280
We'll have over 4,000 mental health nurses

01:40:50.280 --> 01:40:54.480
graduate each year now compared to 2,300.

01:40:54.480 --> 01:40:56.940
So, there is hope for optimism,

01:40:56.940 --> 01:41:00.930
but it's the inpatient
areas that struggle most,

01:41:00.930 --> 01:41:02.525
because the challenge is greater.

01:41:02.525 --> 01:41:05.400
So, there is much more we need to be doing

01:41:05.400 --> 01:41:07.890
around that working environment,

01:41:07.890 --> 01:41:09.240
so that we can support

01:41:09.240 --> 01:41:12.000
our mental health nurses,
so they want to stay.

01:41:12.000 --> 01:41:13.560
So, it's the retention

01:41:13.560 --> 01:41:16.290
is really going to be important
as part of this programme,

01:41:16.290 --> 01:41:17.230
isn't it, with mental health nursing.

01:41:17.230 --> 01:41:19.880
- [Richard] Okay, so hold
that. David, do you want...

01:41:21.390 --> 01:41:25.710
- Yeah, I remember when
this came 12 months ago,

01:41:25.710 --> 01:41:29.430
Liz and Claire, and we'd
had the panorama programme

01:41:29.430 --> 01:41:31.050
and I think there was a sense of,

01:41:31.050 --> 01:41:33.030
it just quite took your
breath away really,

01:41:33.030 --> 01:41:34.800
because it's not the first time

01:41:34.800 --> 01:41:38.583
we'd seen poor care in closed institutions

01:41:38.583 --> 01:41:41.790
or indeed in some open institutions.

01:41:41.790 --> 01:41:43.560
And I remember the conversation we had

01:41:43.560 --> 01:41:47.340
about how do we avoid a plan
which is just making a sequence

01:41:47.340 --> 01:41:49.620
of technical recommendations and changes

01:41:49.620 --> 01:41:52.590
and actually begins to
address the culture?

01:41:52.590 --> 01:41:53.890
And I just want to

01:41:58.980 --> 01:42:03.980
recognise and value the fact
that in paragraph 15 and 16,

01:42:04.170 --> 01:42:06.510
where you've set out the
action you're taking,

01:42:06.510 --> 01:42:07.710
- Yeah.
- there's a real

01:42:07.710 --> 01:42:10.650
and significant attempt
to address the culture.

01:42:10.650 --> 01:42:13.770
My own view, and I don't
want to go back over this,

01:42:13.770 --> 01:42:18.180
but I thought the failure
at Edenfield was a failure

01:42:18.180 --> 01:42:22.320
of the governance of the board
and of clinical governance.

01:42:22.320 --> 01:42:24.900
And I think what you're
setting out in these paragraphs

01:42:24.900 --> 01:42:28.950
is a way of working to
develop both the capacity

01:42:28.950 --> 01:42:31.440
and capability of both clinical oversight,

01:42:31.440 --> 01:42:34.530
as well as the board oversight.

01:42:34.530 --> 01:42:36.330
And I just think that's really crucial

01:42:36.330 --> 01:42:40.050
to get that transformative,
but sustainable change.

01:42:40.050 --> 01:42:41.730
Can I just then make the connection

01:42:41.730 --> 01:42:45.570
to what we talked about on the discharge

01:42:45.570 --> 01:42:47.880
about how you do this is important,

01:42:47.880 --> 01:42:50.730
connecting why people
are going into these jobs

01:42:50.730 --> 01:42:53.700
and what they do with the job they do,

01:42:53.700 --> 01:42:54.747
I just think is really important

01:42:54.747 --> 01:42:56.460
and that was a lesson in leadership

01:42:56.460 --> 01:42:57.660
that I wanted to pull out, really,

01:42:57.660 --> 01:42:58.600
because I think

01:43:00.660 --> 01:43:03.570
it helps us understand how we do some of,

01:43:03.570 --> 01:43:06.120
tackle some of these intractable
problems that we've got.

01:43:06.120 --> 01:43:09.237
So, I just want to thank both of you

01:43:09.237 --> 01:43:12.060
for actually bringing forward a strategy

01:43:12.060 --> 01:43:13.800
which I think is beginning to address

01:43:13.800 --> 01:43:15.600
some of the underlying challenges

01:43:15.600 --> 01:43:18.177
that we've got in some of the services

01:43:18.177 --> 01:43:20.083
and systems that we've got.

01:43:20.083 --> 01:43:22.110
- Thank you.
- And actually, the change

01:43:22.110 --> 01:43:23.700
when we support them to change,

01:43:23.700 --> 01:43:26.070
not when we say this
isn't good enough change,

01:43:26.070 --> 01:43:27.120
and I think that's getting

01:43:27.120 --> 01:43:28.680
alongside people and helping them.

01:43:28.680 --> 01:43:29.730
So, well done.

01:43:29.730 --> 01:43:30.867
- [Richard] Very good. Thank you, David.

01:43:30.867 --> 01:43:31.700
So, Amanda?

01:43:31.700 --> 01:43:34.050
- I've just got a really
quick build actually

01:43:34.050 --> 01:43:35.367
on David's points.
- Okay.

01:43:35.367 --> 01:43:38.490
- So, I think I know
what you're going to say,

01:43:38.490 --> 01:43:40.860
but I just want to ask explicitly

01:43:40.860 --> 01:43:43.320
just in relation to the independent review

01:43:43.320 --> 01:43:46.320
of Greater Manchester Mental
Health NHS Foundation Trust,

01:43:46.320 --> 01:43:48.090
which was published yesterday,

01:43:48.090 --> 01:43:51.060
which obviously was the thing
we talked about a year ago

01:43:51.060 --> 01:43:52.590
that kicked off a lot this work.

01:43:52.590 --> 01:43:54.960
Now that that report by Professor Shanley

01:43:54.960 --> 01:43:58.410
has been produced, has
it changed anything?

01:43:58.410 --> 01:44:00.832
I know you've only had like
a day to read it properly,

01:44:00.832 --> 01:44:02.565
so it's probably really unfair,

01:44:02.565 --> 01:44:04.740
but I know you've been aware of the themes

01:44:04.740 --> 01:44:06.030
as they've been developing,

01:44:06.030 --> 01:44:09.998
but is there anything immediate
that you would say now

01:44:09.998 --> 01:44:13.586
in light of the review you want to change

01:44:13.586 --> 01:44:16.080
in the approach that's
been described today

01:44:16.080 --> 01:44:17.070
or anything further

01:44:17.070 --> 01:44:19.536
that you think you'd
want to explore further?

01:44:19.536 --> 01:44:22.590
Or are you content as far
as you can be in 24 hours

01:44:22.590 --> 01:44:24.450
that actually there is
a really good alignment

01:44:24.450 --> 01:44:27.540
between the findings and the programme?

01:44:27.540 --> 01:44:29.220
- So, I don't think...

01:44:29.220 --> 01:44:31.230
Ruth and David just made
really good comments,

01:44:31.230 --> 01:44:34.317
so there was no need
necessary to respond to those.

01:44:34.317 --> 01:44:37.290
Acknowledge. That's a specific question.

01:44:37.290 --> 01:44:38.850
Mary, I think you've got a question,

01:44:38.850 --> 01:44:40.470
and then I'm going to move us on after.

01:44:40.470 --> 01:44:42.720
- Thank you. I am slightly worried.

01:44:42.720 --> 01:44:45.253
I do understand the sociological term

01:44:45.253 --> 01:44:47.850
of psychiatric institutions,

01:44:47.850 --> 01:44:50.970
but we really need to think
about what terminology

01:44:50.970 --> 01:44:54.240
we want to use if we
want people to change.

01:44:54.240 --> 01:44:56.880
And as a mental health nurse myself,

01:44:56.880 --> 01:44:58.530
I feel that I've only ever worked

01:44:58.530 --> 01:45:02.100
in mental health facilities or hospitals.

01:45:02.100 --> 01:45:05.124
So, I just think we are
falling into the trap

01:45:05.124 --> 01:45:08.525
that they're different
and they shouldn't be.

01:45:08.525 --> 01:45:12.390
And I think Claire put it so well

01:45:12.390 --> 01:45:14.880
about access to family and visitors.

01:45:14.880 --> 01:45:17.490
- Mm.
- The closed nature

01:45:17.490 --> 01:45:21.090
of these places is because it's
too far for people to pop in

01:45:21.090 --> 01:45:23.100
and see what's going on.
- Yes.

01:45:23.100 --> 01:45:25.200
- And so, bringing care closer

01:45:25.200 --> 01:45:29.250
to people's homes is also
going to be fundamental.

01:45:29.250 --> 01:45:30.083
- Okay.

01:45:30.083 --> 01:45:33.060
So, you might want to react
to that comment from Mary,

01:45:33.060 --> 01:45:36.567
but also a very specific
question from Amanda.

01:45:36.567 --> 01:45:37.410
- Thank you.

01:45:37.410 --> 01:45:39.270
I'm going to get be
very brief and go first

01:45:39.270 --> 01:45:41.070
and would like to give Liz the final word,

01:45:41.070 --> 01:45:43.038
because she is driving this,
- Yeah, okay.

01:45:43.038 --> 01:45:43.871
- and it's a
- Yeah.

01:45:43.871 --> 01:45:46.890
- tremendous programme and
I'm very grateful to her.

01:45:46.890 --> 01:45:49.033
I think in terms of the Manchester report,

01:45:49.033 --> 01:45:52.823
two huge things we take away from that

01:45:52.823 --> 01:45:56.520
and Liz has said the voice of staff

01:45:56.520 --> 01:45:58.650
and above all, the voice of patients

01:45:58.650 --> 01:46:01.770
and their families were
not sufficiently heard

01:46:01.770 --> 01:46:04.534
and there wasn't a systematic way

01:46:04.534 --> 01:46:06.000
of the board

01:46:06.000 --> 01:46:07.530
and everybody in between,

01:46:07.530 --> 01:46:09.297
hearing the experience
of stuff on the board.

01:46:09.297 --> 01:46:11.370
And we need to create those systems

01:46:11.370 --> 01:46:14.310
that endure and is sustainable and real.

01:46:14.310 --> 01:46:15.780
And the second thing very much

01:46:15.780 --> 01:46:18.336
was the governance and oversight.

01:46:18.336 --> 01:46:22.482
And this programme will work
with boards on how do they know

01:46:22.482 --> 01:46:24.240
and what more can they do,

01:46:24.240 --> 01:46:26.580
and so we'll get alongside colleagues

01:46:26.580 --> 01:46:29.790
to help them really, really
have that good governance

01:46:29.790 --> 01:46:31.162
and oversight we hope.

01:46:31.162 --> 01:46:34.230
So, I'm going to say that
and just hand over to Liz

01:46:34.230 --> 01:46:35.390
who has worked so hard
- Okay.

01:46:35.390 --> 01:46:37.440
- on this, along with colleagues.

01:46:37.440 --> 01:46:38.651
- Thank you, Claire.

01:46:38.651 --> 01:46:41.220
I think it's probably made me reflect

01:46:41.220 --> 01:46:43.170
on some of the aspects of the programme

01:46:43.170 --> 01:46:44.940
we should go faster on perhaps.

01:46:44.940 --> 01:46:49.940
So, we've got a trust who are
currently trialling a freedom

01:46:50.250 --> 01:46:52.110
to speak up guardian for patients.

01:46:52.110 --> 01:46:54.210
So, it's somebody with lived experience

01:46:54.210 --> 01:46:55.830
who works in a similar role

01:46:55.830 --> 01:46:58.260
to a freedom to speak
up guardian for staff.

01:46:58.260 --> 01:46:59.554
And we've got another trust

01:46:59.554 --> 01:47:02.850
piloting incident reporting by patients

01:47:02.850 --> 01:47:03.930
and I think we might need

01:47:03.930 --> 01:47:06.203
to accelerate some of those learnings.

01:47:06.203 --> 01:47:09.030
And then, Mary, I think
we couldn't agree more

01:47:09.030 --> 01:47:13.214
with you about, we know the risk factors.

01:47:13.214 --> 01:47:16.290
I could write down now a
unit that is in the middle

01:47:16.290 --> 01:47:18.688
of nowhere where the family can't visit,

01:47:18.688 --> 01:47:22.230
that has got people from
all over the country in it.

01:47:22.230 --> 01:47:24.750
That's a risky unit and we
need to be acting in that way.

01:47:24.750 --> 01:47:26.520
We don't need to wait for that to happen,

01:47:26.520 --> 01:47:27.870
we just need to know that's risky

01:47:27.870 --> 01:47:30.481
and take action now to
reduce the use of them.

01:47:30.481 --> 01:47:32.456
And I think again, equally,

01:47:32.456 --> 01:47:35.670
when we are not having
frequent use of advocates

01:47:35.670 --> 01:47:37.920
and advocacy services,
that's another great sign.

01:47:37.920 --> 01:47:39.930
So, completely agree.

01:47:39.930 --> 01:47:42.540
- I'm going to close the topic.

01:47:42.540 --> 01:47:43.740
Thank you very much for the paper.

01:47:43.740 --> 01:47:45.210
Thank you for the engagement.

01:47:45.210 --> 01:47:47.430
It clearly is part of our service

01:47:47.430 --> 01:47:49.879
under very, very real stress

01:47:49.879 --> 01:47:54.879
and we're seeing huge levels
of demand growth coming at us.

01:47:55.470 --> 01:47:59.040
And clearly, there's much to be done.

01:47:59.040 --> 01:48:02.280
But thank you very much for the topic.

01:48:02.280 --> 01:48:04.230
Okay, can we move on then?

01:48:04.230 --> 01:48:07.560
So, we're going to move
on to the transformation

01:48:07.560 --> 01:48:08.850
and innovation delivery update.

01:48:08.850 --> 01:48:11.220
Vin, I think you are leading,
and I think I said earlier,

01:48:11.220 --> 01:48:14.400
I'm delighted that we've
got Joe Harrison who,

01:48:14.400 --> 01:48:16.110
as well as being Chief Executive.

01:48:16.110 --> 01:48:20.127
Milton Keynes is also
leading on the NHS app work

01:48:20.127 --> 01:48:22.440
and with Helen Clifton as well.

01:48:22.440 --> 01:48:23.670
Delighted to see you both here.

01:48:23.670 --> 01:48:24.720
But, Vin, do you want

01:48:24.720 --> 01:48:26.696
to give us the highlights of the paper?

01:48:26.696 --> 01:48:27.960
'Cause there's going to be
lots of commentary on it,

01:48:27.960 --> 01:48:29.010
I think, or questions.
- Thank you, chair.

01:48:29.010 --> 01:48:29.910
So, thank you.

01:48:29.910 --> 01:48:31.170
Well, digital and data have been

01:48:31.170 --> 01:48:32.100
the themes actually.

01:48:32.100 --> 01:48:35.203
You've seen through
every agenda item today.

01:48:35.203 --> 01:48:38.700
I just wanted to make three
brief points about the paper.

01:48:38.700 --> 01:48:39.780
So, the first thing I think

01:48:39.780 --> 01:48:42.780
is to say that where have we come from?

01:48:42.780 --> 01:48:46.410
So, the NHS still lags
behind our international,

01:48:46.410 --> 01:48:48.195
other international health systems

01:48:48.195 --> 01:48:50.190
in terms of our digital maturity.

01:48:50.190 --> 01:48:52.680
We don't have universal access

01:48:52.680 --> 01:48:54.716
to electronic patient records.

01:48:54.716 --> 01:48:57.418
We don't universally
have shared care records.

01:48:57.418 --> 01:48:59.250
Anyone who's been to hospital

01:48:59.250 --> 01:49:01.410
will know you can't just
automatically dial up

01:49:01.410 --> 01:49:03.600
into your computer and see an X-ray

01:49:03.600 --> 01:49:05.910
or a scan that's been seen somewhere else.

01:49:05.910 --> 01:49:07.920
And you just have to
talk to members of staff

01:49:07.920 --> 01:49:09.330
about the frustrations they feel

01:49:09.330 --> 01:49:10.875
with some of our technology

01:49:10.875 --> 01:49:13.740
that's available in trusts.

01:49:13.740 --> 01:49:15.840
That said, there has
been investment certainly

01:49:15.840 --> 01:49:17.160
over the last five years.

01:49:17.160 --> 01:49:18.547
And what the paper sets out

01:49:18.547 --> 01:49:21.090
is a very significant set of achievements

01:49:21.090 --> 01:49:23.553
that I think we, as an
NHS in England, have made.

01:49:24.390 --> 01:49:26.010
So, very briefly, I think first thing,

01:49:26.010 --> 01:49:29.220
the biggest achievements are 90% of trusts

01:49:29.220 --> 01:49:32.078
in England now have an
electronic patient record.

01:49:32.078 --> 01:49:34.380
We did that ahead of schedule,

01:49:34.380 --> 01:49:35.970
but we still have 10% that haven't.

01:49:35.970 --> 01:49:37.740
One of which is in Norfolk and Waveney,

01:49:37.740 --> 01:49:38.700
where they're doing great work

01:49:38.700 --> 01:49:40.764
in preparing the business case

01:49:40.764 --> 01:49:43.860
for theirs across three of their trusts.

01:49:43.860 --> 01:49:45.840
Primary care we've heard is on track now,

01:49:45.840 --> 01:49:47.610
not just to deliver Pharmacy First,

01:49:47.610 --> 01:49:50.000
which has been a real innovation actually

01:49:50.000 --> 01:49:51.180
in the ability to write

01:49:51.180 --> 01:49:52.464
into a GP record.

01:49:52.464 --> 01:49:55.615
But also modern ways now
of registering with your GP

01:49:55.615 --> 01:49:57.720
and ways in which you can interact

01:49:57.720 --> 01:50:00.992
with your GP for triage and
modern telephone systems.

01:50:00.992 --> 01:50:03.750
We have a modern federated data platform

01:50:03.750 --> 01:50:05.470
that this board has heard about before.

01:50:05.470 --> 01:50:07.770
The OPTICA system for discharge

01:50:07.770 --> 01:50:11.370
that Tracey mentioned is a
federated data platform product.

01:50:11.370 --> 01:50:14.430
So, we're seeing real benefits
from the data platform now.

01:50:14.430 --> 01:50:15.870
And in addition to the NHS app,

01:50:15.870 --> 01:50:18.273
which Joe and Helen will
talk about in a minute,

01:50:19.410 --> 01:50:21.990
we have also created 12

01:50:21.990 --> 01:50:24.330
sub-national secure data environments

01:50:24.330 --> 01:50:26.592
where fully anonymized patient data

01:50:26.592 --> 01:50:30.330
can be used for the purposes of research.

01:50:30.330 --> 01:50:31.530
And the NHS Spine.

01:50:31.530 --> 01:50:33.810
I think it's important to
remember that as a result

01:50:33.810 --> 01:50:35.840
of the merger, we are
now one of the providers

01:50:35.840 --> 01:50:39.810
of the largest digital and
data services in Europe.

01:50:39.810 --> 01:50:41.970
The NHS Spine handles a records

01:50:41.970 --> 01:50:44.910
of every single person
registered in the NHS

01:50:44.910 --> 01:50:47.340
and the records of people
who've previously been

01:50:47.340 --> 01:50:49.050
looked after in the NHS

01:50:49.050 --> 01:50:51.900
and has been migrated over
the last year to the cloud

01:50:51.900 --> 01:50:55.380
with no reduction in service levels

01:50:55.380 --> 01:50:59.981
and a more productive and
more efficient service.

01:50:59.981 --> 01:51:02.670
The third point I'd make
though is I'm pleased to say

01:51:02.670 --> 01:51:04.743
that we are in constructive discussions

01:51:04.743 --> 01:51:07.560
about how we do increase our investment

01:51:07.560 --> 01:51:10.230
in technology in forthcoming years,

01:51:10.230 --> 01:51:12.260
particularly to deliver both productivity,

01:51:12.260 --> 01:51:16.020
but also patient benefit,
better patient experience,

01:51:16.020 --> 01:51:17.287
safer clinical care,

01:51:17.287 --> 01:51:19.980
and all the things that you would expect,

01:51:19.980 --> 01:51:22.110
through the quadruple aim.

01:51:22.110 --> 01:51:23.220
And if we're going to do this,

01:51:23.220 --> 01:51:25.380
I think we are not going to just be able

01:51:25.380 --> 01:51:28.440
to achieve better outcomes for patient

01:51:28.440 --> 01:51:31.007
and a better experience
for patients and our staff,

01:51:31.007 --> 01:51:33.090
but also we'll be able to leverage

01:51:33.090 --> 01:51:35.430
the assets we've got in the UK,

01:51:35.430 --> 01:51:39.000
in life sciences, in
manufacturing, in data science,

01:51:39.000 --> 01:51:42.780
in computer science, in
AI, in machine learning,

01:51:42.780 --> 01:51:45.870
to enable us really to deliver benefits

01:51:45.870 --> 01:51:47.730
not just in the experience of patients,

01:51:47.730 --> 01:51:49.320
but in the form of new treatments

01:51:49.320 --> 01:51:50.730
and new insights into disease

01:51:50.730 --> 01:51:52.440
that will benefit patients in the future.

01:51:52.440 --> 01:51:54.720
And they will be truly
transformational, I think,

01:51:54.720 --> 01:51:56.850
for the delivery of
healthcare in the future.

01:51:56.850 --> 01:51:59.130
So, those are the three key
points I wanted to make, chair,

01:51:59.130 --> 01:52:00.570
and I'm going to hand
over to my colleagues

01:52:00.570 --> 01:52:01.920
who you've already introduced.

01:52:01.920 --> 01:52:03.990
They're the fantastic team
who've led all the work

01:52:03.990 --> 01:52:05.820
on the NHS app, which has been one

01:52:05.820 --> 01:52:07.560
of the most transformational
things we can do.

01:52:07.560 --> 01:52:09.117
So, Joe, over to you.

01:52:09.117 --> 01:52:10.617
- Thank you, Vin.

01:52:10.617 --> 01:52:12.450
And I'll start by saying that this board

01:52:12.450 --> 01:52:16.140
is presiding over what is, I believe,

01:52:16.140 --> 01:52:20.865
the biggest change in how the
NHS engages with its citizens

01:52:20.865 --> 01:52:25.470
that we have seen for many, many decades.

01:52:25.470 --> 01:52:28.020
We are in a place now whereby

01:52:28.020 --> 01:52:30.990
we have got more than three
quarters of our adults

01:52:30.990 --> 01:52:34.080
who have downloaded the app.

01:52:34.080 --> 01:52:37.080
We've got 97% of our practises

01:52:37.080 --> 01:52:39.183
able to communicate with those patients,

01:52:40.260 --> 01:52:44.700
and the numbers just keep
growing exponentially.

01:52:44.700 --> 01:52:48.180
And I just, there are lots of numbers

01:52:48.180 --> 01:52:49.013
that you've heard today,

01:52:49.013 --> 01:52:52.350
but over 4 million people a day

01:52:52.350 --> 01:52:55.473
access their medical records last week.

01:52:56.670 --> 01:52:59.370
This is an appetite that we are fulfilling

01:52:59.370 --> 01:53:01.860
through the NHS app that people

01:53:01.860 --> 01:53:03.750
didn't necessarily know existed,

01:53:03.750 --> 01:53:07.170
but is changing the way that
our citizens engage with us.

01:53:07.170 --> 01:53:09.390
And we've only just started.

01:53:09.390 --> 01:53:11.760
So, the last 12 months, we've presided,

01:53:11.760 --> 01:53:13.890
and Helen and the team have delivered

01:53:13.890 --> 01:53:16.530
against all of the
priorities that were set

01:53:16.530 --> 01:53:18.480
and have delivered early
on a couple of them.

01:53:18.480 --> 01:53:19.830
Now, that's not often you can say that

01:53:19.830 --> 01:53:22.581
about really big tech
programmes in the NHS.

01:53:22.581 --> 01:53:25.410
So, it is to celebrate

01:53:25.410 --> 01:53:27.870
and one should, as a member of this board,

01:53:27.870 --> 01:53:30.027
you should be celebrating this hugely.

01:53:30.027 --> 01:53:32.982
But it's so much more than that

01:53:32.982 --> 01:53:35.340
from a productivity perspective,

01:53:35.340 --> 01:53:39.210
from an engagement perspective,
and from the way that we can

01:53:39.210 --> 01:53:42.810
and will change the way that
we deliver care across our NHS.

01:53:42.810 --> 01:53:44.370
It is staggering.

01:53:44.370 --> 01:53:45.203
And let me just talk

01:53:45.203 --> 01:53:47.430
about a few of the
things that we're doing.

01:53:47.430 --> 01:53:50.280
I won't take up all the
remaining time, I promise, chair.

01:53:51.219 --> 01:53:53.270
We've started over the last 12 months

01:53:53.270 --> 01:53:56.460
of putting basic
functionality into the app,

01:53:56.460 --> 01:53:58.890
stuff that people want,

01:53:58.890 --> 01:54:01.680
to be able to look at their
appointments, their letters

01:54:01.680 --> 01:54:05.910
to engage at a time of
their choosing with our NHS.

01:54:05.910 --> 01:54:08.133
Our citizens absolutely love it.

01:54:09.150 --> 01:54:11.460
Have we got countrywide coverage yet?

01:54:11.460 --> 01:54:13.020
Not quite yet. No, we haven't.

01:54:13.020 --> 01:54:15.360
Is there more to do? Of course there is.

01:54:15.360 --> 01:54:18.637
It was brilliant hearing
about Pharmacy First and 111.

01:54:19.500 --> 01:54:22.027
It's even better sitting here knowing

01:54:22.027 --> 01:54:25.470
that we've already integrated
111 onto the front page

01:54:25.470 --> 01:54:28.500
of the app that has already
got integrated in it,

01:54:28.500 --> 01:54:31.053
the Pharmacy First pathway.

01:54:31.053 --> 01:54:32.790
And so, when we talk about have people

01:54:32.790 --> 01:54:36.219
picked up the phone to 111,
I sort of hope they don't,

01:54:36.219 --> 01:54:38.430
because we know

01:54:38.430 --> 01:54:41.040
that those that are using 111 on the app

01:54:41.040 --> 01:54:43.170
have got better completion rates

01:54:43.170 --> 01:54:46.200
than those that are
using it through the web.

01:54:46.200 --> 01:54:48.900
And we know that we can move more

01:54:48.900 --> 01:54:52.410
and more people to managing
their own care through the app.

01:54:52.410 --> 01:54:54.330
So, let's talk about a
couple of other things.

01:54:54.330 --> 01:54:55.707
Things that we're going to do.

01:54:55.707 --> 01:54:57.870
At the moment, the app's focused on people

01:54:57.870 --> 01:55:01.923
that need the NHS to
support their illness.

01:55:01.923 --> 01:55:05.061
We've got a full programme
coming up over the next 12 months

01:55:05.061 --> 01:55:09.420
to move the vaccination screening
programmes onto the app,

01:55:09.420 --> 01:55:10.860
because we know that patients

01:55:10.860 --> 01:55:12.450
who book their own appointments

01:55:12.450 --> 01:55:14.550
are more likely to turn up for them.

01:55:14.550 --> 01:55:15.390
And the more patients

01:55:15.390 --> 01:55:18.243
we can get screened,
vaccinated, the better.

01:55:19.143 --> 01:55:23.018
We're putting the digital
health check onto the NHS app.

01:55:23.018 --> 01:55:27.231
I have to say to start with,
it's not going to be amazing,

01:55:27.231 --> 01:55:30.180
but, and I shouldn't say but,

01:55:30.180 --> 01:55:33.630
I should say and, and we
have to start somewhere,

01:55:33.630 --> 01:55:34.890
which is allow the patient

01:55:34.890 --> 01:55:37.890
to order their home testing
kit and send it back,

01:55:37.890 --> 01:55:41.880
because we know over the next
year, two years, five years

01:55:41.880 --> 01:55:45.420
that home testing kit will
be available on their phone.

01:55:45.420 --> 01:55:47.850
It will be available electronically.

01:55:47.850 --> 01:55:50.890
And if you don't believe
me, I looked yesterday

01:55:50.890 --> 01:55:53.964
at the camera that sits by the bedside

01:55:53.964 --> 01:55:57.120
that takes all of the
vital signs of the patient

01:55:57.120 --> 01:55:57.953
in front of them

01:55:57.953 --> 01:56:00.240
without touching the patient.

01:56:00.240 --> 01:56:02.100
And when you then think
about what that might do

01:56:02.100 --> 01:56:05.940
to the new hospital programme
and how that tech can evolve

01:56:05.940 --> 01:56:08.700
and when we talked about
virtual wards earlier on,

01:56:08.700 --> 01:56:11.490
how do we connect the
virtual ward technology

01:56:11.490 --> 01:56:15.379
into the NHS app, so that
citizens can use that

01:56:15.379 --> 01:56:17.914
and monitor their health data?

01:56:17.914 --> 01:56:20.850
Most of us have wearables on us.

01:56:20.850 --> 01:56:23.130
The future of how we use our NHS app

01:56:23.130 --> 01:56:26.700
and giving our citizens
their digital account

01:56:26.700 --> 01:56:30.398
to manage their own
healthcare is stunning.

01:56:30.398 --> 01:56:33.450
And for those that don't believe,

01:56:33.450 --> 01:56:36.660
and there are some, we are saying,

01:56:36.660 --> 01:56:40.440
I think very conservatively
that we want to move a third

01:56:40.440 --> 01:56:42.930
of all of our health interactions

01:56:42.930 --> 01:56:45.663
to being digital-first
over the next five years.

01:56:47.250 --> 01:56:48.540
If we can do that,

01:56:48.540 --> 01:56:51.120
then what we're doing is
we are enabling our NHS

01:56:51.120 --> 01:56:53.070
to cope with the relentless demands

01:56:53.070 --> 01:56:54.180
that we've talked about

01:56:54.180 --> 01:56:58.050
in every aspect of the
services around the table.

01:56:58.050 --> 01:56:58.980
Because we know the money

01:56:58.980 --> 01:57:01.770
isn't always going to be
there to keep going up.

01:57:01.770 --> 01:57:05.880
So, this, I have no doubt
is the future of the NHS.

01:57:05.880 --> 01:57:10.076
And I've got Helen here who is
just brilliant with the team

01:57:10.076 --> 01:57:11.880
- Yeah.
- and gets into the detail

01:57:11.880 --> 01:57:16.020
and gets into the numbers in
a way that you cannot imagine.

01:57:16.020 --> 01:57:21.020
And it's how we enable
Helen, the team, through Vin,

01:57:21.055 --> 01:57:22.620
to keep investing,

01:57:22.620 --> 01:57:25.380
because the efficiencies
and the productivity,

01:57:25.380 --> 01:57:30.029
getting people back to work,
helping our economy are huge.

01:57:30.029 --> 01:57:33.573
- Helen, that. That's a hell of a billing.

01:57:35.138 --> 01:57:37.350
- I think Joe-
- Just a couple minutes,

01:57:37.350 --> 01:57:39.330
'cause there were hands
going up all over the,

01:57:39.330 --> 01:57:40.170
all around the board.

01:57:40.170 --> 01:57:41.910
So, you've got to give us time

01:57:41.910 --> 01:57:42.780
to ask you some questions.

01:57:42.780 --> 01:57:45.240
- I think answering questions is fine.

01:57:45.240 --> 01:57:47.280
I think Joe's said it all.

01:57:47.280 --> 01:57:50.820
I think there are so many
amazing human stories

01:57:50.820 --> 01:57:54.150
that come out of being
able to interact directly

01:57:54.150 --> 01:57:57.750
with services and experiences
through the mobile device.

01:57:57.750 --> 01:57:59.340
And I've heard many of those as well.

01:57:59.340 --> 01:58:01.074
And sometimes we don't touch on that

01:58:01.074 --> 01:58:03.540
in terms of what it means to people

01:58:03.540 --> 01:58:04.890
to be able to navigate it through

01:58:04.890 --> 01:58:06.960
to the right service or find a way

01:58:06.960 --> 01:58:09.750
to self-serve and self-manage,

01:58:09.750 --> 01:58:13.800
because people do want to take
the burden away from the NHS

01:58:13.800 --> 01:58:15.363
and help out where they can.

01:58:17.010 --> 01:58:17.843
- Okay.

01:58:19.170 --> 01:58:20.400
Mark's hand came up first.

01:58:20.400 --> 01:58:21.750
Very fast on the draw there, Mark.

01:58:21.750 --> 01:58:22.710
So, you go first,

01:58:22.710 --> 01:58:24.090
and then we'll go around on it.

01:58:24.090 --> 01:58:25.500
- Well, those are terrific presentations.

01:58:25.500 --> 01:58:26.810
I think I'd buy anything from you.

01:58:28.230 --> 01:58:31.260
But I am an enthusiastic,
I'm quite evidence-based,

01:58:31.260 --> 01:58:34.050
so I am an enthusiastic user of the app

01:58:34.050 --> 01:58:36.570
and it gets me my blood test results back

01:58:36.570 --> 01:58:38.730
faster than I've ever had them before

01:58:38.730 --> 01:58:40.620
from turning up in a general practise.

01:58:40.620 --> 01:58:42.780
It gets me my repeat prescriptions.

01:58:42.780 --> 01:58:44.220
- Yeah.
- So, it is

01:58:44.220 --> 01:58:46.410
fantastically useful.

01:58:46.410 --> 01:58:49.230
And I think it as you
say, has a long way to go.

01:58:49.230 --> 01:58:50.220
But I just want to return

01:58:50.220 --> 01:58:51.360
to where Vin started,

01:58:51.360 --> 01:58:53.467
which is actually just the power of data

01:58:53.467 --> 01:58:55.830
for improving patient health,

01:58:55.830 --> 01:58:57.630
and that actually needs to happen

01:58:57.630 --> 01:59:00.180
through research and innovation.

01:59:00.180 --> 01:59:01.980
And I think that there is one missing

01:59:01.980 --> 01:59:05.368
and important piece from
this, which is the workforce

01:59:05.368 --> 01:59:07.680
that has the advanced training

01:59:07.680 --> 01:59:10.576
and skills to develop and use it.

01:59:10.576 --> 01:59:13.320
And I think it's not a
discussion for this afternoon,

01:59:13.320 --> 01:59:16.181
but I think it's one
of the critical issues.

01:59:16.181 --> 01:59:18.450
So, technology is at
least as much about people

01:59:18.450 --> 01:59:22.461
as it is actually about the
software and hardware itself.

01:59:22.461 --> 01:59:24.810
And I think that that's a critical aspect.

01:59:24.810 --> 01:59:27.591
If we're going to get
the most out of the data

01:59:27.591 --> 01:59:30.300
for the benefit of all of us,

01:59:30.300 --> 01:59:32.160
then we need really skilled people.

01:59:32.160 --> 01:59:33.450
- And Wol made that point actually

01:59:33.450 --> 01:59:35.850
in the private board this morning.

01:59:35.850 --> 01:59:38.520
Just going round for comments.

01:59:38.520 --> 01:59:40.440
So, Wol then...

01:59:40.440 --> 01:59:42.570
No, yeah, Amanda, Wol, Steven.

01:59:42.570 --> 01:59:43.403
- Well.
- You first.

01:59:43.403 --> 01:59:44.236
- Yeah,

01:59:44.236 --> 01:59:45.069
I was definitely first.
- All right.

01:59:45.069 --> 01:59:45.902
- I'm just working right to left.

01:59:45.902 --> 01:59:47.043
It's not a...

01:59:47.043 --> 01:59:49.020
- It's a quick one actually. Sorry.

01:59:49.020 --> 01:59:50.601
No, it's you know,

01:59:50.601 --> 01:59:52.290
well because of where

01:59:52.290 --> 01:59:53.697
I'm sitting, Wol.

01:59:53.697 --> 01:59:56.070
Oh, it's just picking up
on Mark's point actually

01:59:56.070 --> 01:59:58.950
a little bit, which is if you
are a regular user of the app,

01:59:58.950 --> 02:00:00.060
every single time you turn it on

02:00:00.060 --> 02:00:04.320
this new functionality, and
actually that is just fantastic.

02:00:04.320 --> 02:00:06.870
You and I have talked before for example,

02:00:06.870 --> 02:00:08.250
about a proxy login

02:00:08.250 --> 02:00:10.020
and the ability to do it for children

02:00:10.020 --> 02:00:11.940
or older parents, whatever.

02:00:11.940 --> 02:00:13.167
And actually, I've just turned mine on

02:00:13.167 --> 02:00:16.890
and it's offering me the
opportunity to do that.

02:00:16.890 --> 02:00:18.540
So, it is just fantastic.

02:00:18.540 --> 02:00:19.712
What about those people

02:00:19.712 --> 02:00:21.180
who are not regular users?

02:00:21.180 --> 02:00:22.950
Because I think it's a
collective challenge.

02:00:22.950 --> 02:00:26.460
But given it is changing so quickly,

02:00:26.460 --> 02:00:27.810
I'm conscious that despite the fact

02:00:27.810 --> 02:00:30.510
that we've got huge
amounts of activity now

02:00:30.510 --> 02:00:31.830
going through the app, there's still a bit

02:00:31.830 --> 02:00:34.259
of a risk that that's some of
the same people we're using.

02:00:34.259 --> 02:00:36.030
And actually perhaps for those people

02:00:36.030 --> 02:00:38.310
who don't have repeat
prescriptions regularly

02:00:38.310 --> 02:00:40.920
or aren't having regular blood
tests, they will not be aware

02:00:40.920 --> 02:00:42.900
of all the functionality
that currently exists.

02:00:42.900 --> 02:00:44.520
So, I recognise that's
a collective challenge,

02:00:44.520 --> 02:00:46.140
but I'd just be really
interested in your thoughts

02:00:46.140 --> 02:00:48.330
on how we can help encourage people

02:00:48.330 --> 02:00:52.380
who maybe aren't traditional
or current users to go back

02:00:52.380 --> 02:00:54.180
and see what more is now available.

02:00:54.180 --> 02:00:56.550
- So, hold, I'm going to get,
I'm doing the same trick,

02:00:56.550 --> 02:00:57.420
'cause otherwise you never
- Yeah.

02:00:57.420 --> 02:00:58.380
- manage to the time.

02:00:58.380 --> 02:01:02.013
I'm heading to, Wol soon,
but as as I've got the mic,

02:01:03.120 --> 02:01:05.310
I think it is, you heard me this one,

02:01:05.310 --> 02:01:06.420
I think it's brilliant,

02:01:06.420 --> 02:01:10.256
but I would also say I think
it is still a bit clunky.

02:01:10.256 --> 02:01:13.312
And I would also say that I'm not sure

02:01:13.312 --> 02:01:17.490
that the headline stat's always reflective

02:01:17.490 --> 02:01:19.877
of the experience on the ground.

02:01:19.877 --> 02:01:22.710
So, Wol and I just opened the NHS app,

02:01:22.710 --> 02:01:24.268
sitting next to each other here.

02:01:24.268 --> 02:01:25.410
Mine worked

02:01:25.410 --> 02:01:26.243
and Wol's didn't.

02:01:26.243 --> 02:01:28.410
It said not available.

02:01:28.410 --> 02:01:29.970
And so, actually what we need in the app,

02:01:29.970 --> 02:01:34.380
we need to keep on driving
its extended functionality,

02:01:34.380 --> 02:01:37.530
but we also need to make
sure that the resilience

02:01:37.530 --> 02:01:42.530
and reach and availability
are fully operational,

02:01:43.500 --> 02:01:46.015
'cause actually nothing
will turn off people faster

02:01:46.015 --> 02:01:50.040
than actually if it
suddenly starts not to work.

02:01:50.040 --> 02:01:51.600
So, on the clunkiness point,

02:01:51.600 --> 02:01:55.020
it's the point I made when
we met a couple weeks ago,

02:01:55.020 --> 02:01:57.660
I went back to it, I'd like to volunteer

02:01:57.660 --> 02:02:01.920
to take part in cancer
research and I can't do it.

02:02:01.920 --> 02:02:04.495
Well, I could do it, but
actually, it takes me to a page.

02:02:04.495 --> 02:02:06.750
And it says, do you want to do it?

02:02:06.750 --> 02:02:09.660
And then, I can't say
yes to cancer research.

02:02:09.660 --> 02:02:12.182
It lists about 200 cancers

02:02:12.182 --> 02:02:15.810
and says, which of these do
you want to enter research for?

02:02:15.810 --> 02:02:18.270
Which point, to be honest, I just say,

02:02:18.270 --> 02:02:20.760
I'm not going to bother,
'cause I don't know.

02:02:20.760 --> 02:02:21.900
I don't even recognise

02:02:21.900 --> 02:02:22.890
what some of them are actually,

02:02:22.890 --> 02:02:26.940
because I'm not suitably educated.

02:02:26.940 --> 02:02:28.530
So, there is something about clunkiness,

02:02:28.530 --> 02:02:31.380
which we must be very
open with each other,

02:02:31.380 --> 02:02:33.960
but it's a brilliant innovation.

02:02:33.960 --> 02:02:36.180
The other one lastly is,

02:02:36.180 --> 02:02:39.330
and it's probably the wrong philosophy,

02:02:39.330 --> 02:02:41.730
but is it open architecture?

02:02:41.730 --> 02:02:46.410
And would we ever considered
opening it to, not commercial,

02:02:46.410 --> 02:02:48.780
but developers who will
put other activities on it

02:02:48.780 --> 02:02:52.866
that would drive foot flow

02:02:52.866 --> 02:02:55.530
to actually get ever
more engagement with it,

02:02:55.530 --> 02:02:56.550
and then allow us actually

02:02:56.550 --> 02:02:59.730
to put more nudge behaviour
and more capability on it.

02:02:59.730 --> 02:03:01.920
But would we ever have open architecture

02:03:01.920 --> 02:03:03.060
rather than our own teams

02:03:03.060 --> 02:03:05.157
designing the new idea of
functionality and adding it?

02:03:05.157 --> 02:03:07.890
And that may be a really
stupid idea of course,

02:03:07.890 --> 02:03:09.017
but I just ask it.

02:03:10.020 --> 02:03:11.820
- Both things I was going
to ask, but thank you.

02:03:11.820 --> 02:03:12.653
Nevermind.

02:03:12.653 --> 02:03:13.899
I'll come at it slightly differently.

02:03:13.899 --> 02:03:16.587
So, one of the things is that,

02:03:16.587 --> 02:03:19.050
and the reasons why it
didn't work was one,

02:03:19.050 --> 02:03:20.910
Rich could get his medical
records, I couldn't.

02:03:20.910 --> 02:03:23.430
And that's the surgery. So, it's patchy.

02:03:23.430 --> 02:03:25.934
So, the experience, the headline is great,

02:03:25.934 --> 02:03:27.900
but actually experience is different,

02:03:27.900 --> 02:03:29.040
depending on what your surgery

02:03:29.040 --> 02:03:30.780
has decided to allow you to do.

02:03:30.780 --> 02:03:32.370
They told me, I can't
access medical records,

02:03:32.370 --> 02:03:33.930
they checked up blah, blah, blah, blah.

02:03:33.930 --> 02:03:36.698
Sometime actually in
2024, which we're now in.

02:03:36.698 --> 02:03:40.320
The second thing is, and please
take this the nicest way.

02:03:40.320 --> 02:03:41.790
It was a great presentation.

02:03:41.790 --> 02:03:43.440
I'm a huge believer in the app,

02:03:43.440 --> 02:03:44.640
but one of the reasons it's clunky

02:03:44.640 --> 02:03:46.020
is because it's not native on your phone.

02:03:46.020 --> 02:03:47.460
- Absolutely.
- Right?

02:03:47.460 --> 02:03:48.477
And actually, we all...

02:03:48.477 --> 02:03:52.080
It's a markedly different experience

02:03:52.080 --> 02:03:55.320
when you have a app that's
native on your phone,

02:03:55.320 --> 02:03:56.667
'cause you do so much more with it.

02:03:56.667 --> 02:03:59.820
So, you can customise what you
see with your cancer stuff,

02:03:59.820 --> 02:04:00.870
link to your records.

02:04:00.870 --> 02:04:03.993
In a way, we can't, because we basically,

02:04:04.890 --> 02:04:07.470
you got to, you're
basically linking to a page.

02:04:07.470 --> 02:04:09.450
Not quite as bad as that, but nearly.

02:04:09.450 --> 02:04:11.400
- Yeah.
- And that's not great.

02:04:11.400 --> 02:04:12.633
And so, my question is,

02:04:14.010 --> 02:04:17.520
you've given us the
benefits and the vision.

02:04:17.520 --> 02:04:18.820
Tell me about the chassis.

02:04:19.740 --> 02:04:22.823
Tell me about what needs
to be built to get there,

02:04:22.823 --> 02:04:26.190
because the danger is
we'll overexcite people,

02:04:26.190 --> 02:04:29.850
and native apps, scalability, security,

02:04:29.850 --> 02:04:33.210
ability to add stuff won't
happen the way we expect.

02:04:33.210 --> 02:04:34.043
- Very good.

02:04:34.043 --> 02:04:35.730
So, the last question coming at you

02:04:35.730 --> 02:04:37.170
is from Steve-
- Well, you just asked the...

02:04:37.170 --> 02:04:39.110
- So, the question was going
to be it's just a front

02:04:39.110 --> 02:04:41.130
to a webpage at the moment, isn't it?

02:04:41.130 --> 02:04:42.720
When's it going to be
a full functioning app?

02:04:42.720 --> 02:04:44.220
Because that will give
a lot of functionality

02:04:44.220 --> 02:04:46.170
that will avoid the clunkiness.

02:04:46.170 --> 02:04:47.003
- [Richard] I didn't realise

02:04:47.003 --> 02:04:47.836
that's what I'd asked, actually.

02:04:47.836 --> 02:04:48.830
- That is what you asked.

02:04:49.703 --> 02:04:50.536
- [Wol] Slightly differently.

02:04:50.536 --> 02:04:51.704
- That's the technical way of asking it.

02:04:51.704 --> 02:04:54.570
- Thank you very much.

02:04:54.570 --> 02:04:55.890
So, Joe, Helen, Vin,

02:04:55.890 --> 02:04:58.786
I dunno how you want to divide those up.

02:04:58.786 --> 02:04:59.880
- Like I'm being university challenged,

02:04:59.880 --> 02:05:00.713
we've got an order

02:05:00.713 --> 02:05:01.740
which we'll answer these.

02:05:01.740 --> 02:05:06.540
So, the first thing is I
think digital workforce.

02:05:06.540 --> 02:05:10.361
So, we will shortly be publishing
a digital workforce plan

02:05:10.361 --> 02:05:12.480
for the NHS.
- Thank you.

02:05:12.480 --> 02:05:15.000
- Bob Wachter did a review

02:05:15.000 --> 02:05:19.860
of the country's digital
readiness several years ago now

02:05:19.860 --> 02:05:23.247
and he returned back to the
UK earlier late last year.

02:05:23.247 --> 02:05:25.470
And in fact, commented
on how much progress

02:05:25.470 --> 02:05:27.720
we'd made with things
like the digital academy,

02:05:27.720 --> 02:05:29.610
but also commented on
how much more we will do.

02:05:29.610 --> 02:05:31.738
So, we will be publishing
a digital workforce plan

02:05:31.738 --> 02:05:33.603
in the first half of this year.

02:05:34.656 --> 02:05:37.290
The second part then
with regard to signing up

02:05:37.290 --> 02:05:39.120
to research should be part of research

02:05:39.120 --> 02:05:41.628
is not NHS England's webpage,

02:05:41.628 --> 02:05:44.730
it is run by the National
Institute of Health Research.

02:05:44.730 --> 02:05:46.890
We've taken the feedback
that you've given us, chair,

02:05:46.890 --> 02:05:48.960
and others have given
us about the clunkiness

02:05:48.960 --> 02:05:50.550
by which you can sign on to research

02:05:50.550 --> 02:05:53.097
and a meeting with Lucy
Chappell, the chief exec of NHR

02:05:53.097 --> 02:05:54.960
and the chief scientific advisor

02:05:54.960 --> 02:05:57.180
to the Department of Health shortly,

02:05:57.180 --> 02:05:58.500
to discuss how we can improve
- Okay.

02:05:58.500 --> 02:06:00.360
- that user experience for them.

02:06:00.360 --> 02:06:03.240
And in terms of the access
to the medical record.

02:06:03.240 --> 02:06:07.140
So, in order to get full
access to your medical record,

02:06:07.140 --> 02:06:08.250
you have to ask your GP

02:06:08.250 --> 02:06:10.225
to give you access to the medical record.

02:06:10.225 --> 02:06:14.130
Around 85% of the GPs in
the country have done that.

02:06:14.130 --> 02:06:16.920
And we continue to work
with the remaining 15%

02:06:16.920 --> 02:06:19.140
to make it possible for them to do that.

02:06:19.140 --> 02:06:19.973
They have some

02:06:19.973 --> 02:06:20.806
understandable concerns,

02:06:20.806 --> 02:06:21.840
particularly around safeguarding

02:06:21.840 --> 02:06:24.330
to make sure it's safe
access to the record.

02:06:24.330 --> 02:06:25.920
But obviously, we are continuing

02:06:25.920 --> 02:06:26.880
to increase that number

02:06:26.880 --> 02:06:28.170
while we work with them on a practise

02:06:28.170 --> 02:06:29.955
by practise basis.
- Very good.

02:06:29.955 --> 02:06:31.590
- Just on the question of the app,

02:06:31.590 --> 02:06:34.230
we are in the transformation
to absolutely determine

02:06:34.230 --> 02:06:35.560
to back the developments in the app.

02:06:35.560 --> 02:06:37.080
- Yeah.
- I think many of the comments

02:06:37.080 --> 02:06:38.040
that colleagues have made

02:06:38.040 --> 02:06:39.360
around the table relate

02:06:39.360 --> 02:06:42.254
to how we can improve it even
more than it is at the moment,

02:06:42.254 --> 02:06:43.680
but it's no doubt that it is transforming

02:06:43.680 --> 02:06:44.880
the relationship with the citizen.

02:06:44.880 --> 02:06:46.230
- Definitely.
- So, Joe and Helen,

02:06:46.230 --> 02:06:47.760
I'll hand over to you.

02:06:47.760 --> 02:06:52.007
- I suppose briefly just
a sense of scale really.

02:06:52.007 --> 02:06:54.780
We spend about a third on the NHS app

02:06:54.780 --> 02:06:59.100
as we do in my hospital,
which is just gives you

02:06:59.100 --> 02:07:03.030
a sense of where we're at investment-wise.

02:07:03.030 --> 02:07:03.863
And

02:07:05.039 --> 02:07:10.039
it's where I think we're
going is when we ask the NHS,

02:07:11.070 --> 02:07:13.146
we know we can always spend more money

02:07:13.146 --> 02:07:16.710
and there's a bit of selling the vision

02:07:16.710 --> 02:07:19.800
is part of how we take the NHS

02:07:19.800 --> 02:07:21.600
on this journey with us as well as...

02:07:21.600 --> 02:07:23.370
Our citizens are there already.

02:07:23.370 --> 02:07:25.677
Our citizens are one
that absolutely wanted.

02:07:25.677 --> 02:07:27.960
And we're at that place at the moment

02:07:27.960 --> 02:07:30.810
of how do we engage
with every organisation,

02:07:30.810 --> 02:07:33.930
whether it's GP practise
to switch the record on,

02:07:33.930 --> 02:07:35.400
whether it's the acute hospital

02:07:35.400 --> 02:07:37.640
to put the Wi-Fi, whatever it is.

02:07:37.640 --> 02:07:39.608
At the moment, if you ask the NHS,

02:07:39.608 --> 02:07:41.468
there's a bit of we want a faster horse

02:07:41.468 --> 02:07:44.790
and actually this is the car.

02:07:44.790 --> 02:07:45.623
And the question

02:07:45.623 --> 02:07:46.800
about the chassis,
- Chassis, yeah.

02:07:46.800 --> 02:07:48.780
- is completely spot on.

02:07:48.780 --> 02:07:50.280
And I'll hand you over to Helen,

02:07:50.280 --> 02:07:52.650
because we are not going for a Ferrari,

02:07:52.650 --> 02:07:55.314
we actually want something
that everybody can use.

02:07:55.314 --> 02:07:57.330
So, Helen.
- And the chassis question

02:07:57.330 --> 02:07:59.550
was the one I asked without
realising, I just thought.

02:07:59.550 --> 02:08:00.474
- [Wol] No, that wasn't yours.

02:08:00.474 --> 02:08:02.250
- It wasn't mine. Okay.

02:08:02.250 --> 02:08:03.083
Helen.

02:08:03.083 --> 02:08:05.190
- Well, actually, I was
just going to say in answer

02:08:05.190 --> 02:08:08.580
to the question about how do
we move to a native environment

02:08:08.580 --> 02:08:11.288
and get a lot of the smart
functions that you see

02:08:11.288 --> 02:08:13.890
with other applications
on your phone today.

02:08:13.890 --> 02:08:15.570
So, I suppose I've got good news on that

02:08:15.570 --> 02:08:19.410
in terms of we are
currently developing that

02:08:19.410 --> 02:08:21.810
and we're hoping in the next month or two

02:08:21.810 --> 02:08:26.160
to actually put that native
code transition in place.

02:08:26.160 --> 02:08:28.890
And then, from there, once
you've put that base layer in

02:08:28.890 --> 02:08:32.280
or a chassis if you like,
we will then be building

02:08:32.280 --> 02:08:33.540
on that the kind of features

02:08:33.540 --> 02:08:37.080
that you are used to seeing
on a mobile application.

02:08:37.080 --> 02:08:39.453
So, I think that was the first question.

02:08:40.365 --> 02:08:44.970
There is a lot of work
under the hood of the app

02:08:44.970 --> 02:08:48.090
and it is, I don't want
to say uniquely complex,

02:08:48.090 --> 02:08:48.923
'cause other people

02:08:48.923 --> 02:08:50.820
have very complex technical environments,

02:08:50.820 --> 02:08:52.050
but I think everybody knows

02:08:52.050 --> 02:08:53.910
that we've got a very fragmented

02:08:53.910 --> 02:08:56.487
technical landscape in the NHS

02:08:56.487 --> 02:08:57.900
and the complexity

02:08:57.900 --> 02:09:01.470
of all of the integrations we
need to do to move forward.

02:09:01.470 --> 02:09:04.417
So, in terms of that question
about open architecture

02:09:04.417 --> 02:09:06.351
and how does it make sense

02:09:06.351 --> 02:09:09.840
or not to integrate is a
really big trade off call.

02:09:09.840 --> 02:09:11.790
It's a bit like the feedback

02:09:11.790 --> 02:09:15.360
about be part of research being clunky.

02:09:15.360 --> 02:09:17.490
A lot of our integrations are handoffs

02:09:17.490 --> 02:09:21.930
to a third party web environment.

02:09:21.930 --> 02:09:25.110
And therefore we've got
control of a certain part

02:09:25.110 --> 02:09:27.900
of the experience, but
not the full end-to-end.

02:09:27.900 --> 02:09:29.735
And I think we have to be really mindful

02:09:29.735 --> 02:09:31.170
given we want to give

02:09:31.170 --> 02:09:34.530
the very best possible patient experience,

02:09:34.530 --> 02:09:36.750
empower people to self-serve,

02:09:36.750 --> 02:09:38.340
and do things through the app

02:09:38.340 --> 02:09:41.160
about how much we control
and develop ourselves,

02:09:41.160 --> 02:09:45.120
because it is a very unique
proposition as the NHS.

02:09:45.120 --> 02:09:47.218
And I think we really
want to leverage that

02:09:47.218 --> 02:09:49.040
as much as possible.

02:09:49.040 --> 02:09:51.009
- So, I would finish up by saying,

02:09:51.009 --> 02:09:54.810
we've seen unbelievable
progress in the last 12 years.

02:09:54.810 --> 02:09:56.670
- Amazing.
- And the reality

02:09:56.670 --> 02:09:57.900
of where we're going with this

02:09:57.900 --> 02:10:01.140
and the appetite of our
citizens to get on board

02:10:01.140 --> 02:10:04.650
and engage with it is voracious.

02:10:04.650 --> 02:10:06.990
And we need to make sure that we,

02:10:06.990 --> 02:10:10.470
in every aspect of the NHS family,

02:10:10.470 --> 02:10:13.350
we are playing our part as
I say, whether it's the GP,

02:10:13.350 --> 02:10:14.670
whether it's the mental health services,

02:10:14.670 --> 02:10:16.560
supporting them, pharmacy,

02:10:16.560 --> 02:10:20.400
and just getting the NHS,
the health and care system,

02:10:20.400 --> 02:10:21.660
'cause don't forget social care,

02:10:21.660 --> 02:10:24.270
because we have to get that connected

02:10:24.270 --> 02:10:26.160
in the same way in order

02:10:26.160 --> 02:10:29.583
that we can provide that full
data set for our systems.

02:10:30.450 --> 02:10:31.590
- Very good. So-

02:10:31.590 --> 02:10:33.960
- One final comment,
chair, just to finish up.

02:10:33.960 --> 02:10:36.570
It'll literally be two
sentences if that's okay.

02:10:36.570 --> 02:10:38.700
So, just saying this week,
we've launched a campaign

02:10:38.700 --> 02:10:40.410
through social media broadcast meeting

02:10:40.410 --> 02:10:42.420
and others to encourage
the remaining members

02:10:42.420 --> 02:10:45.480
of the population who've not
downloaded the app to do so.

02:10:45.480 --> 02:10:47.911
It's got the catchy phrase, tap the app.

02:10:47.911 --> 02:10:49.650
And as I said-

02:10:49.650 --> 02:10:50.483
- [Richard] Did you made that up?

02:10:50.483 --> 02:10:52.113
- If it wasn't you, no, Chris did.

02:10:52.113 --> 02:10:52.946
If it were...

02:10:52.946 --> 02:10:54.840
So, yeah, I'm not taking credit for that,

02:10:54.840 --> 02:10:56.490
but I just, we encourage people, I think,

02:10:56.490 --> 02:10:57.690
both through this public board

02:10:57.690 --> 02:11:00.690
and through other means to
use the app and make sure,

02:11:00.690 --> 02:11:01.860
'cause it'll only get better and better.

02:11:01.860 --> 02:11:03.000
- So, look, I think we could have a,

02:11:03.000 --> 02:11:06.330
we could go for a long
time on the potential here,

02:11:06.330 --> 02:11:08.520
and so it's been a very good,

02:11:08.520 --> 02:11:11.760
it's a glimpse only 'cause
of the time afforded to you,

02:11:11.760 --> 02:11:13.920
to what I think Amanda said, I've said,

02:11:13.920 --> 02:11:17.037
colleagues have said is, and
you've reflected back to us

02:11:17.037 --> 02:11:19.801
and I completely agree is
utterly transformational.

02:11:19.801 --> 02:11:21.987
And to think that five,
six, seven years ago

02:11:21.987 --> 02:11:24.090
and we just think, the NHS continues

02:11:24.090 --> 02:11:27.480
to evolve all the time,
continues to innovate and change.

02:11:27.480 --> 02:11:29.370
And I think this NHS app

02:11:29.370 --> 02:11:30.750
increasing its functionality,

02:11:30.750 --> 02:11:32.130
increasing its interoperability,

02:11:32.130 --> 02:11:36.660
the way people behave,
it will be remarkable.

02:11:36.660 --> 02:11:39.420
So, thank you for all your
leadership in the space actually.

02:11:39.420 --> 02:11:40.620
Very good.

02:11:40.620 --> 02:11:44.183
So, we're going to get to
the last item on the agenda.

02:11:44.183 --> 02:11:46.080
And, Claire, I think it's you and John,

02:11:46.080 --> 02:11:48.180
but I think John's going
to lead off, is he?

02:11:48.180 --> 02:11:49.740
And again, it's a really good,

02:11:49.740 --> 02:11:52.470
it's a really good paper setting out

02:11:52.470 --> 02:11:55.560
quite an interesting set
of potential challenges,

02:11:55.560 --> 02:11:57.390
but also opportunities for us.

02:11:57.390 --> 02:11:59.010
So, it's dementia programme

02:11:59.010 --> 02:12:01.440
and Alzheimer's disease
modifying treatments.

02:12:01.440 --> 02:12:03.150
- Great. Thank you, chair.

02:12:03.150 --> 02:12:06.060
I will try and be concise,
being the last item,

02:12:06.060 --> 02:12:09.060
but I won't rush, 'cause
this is so important

02:12:09.060 --> 02:12:11.606
for so many patients and families.

02:12:11.606 --> 02:12:14.070
And actually, when we come on

02:12:14.070 --> 02:12:16.058
and talk about the
potential new treatments,

02:12:16.058 --> 02:12:18.150
the financial implications

02:12:18.150 --> 02:12:21.873
for the NHS we're also very
significant potentially.

02:12:22.864 --> 02:12:25.650
So, the paper is really
divided up into two sections.

02:12:25.650 --> 02:12:27.990
The first part provides an update

02:12:27.990 --> 02:12:30.180
on the wider dementia programme

02:12:30.180 --> 02:12:32.100
with a specific focus on progress

02:12:32.100 --> 02:12:34.050
to improve rates of dementia diagnosis.

02:12:34.050 --> 02:12:36.750
And the second part then focuses in

02:12:36.750 --> 02:12:38.640
really on NHS preparations

02:12:38.640 --> 02:12:41.220
for potential new
disease-modifying treatments

02:12:41.220 --> 02:12:43.140
for Alzheimer's disease,

02:12:43.140 --> 02:12:45.450
which is the most common
cause of dementia.

02:12:45.450 --> 02:12:48.963
So, representing up to two
thirds of all dementia cases.

02:12:50.100 --> 02:12:52.399
For context, rates of dementia

02:12:52.399 --> 02:12:55.560
including those caused by
Alzheimer's are growing

02:12:55.560 --> 02:12:58.410
and are once again, actually
the leading cause of death

02:12:58.410 --> 02:13:02.460
after having very briefly
being overtaken by coronavirus.

02:13:02.460 --> 02:13:05.640
But dementia is now the
leading cause of death.

02:13:05.640 --> 02:13:08.130
A diagnosis of dementia
we know is devastating

02:13:08.130 --> 02:13:10.650
for individuals and their families,

02:13:10.650 --> 02:13:11.970
and the impact on society

02:13:11.970 --> 02:13:14.130
and the economy more broadly is huge.

02:13:14.130 --> 02:13:16.317
In terms of the impact on the NHS

02:13:16.317 --> 02:13:19.599
and this is set out to
write at paragraph one,

02:13:19.599 --> 02:13:22.410
we know that 25% of acute beds

02:13:22.410 --> 02:13:24.909
are occupied by people with dementia.

02:13:24.909 --> 02:13:27.690
Their length of stay is on average,

02:13:27.690 --> 02:13:32.372
twice as long as other people
aged over 65 without dementia.

02:13:32.372 --> 02:13:35.250
90% of people with
dementia found admission

02:13:35.250 --> 02:13:38.760
to hospital frightening and confusing.

02:13:38.760 --> 02:13:40.980
43% of those admitted to hospital

02:13:40.980 --> 02:13:45.180
with dementia were admitted due
to a UTI or chest infection.

02:13:45.180 --> 02:13:48.930
And over a one year period,
25% of people with dementia

02:13:48.930 --> 02:13:51.390
living in their own homes were admitted

02:13:51.390 --> 02:13:55.360
to hospital with a potentially
treatable condition at home.

02:13:56.940 --> 02:14:00.990
So, a timely diagnosis
of dementia is critical

02:14:00.990 --> 02:14:02.430
and it enables people

02:14:02.430 --> 02:14:03.840
to access advice,

02:14:03.840 --> 02:14:06.330
information, care, and
support to live well

02:14:06.330 --> 02:14:09.270
and reduce their chances of
needing hospital admission.

02:14:09.270 --> 02:14:12.300
And that is why actually back in 2015,

02:14:12.300 --> 02:14:17.300
we set a national ambition for
at least two thirds of people

02:14:17.370 --> 02:14:19.437
with dementia having a formal diagnosis.

02:14:19.437 --> 02:14:23.160
So, that's target of 66.7%.

02:14:23.160 --> 02:14:24.720
And just prior to the pandemic,

02:14:24.720 --> 02:14:26.190
the dementia diagnosis rate

02:14:26.190 --> 02:14:28.010
had been tracking above this ambition.

02:14:28.010 --> 02:14:30.930
But we did lose ground during the pandemic

02:14:30.930 --> 02:14:33.608
as the numbers of people coming
forward into primary care

02:14:33.608 --> 02:14:37.290
with symptoms dropped off significantly.

02:14:37.290 --> 02:14:39.060
Our focus on recovery has meant

02:14:39.060 --> 02:14:40.650
we are now seeing consistent,

02:14:40.650 --> 02:14:44.070
but sustained improvements
in the diagnosis rate

02:14:44.070 --> 02:14:45.498
and the December data

02:14:45.498 --> 02:14:50.367
from '23 shows that we are now at 64.6%.

02:14:50.367 --> 02:14:54.115
So, just under the target we set.

02:14:54.115 --> 02:14:57.330
But we have also seen a
17% increase in referrals

02:14:57.330 --> 02:14:59.091
into memory assessment services

02:14:59.091 --> 02:15:01.620
between April to December 23

02:15:01.620 --> 02:15:04.987
compared to for previous
period and the previous year.

02:15:04.987 --> 02:15:08.100
And we are pretty clear
that with growing awareness

02:15:08.100 --> 02:15:09.780
and in fact media reporting

02:15:09.780 --> 02:15:12.180
of potential new
disease-modifying treatments,

02:15:12.180 --> 02:15:15.750
which I will come onto next,
we expect referrals to continue

02:15:15.750 --> 02:15:18.959
to increase further, supported
by a range of activities

02:15:18.959 --> 02:15:22.487
set out in the paper
at paragraphs 8 to 16,

02:15:22.487 --> 02:15:24.870
which I will assume people have read.

02:15:24.870 --> 02:15:29.054
So, if I turn now to the new
treatments for Alzheimer's,

02:15:29.054 --> 02:15:31.470
the first thing to say
is current treatments

02:15:31.470 --> 02:15:33.510
for the disease are largely focused

02:15:33.510 --> 02:15:35.845
on helping with dementia symptoms,

02:15:35.845 --> 02:15:37.890
but they don't alter the decline

02:15:37.890 --> 02:15:41.820
in cognition, language,
or problem solving.

02:15:41.820 --> 02:15:43.980
But there is an exciting pipeline of new

02:15:43.980 --> 02:15:46.200
and treatments that
are now showing promise

02:15:46.200 --> 02:15:48.360
in actually modifying
the different elements

02:15:48.360 --> 02:15:50.340
of the disease process.

02:15:50.340 --> 02:15:51.600
So, our horizon scanning

02:15:51.600 --> 02:15:53.550
and some of this is set
out to turn next year,

02:15:53.550 --> 02:15:57.120
shows that there are 28
disease-modifying treatments

02:15:57.120 --> 02:15:58.760
in late stage trials.

02:15:58.760 --> 02:16:02.220
And the first two of these,
Lecanemab and Donanemab,

02:16:02.220 --> 02:16:06.210
are currently applying for
UK marketing authorisations.

02:16:06.210 --> 02:16:07.770
NICE technology appraisals

02:16:07.770 --> 02:16:11.190
are also now running in parallel
with that licencing process

02:16:11.190 --> 02:16:14.280
to minimise delays in reaching
decisions around the clinical

02:16:14.280 --> 02:16:16.860
and cost-effectiveness of these treatments

02:16:16.860 --> 02:16:19.708
should those licencing
decisions be positive.

02:16:19.708 --> 02:16:21.933
And there are a number of
different recommendations

02:16:21.933 --> 02:16:23.970
that actually could be made by NICE,

02:16:23.970 --> 02:16:27.360
which include recommended for routine use,

02:16:27.360 --> 02:16:29.478
not recommended for routine use,

02:16:29.478 --> 02:16:32.125
recommended for managed access.

02:16:32.125 --> 02:16:35.460
And finally, and this
is rarely actually used,

02:16:35.460 --> 02:16:39.330
a recommendation for use only in research.

02:16:39.330 --> 02:16:40.650
And it's worth actually just saying

02:16:40.650 --> 02:16:43.172
that under a routine
commissioning recommendation,

02:16:43.172 --> 02:16:47.301
NHS commissioners, and
this is principally ICBs,

02:16:47.301 --> 02:16:49.590
but does include NHS England

02:16:49.590 --> 02:16:51.979
as the commissioner of PET CT capacity

02:16:51.979 --> 02:16:53.910
and the genomic testing

02:16:53.910 --> 02:16:57.000
that is associated with this pathway.

02:16:57.000 --> 02:16:58.020
But those commissioners

02:16:58.020 --> 02:17:01.050
are under a legal requirement
to make funding available

02:17:01.050 --> 02:17:03.960
for the entire eligible patient population

02:17:03.960 --> 02:17:07.458
within 90 days of NICE
final guidance being issued

02:17:07.458 --> 02:17:11.460
unless a funding variation
is agreed by NICE.

02:17:11.460 --> 02:17:13.800
So, I think that's
quite an important point

02:17:13.800 --> 02:17:15.960
for the board to recognise.

02:17:15.960 --> 02:17:20.430
Nevertheless, whilst these two treatments

02:17:20.430 --> 02:17:22.770
are rightly considered
breakthrough medicines,

02:17:22.770 --> 02:17:25.491
creating significant hope,
I think, for the future,

02:17:25.491 --> 02:17:28.950
their benefit is actually
relatively modest.

02:17:28.950 --> 02:17:31.680
They will not stop people
with Alzheimer's disease

02:17:31.680 --> 02:17:36.090
from continuing to decline
and they do not offer a cure.

02:17:36.090 --> 02:17:38.370
Additionally, they come
with a potential risk

02:17:38.370 --> 02:17:41.790
of brain bleeds or swelling known as ARIA,

02:17:41.790 --> 02:17:43.354
and the treatment regime

02:17:43.354 --> 02:17:47.100
which requires fortnightly infusions

02:17:47.100 --> 02:17:48.600
in the case of Lecanemab

02:17:48.600 --> 02:17:52.019
and for weekly infusions
in the case of Donanemab,

02:17:52.019 --> 02:17:54.300
will clearly be very intensive

02:17:54.300 --> 02:17:56.730
for patients and their families.

02:17:56.730 --> 02:17:59.521
So, the programme we've
set up to prepare the NHS

02:17:59.521 --> 02:18:04.020
for implementing these treatments
should they be licenced

02:18:04.020 --> 02:18:07.560
and approved by NICE is
really having to deal

02:18:07.560 --> 02:18:09.960
with considerable number of uncertainties.

02:18:09.960 --> 02:18:11.430
And we are planning at the moment

02:18:11.430 --> 02:18:14.010
and modelling for a range
of possible scenarios

02:18:14.010 --> 02:18:17.275
and absolutely with an eye on
potential future treatments

02:18:17.275 --> 02:18:21.000
and diagnostic tests which
we know are on the horizon,

02:18:21.000 --> 02:18:24.930
including things like the
blood-based biomarkers.

02:18:24.930 --> 02:18:27.900
So, just to emphasise some
of these uncertainties,

02:18:27.900 --> 02:18:31.440
nobody yet knows whether UK licences

02:18:31.440 --> 02:18:36.440
will be granted by the MHRA
for these first treatments.

02:18:37.080 --> 02:18:38.970
We are working very closely with NICE,

02:18:38.970 --> 02:18:42.160
but it is for the NICE
independent committee process

02:18:43.200 --> 02:18:44.430
to reach conclusions

02:18:44.430 --> 02:18:47.072
and recommendations on the
use of these treatments.

02:18:47.072 --> 02:18:48.480
We don't yet know actually

02:18:48.480 --> 02:18:50.970
how many of the estimated
1.6 million people

02:18:50.970 --> 02:18:52.977
with mild cognitive impairment

02:18:52.977 --> 02:18:57.381
may present in primary care
for further assessment.

02:18:57.381 --> 02:18:59.040
Furthermore, we don't know

02:18:59.040 --> 02:19:02.310
how many patients may actually
be eligible for treatment,

02:19:02.310 --> 02:19:04.800
but we know from NICE
that they have estimated

02:19:04.800 --> 02:19:09.240
that this could be between
50,000 and 280,000.

02:19:09.240 --> 02:19:12.060
So, quite a big range at present.

02:19:12.060 --> 02:19:14.520
And finally, and this is important,

02:19:14.520 --> 02:19:15.810
we don't actually yet know

02:19:15.810 --> 02:19:18.040
how many of the eligible
patient population

02:19:18.040 --> 02:19:20.784
may choose to take up the treatment

02:19:20.784 --> 02:19:23.920
given the relatively
modest clinical benefits,

02:19:23.920 --> 02:19:27.480
the safety profile associated
with these medicines,

02:19:27.480 --> 02:19:30.330
and the intensive nature
of a treatment regime.

02:19:30.330 --> 02:19:33.312
But despite all of those uncertainties,

02:19:33.312 --> 02:19:36.840
I think hopefully board members
will see from the diagram

02:19:36.840 --> 02:19:40.710
in annex B that we really do
have a very good understanding

02:19:40.710 --> 02:19:43.789
of the patient pathway
for these first treatments

02:19:43.789 --> 02:19:47.460
and where we would need to
expand existing capacity

02:19:47.460 --> 02:19:49.170
and put in place new services

02:19:49.170 --> 02:19:51.370
in order to make these
treatments available.

02:19:52.851 --> 02:19:54.540
Last few comments.

02:19:54.540 --> 02:19:57.720
The potential costs under
a full rollout scenario

02:19:57.720 --> 02:20:00.600
are currently estimated
between 500 million

02:20:00.600 --> 02:20:02.624
and a billion pounds per year,

02:20:02.624 --> 02:20:04.800
and we'll need to be carefully considered

02:20:04.800 --> 02:20:06.990
as part of an next spending review.

02:20:06.990 --> 02:20:09.270
These figures include both the drug costs

02:20:09.270 --> 02:20:11.340
and the service implementation costs

02:20:11.340 --> 02:20:14.010
and should be treated
with a degree of caution,

02:20:14.010 --> 02:20:15.510
because they are in part,

02:20:15.510 --> 02:20:17.940
based on US drug pricing information

02:20:17.940 --> 02:20:19.890
that's in the public domain.

02:20:19.890 --> 02:20:22.410
But companies will of
course be able to offer

02:20:22.410 --> 02:20:23.970
confidential discounts

02:20:23.970 --> 02:20:26.112
as part of the standard NICE process

02:20:26.112 --> 02:20:27.600
and potentially enter

02:20:27.600 --> 02:20:30.660
into commercial negotiations
with NHS England

02:20:30.660 --> 02:20:33.690
to further support their
value proposition to NICE.

02:20:33.690 --> 02:20:37.710
So, in summary, I think we
are at an exciting point

02:20:37.710 --> 02:20:40.527
with some potential new
medicines to roll out on the NHS

02:20:40.527 --> 02:20:43.590
and a significant number in the pipeline.

02:20:43.590 --> 02:20:45.630
But implementation will absolutely need

02:20:45.630 --> 02:20:48.598
to be dependent on MHRA
licencing decisions

02:20:48.598 --> 02:20:50.754
and recommendations from NICE.

02:20:50.754 --> 02:20:53.880
The potential budget
impact is very significant

02:20:53.880 --> 02:20:55.170
and will need to be considered

02:20:55.170 --> 02:20:57.652
in the context of the spending review.

02:20:57.652 --> 02:21:00.300
Provision of accurate and
accessible information

02:21:00.300 --> 02:21:02.010
for the public will also be key,

02:21:02.010 --> 02:21:05.910
including on the potential
benefits and risks of treatment.

02:21:05.910 --> 02:21:07.590
There will need to be a particular focus

02:21:07.590 --> 02:21:09.930
on developing the workforce of experts

02:21:09.930 --> 02:21:11.460
in cognitive assessments,

02:21:11.460 --> 02:21:13.564
including potential digital options,

02:21:13.564 --> 02:21:16.656
diagnosis and treatment administration.

02:21:16.656 --> 02:21:20.560
The diagnostic pathways
including the use of MRI

02:21:21.450 --> 02:21:24.420
and safety monitoring is significant,

02:21:24.420 --> 02:21:25.950
including standing up

02:21:25.950 --> 02:21:29.130
genetic tests, a new
type of PET CT scanning,

02:21:29.130 --> 02:21:32.070
and additional lumbar puncture support.

02:21:32.070 --> 02:21:33.483
So, we will-

02:21:34.560 --> 02:21:37.202
- I think you've given us quite
an expansive description of-

02:21:37.202 --> 02:21:38.760
- That's a brief summary, chair.

02:21:38.760 --> 02:21:40.830
And I will stop there

02:21:40.830 --> 02:21:42.821
and see if people have any questions.

02:21:42.821 --> 02:21:43.704
- I'm sure there are.

02:21:43.704 --> 02:21:45.074
So, Mark, I know wants to go.

02:21:45.074 --> 02:21:47.083
And David want-
- Here's a question.

02:21:47.083 --> 02:21:49.930
So, firstly, I thought it was
a very good balanced paper.

02:21:49.930 --> 02:21:52.200
It has been an enormous journey

02:21:52.200 --> 02:21:54.990
to get anything that has
any significant effect

02:21:54.990 --> 02:21:59.520
and I think you rightly
said that this is probably

02:21:59.520 --> 02:22:03.540
the sort of a first generation effect.

02:22:03.540 --> 02:22:05.730
And it's worth also remembering

02:22:05.730 --> 02:22:07.590
that there are other treatments

02:22:07.590 --> 02:22:09.840
that affect the progression
of Alzheimer's disease.

02:22:09.840 --> 02:22:12.690
For example, the
treatment of comorbidities

02:22:12.690 --> 02:22:15.000
such as diabetes, hypertension,

02:22:15.000 --> 02:22:17.400
and other causes of vascular disease.

02:22:17.400 --> 02:22:18.660
So, one shouldn't forget that,

02:22:18.660 --> 02:22:22.124
because you can make a difference
using existing approaches.

02:22:22.124 --> 02:22:26.130
But as I say, these antibodies
do share some effect,

02:22:26.130 --> 02:22:27.420
but they're not the magic bullet

02:22:27.420 --> 02:22:29.820
that everyone would like, I think.

02:22:29.820 --> 02:22:30.960
- Thank you. Does that help?

02:22:30.960 --> 02:22:32.430
David?
- Yeah, exactly, John,

02:22:32.430 --> 02:22:34.170
they're very clear, so thank you

02:22:34.170 --> 02:22:35.863
and thanks for the explanation as well.

02:22:35.863 --> 02:22:38.310
Am I right in understanding though

02:22:38.310 --> 02:22:41.880
that in order to get
access to this medication,

02:22:41.880 --> 02:22:43.920
if it was approved,

02:22:43.920 --> 02:22:46.950
there needs to be a
diagnosis by a clinician

02:22:46.950 --> 02:22:50.400
that somebody is experiencing dementia.

02:22:50.400 --> 02:22:55.400
And given that diagnosis rates
are around 60 plus percent,

02:22:55.710 --> 02:22:57.870
there is a stretch that in my mind,

02:22:57.870 --> 02:23:00.993
means you've got to get your
diagnosis rates up to 100%.

02:23:00.993 --> 02:23:03.123
Have I missed the point?

02:23:04.170 --> 02:23:06.770
It's more than likely that
I have at this time, but.

02:23:08.339 --> 02:23:12.000
- [John] So, these treatments are...

02:23:12.000 --> 02:23:13.620
Sorry, chair.

02:23:13.620 --> 02:23:16.123
- If you can do make a
note of the questions,

02:23:16.123 --> 02:23:18.390
- Yeah.
- so Simon and then Vin.

02:23:18.390 --> 02:23:19.230
That's right.

02:23:19.230 --> 02:23:21.510
- I think it's quite a
difficult situation actually,

02:23:21.510 --> 02:23:24.120
because the results as you say,

02:23:24.120 --> 02:23:25.650
may even be less than a minimally

02:23:25.650 --> 02:23:27.690
clinically important difference.

02:23:27.690 --> 02:23:30.690
The data isn't that robust.

02:23:30.690 --> 02:23:32.880
It's obviously excited a lot of people,

02:23:32.880 --> 02:23:34.110
because it's a difficult area

02:23:34.110 --> 02:23:36.150
and we've been struggling for some time.

02:23:36.150 --> 02:23:39.022
And the side effects
are not trivial at all.

02:23:39.022 --> 02:23:42.873
And the way in which it has to
be given is also not trivial.

02:23:43.890 --> 02:23:45.720
And answer to your comment, David,

02:23:45.720 --> 02:23:47.507
it may be an awful lot of
people don't want this actually,

02:23:47.507 --> 02:23:50.940
'cause it's got some lethal
side effects as we know.

02:23:50.940 --> 02:23:53.190
And the other thing is it's the first two.

02:23:53.190 --> 02:23:54.750
I didn't realise there were 28 coming.

02:23:54.750 --> 02:23:56.700
I thought there were a dozen.

02:23:56.700 --> 02:24:01.110
And it's very tempting to leap
in when the first two arrive.

02:24:01.110 --> 02:24:04.650
And during COVID, when this realising

02:24:04.650 --> 02:24:07.129
that this was going to
happen, a similar situation,

02:24:07.129 --> 02:24:09.720
they set up that what we
call a platform trial,

02:24:09.720 --> 02:24:12.330
which you have one very good control group

02:24:12.330 --> 02:24:15.060
and you can keep adding
against that control

02:24:15.060 --> 02:24:16.980
and it worked very successfully.

02:24:16.980 --> 02:24:19.170
And this seems to really be calling out

02:24:19.170 --> 02:24:21.750
for a similar approach,

02:24:21.750 --> 02:24:23.340
because we know there's going to be lots

02:24:23.340 --> 02:24:25.800
of other drugs coming, which may be worse.

02:24:25.800 --> 02:24:29.340
Well, unlikely, but they may
be better or just the same.

02:24:29.340 --> 02:24:33.330
And we also have very
little long-term data,

02:24:33.330 --> 02:24:36.180
so we don't know, it seems unlikely

02:24:36.180 --> 02:24:37.440
that's going to actually lead

02:24:37.440 --> 02:24:39.450
to a lasting clinical improvement.

02:24:39.450 --> 02:24:40.800
- Thank you.

02:24:40.800 --> 02:24:41.670
You may comment on that,

02:24:41.670 --> 02:24:43.565
probably might want to
comment on that in a moment,

02:24:43.565 --> 02:24:44.580
but, Vin?

02:24:44.580 --> 02:24:46.200
- Yeah, so two comments, chair.

02:24:46.200 --> 02:24:48.960
So, firstly, I just may
just wanted to make sure

02:24:48.960 --> 02:24:51.390
we don't lose the
significant amount of work

02:24:51.390 --> 02:24:52.410
we're doing in the NHS

02:24:52.410 --> 02:24:53.910
to get the basics right
- Yeah.

02:24:53.910 --> 02:24:55.440
- for people with dementia.

02:24:55.440 --> 02:24:57.540
So, people in hospital general practises

02:24:57.540 --> 02:25:00.660
have training as dementia
friendly friends.

02:25:00.660 --> 02:25:02.400
There are some fantastic examples

02:25:02.400 --> 02:25:03.510
of dementia friendly wards

02:25:03.510 --> 02:25:05.640
in some of our new hospital buildings

02:25:05.640 --> 02:25:07.050
and there are really good partnerships.

02:25:07.050 --> 02:25:09.420
I'm sure Tracey's got some at local level

02:25:09.420 --> 02:25:12.210
with local government and partnerships,

02:25:12.210 --> 02:25:14.640
so that actually, the
whole of the environment

02:25:14.640 --> 02:25:18.513
in the community is supportive of people

02:25:18.513 --> 02:25:21.270
that are unfortunate to have dementia.

02:25:21.270 --> 02:25:22.440
Second point I just wanted to make

02:25:22.440 --> 02:25:23.610
was on the new treatments.

02:25:23.610 --> 02:25:25.350
This is something that
has of huge interest

02:25:25.350 --> 02:25:26.970
to the life sciences industry

02:25:26.970 --> 02:25:28.620
and to the life sciences council,

02:25:28.620 --> 02:25:30.600
is one of the life sciences mission.

02:25:30.600 --> 02:25:32.688
I sit on the life sciences delivery board.

02:25:32.688 --> 02:25:35.580
And one of the things I
think that John's illustrated

02:25:35.580 --> 02:25:37.830
is that firstly, I'm a strong believer

02:25:37.830 --> 02:25:39.644
in the phrase make haste slowly.

02:25:39.644 --> 02:25:41.010
And one of the most

02:25:41.010 --> 02:25:42.780
important things we
should do with this is,

02:25:42.780 --> 02:25:43.890
I think John's demonstrated,

02:25:43.890 --> 02:25:45.916
we are doing a huge amount of planning

02:25:45.916 --> 02:25:48.180
for what potential future

02:25:48.180 --> 02:25:50.220
with these new exciting drugs might hold.

02:25:50.220 --> 02:25:52.953
But we must not also rush into things.

02:25:52.953 --> 02:25:55.560
It's really important we spend
the time developing this.

02:25:55.560 --> 02:25:56.460
So, actually, we end up

02:25:56.460 --> 02:25:58.890
delivering a really effective
service for patients

02:25:58.890 --> 02:25:59.820
and really good delivery

02:25:59.820 --> 02:26:02.370
of these new drugs when
they come on the market.

02:26:02.370 --> 02:26:03.203
- Thank you.

02:26:03.203 --> 02:26:04.050
And then, there's one more comment,

02:26:04.050 --> 02:26:05.313
and then I'll let you respond.

02:26:05.313 --> 02:26:06.690
- Well, I was going to
really comment on Simon,

02:26:06.690 --> 02:26:08.670
because I think you've illustrated

02:26:08.670 --> 02:26:13.087
why John made this point that
we must wait for the MHRA

02:26:15.462 --> 02:26:18.780
- And we are not getting ahead
of ourselves in that sense.

02:26:18.780 --> 02:26:20.310
There is a set of processes to go through

02:26:20.310 --> 02:26:22.830
in terms of regulation and clinical

02:26:22.830 --> 02:26:25.050
and cost-effectiveness.

02:26:25.050 --> 02:26:28.050
But I think the important
thing is seeing the pipeline

02:26:28.050 --> 02:26:30.810
potentially coming that
we do the preliminary work

02:26:30.810 --> 02:26:35.651
and we don't wait for NICE and MHRA.

02:26:35.651 --> 02:26:39.060
We get ahead in terms of
our thinking around this.

02:26:39.060 --> 02:26:41.660
And that's exactly what
John and the team are doing.

02:26:43.027 --> 02:26:44.850
There is clearly the possibility,

02:26:44.850 --> 02:26:47.100
obviously there will be
more trials in these areas.

02:26:47.100 --> 02:26:48.840
The pharma companies will do them there.

02:26:48.840 --> 02:26:50.040
There's always the opportunity

02:26:50.040 --> 02:26:53.520
to either encourage trials through NIHR

02:26:53.520 --> 02:26:56.357
or to do pilots in terms of
the way we introduce these.

02:26:56.357 --> 02:26:58.560
We're doing that for instance
in the obesity drugs,

02:26:58.560 --> 02:26:59.850
which is another area
- Yeah.

02:26:59.850 --> 02:27:01.860
- which potentially could have a lot

02:27:01.860 --> 02:27:03.990
of cost implications to the NHS,

02:27:03.990 --> 02:27:04.823
but clearly,

02:27:04.823 --> 02:27:06.397
could have benefit as well.

02:27:06.397 --> 02:27:09.600
And I think part of the reason
for doing this with dementia,

02:27:09.600 --> 02:27:10.800
it's not the only area

02:27:10.800 --> 02:27:14.580
in which we are going to
see this sort of issue.

02:27:14.580 --> 02:27:16.560
And I think getting ahead

02:27:16.560 --> 02:27:19.260
and working on a standardised approach

02:27:19.260 --> 02:27:21.990
to how we think ahead is as important

02:27:21.990 --> 02:27:24.292
as just the particular
case around dementia.

02:27:24.292 --> 02:27:26.820
- I'm trying to get to the final point,

02:27:26.820 --> 02:27:28.680
but the finance stretch is just flagged

02:27:28.680 --> 02:27:30.990
as he wants to make a comment.

02:27:30.990 --> 02:27:33.843
- I was going to say, Simon's point about,

02:27:35.940 --> 02:27:37.080
can you create something that feels

02:27:37.080 --> 02:27:41.850
closer to a platform
trial would be the ideal.

02:27:41.850 --> 02:27:44.250
Our processes don't entirely allow us

02:27:44.250 --> 02:27:46.840
to get there because of the way we assess

02:27:46.840 --> 02:27:49.980
and almost like, tick
yes, this drug has passed,

02:27:49.980 --> 02:27:52.410
muster for commissioning.

02:27:52.410 --> 02:27:54.750
And I think one of the challenges really

02:27:54.750 --> 02:27:56.910
should be thinking about
with the department,

02:27:56.910 --> 02:28:01.910
with NICE, with NIHR is
honestly within our processes,

02:28:03.240 --> 02:28:05.310
how can we, for something like this,

02:28:05.310 --> 02:28:07.590
create something like a platform trial?

02:28:07.590 --> 02:28:09.068
- Good idea.
- Because if things

02:28:09.068 --> 02:28:10.470
are coming down the line,

02:28:10.470 --> 02:28:13.560
you also want to think
how is it cost-effective

02:28:13.560 --> 02:28:15.540
on treatment versus another different...

02:28:15.540 --> 02:28:17.427
You actually want to look at in the round

02:28:17.427 --> 02:28:20.190
and our processes don't really
allow us to get at that.

02:28:20.190 --> 02:28:23.376
It is one of the things we have discussed,

02:28:23.376 --> 02:28:24.870
but at the moment,

02:28:24.870 --> 02:28:27.720
we can't quite see our way to get there.

02:28:27.720 --> 02:28:29.700
But that would be the ideal.

02:28:29.700 --> 02:28:31.170
- Mark?
- Well, I think,

02:28:31.170 --> 02:28:33.120
you've asked a really
key question, Julian,

02:28:33.120 --> 02:28:35.550
because it seems to me that
we are moving to a world

02:28:35.550 --> 02:28:37.373
where we have such powerful data

02:28:37.373 --> 02:28:39.600
that you can have phase four studies.

02:28:39.600 --> 02:28:42.030
In other words, studies in the real world,

02:28:42.030 --> 02:28:45.063
which should probably
go on for most new drugs

02:28:45.063 --> 02:28:46.517
that are introduced actually.

02:28:46.517 --> 02:28:49.080
'Cause it's only when you use it at scale

02:28:49.080 --> 02:28:51.180
that you suddenly start discovering

02:28:51.180 --> 02:28:54.494
not only the benefits in
a much wider population,

02:28:54.494 --> 02:28:56.340
but also potential side effects

02:28:56.340 --> 02:28:59.136
that weren't recognised in
relatively small trials.

02:28:59.136 --> 02:29:01.530
So, there is the opportunity

02:29:01.530 --> 02:29:04.890
to change the whole
enormously expensive process

02:29:04.890 --> 02:29:07.073
for developing and evaluating new drugs.

02:29:07.073 --> 02:29:08.730
- So, that I think is important-

02:29:08.730 --> 02:29:09.600
- [Mark] For something like this.

02:29:09.600 --> 02:29:11.850
- I think that's a topic
we should take away,

02:29:13.304 --> 02:29:14.137
think about and have a conversation about.

02:29:14.137 --> 02:29:16.515
And obviously, there's a
series of other partners

02:29:16.515 --> 02:29:18.135
if that were to merge

02:29:18.135 --> 02:29:20.670
or we were to seek to cause that to merge.

02:29:20.670 --> 02:29:23.880
So, John, final comments
on any of those comments

02:29:23.880 --> 02:29:24.713
or questions?
- Yeah.

02:29:24.713 --> 02:29:28.080
So, there are a lot of
comments there rather than

02:29:28.080 --> 02:29:29.760
necessarily questions.
- Questions. Yeah.

02:29:29.760 --> 02:29:31.263
- A couple of reflections.

02:29:32.190 --> 02:29:34.050
On the point about the platform trial,

02:29:34.050 --> 02:29:36.407
I think, yeah, that is really important

02:29:36.407 --> 02:29:38.670
and that is something we, a
discussion we've been having

02:29:38.670 --> 02:29:41.187
with colleagues in the
department, with NICE, and so on.

02:29:41.187 --> 02:29:45.030
And I think it is not the case,

02:29:45.030 --> 02:29:50.030
but a NICE technology appraisal process,

02:29:50.250 --> 02:29:51.987
completely excludes for
possibility of that.

02:29:51.987 --> 02:29:53.700
And I think that's something as a system,

02:29:53.700 --> 02:29:55.254
we need to continue to discuss

02:29:55.254 --> 02:29:58.170
and look at options for doing that.

02:29:58.170 --> 02:30:02.670
But nevertheless, we do need
to tackle the first treatments

02:30:02.670 --> 02:30:04.080
that are coming through this process.

02:30:04.080 --> 02:30:06.349
We have a responsibility for doing that.

02:30:06.349 --> 02:30:09.660
But we are doing that
very much with a view

02:30:09.660 --> 02:30:13.710
of the pipeline of '28 on the horizon.

02:30:13.710 --> 02:30:15.090
Some of which are very different,

02:30:15.090 --> 02:30:17.831
different modes of delivery, tablet forms.

02:30:17.831 --> 02:30:21.416
We don't know yet what the
licence requirements will be.

02:30:21.416 --> 02:30:25.272
So, I think it's important
that we do tackle

02:30:25.272 --> 02:30:27.750
for the first coming through,

02:30:27.750 --> 02:30:32.130
but in the context of a broader strategy,

02:30:32.130 --> 02:30:36.030
including commercial
strategy in the future.

02:30:36.030 --> 02:30:40.443
I think on David's point
about diagnosis rates,

02:30:41.790 --> 02:30:44.820
yes, yeah, we will need
to clearly increase

02:30:44.820 --> 02:30:47.129
the clinical diagnosis rates

02:30:47.129 --> 02:30:51.370
and the licences for
these first treatments.

02:30:51.370 --> 02:30:53.431
I think, we'll almost certainly require

02:30:53.431 --> 02:30:57.960
also a kind of confirmatory diagnosis

02:30:57.960 --> 02:31:01.980
of amyloid within, via PET CT

02:31:01.980 --> 02:31:03.420
and lumbar puncture, hence the need

02:31:03.420 --> 02:31:05.877
to expand that capacity.
- Yeah.

02:31:05.877 --> 02:31:09.180
- So, we're absolutely thinking
about those requirements,

02:31:09.180 --> 02:31:13.203
but we also suspect that as new treatments

02:31:13.203 --> 02:31:15.960
that actually can begin
to modify the disease.

02:31:15.960 --> 02:31:18.000
And I think we've already
started seeing that.

02:31:18.000 --> 02:31:19.830
I think more and more patients

02:31:19.830 --> 02:31:22.440
and people are going to be more willing

02:31:22.440 --> 02:31:24.540
and prepared to come forward

02:31:24.540 --> 02:31:27.240
to have those kind of
assessments where currently,

02:31:27.240 --> 02:31:30.300
there is a kind of limited
range of treatment options.

02:31:30.300 --> 02:31:32.130
So, I think the new treatments

02:31:32.130 --> 02:31:36.150
will drive up the diagnosis rate.

02:31:36.150 --> 02:31:37.110
- Thank you.

02:31:37.110 --> 02:31:37.950
Look, having it's a,

02:31:37.950 --> 02:31:39.237
it was a really good paper

02:31:39.237 --> 02:31:41.520
and it was a good
conversation to have actually.

02:31:41.520 --> 02:31:42.720
It's shown I think the complexity

02:31:42.720 --> 02:31:43.830
of some of these judgments,

02:31:43.830 --> 02:31:46.440
and these judgments are made outside us.

02:31:46.440 --> 02:31:51.440
Of course what I take huge
confidence from actually

02:31:51.540 --> 02:31:53.114
and optimism is,

02:31:53.114 --> 02:31:54.630
it's the optimism around

02:31:54.630 --> 02:31:56.520
what you are indicating about pipeline

02:31:56.520 --> 02:31:59.850
of potential medical treatments here.

02:31:59.850 --> 02:32:03.220
Plus the fact that the NHS
is already doing planning,

02:32:03.220 --> 02:32:06.210
thinking about capacity and getting ready.

02:32:06.210 --> 02:32:09.570
And then, the last conversation
with Mark and Steve

02:32:09.570 --> 02:32:11.520
and Julian about is a different way

02:32:11.520 --> 02:32:13.950
to get to a sort of a platform approaches,

02:32:13.950 --> 02:32:14.880
then we should really pick up

02:32:14.880 --> 02:32:18.773
and take up with our partner colleagues.

02:32:18.773 --> 02:32:22.050
And then, I'm going to
finish with one last comment,

02:32:22.050 --> 02:32:24.120
which is you did give the stats

02:32:24.120 --> 02:32:26.640
and I bang on and bang on about the stats

02:32:26.640 --> 02:32:29.280
away from the dementia point
and possible treatments.

02:32:29.280 --> 02:32:33.300
It's this use of our
infrastructure capacity

02:32:33.300 --> 02:32:35.220
and it's that combination of statistics,

02:32:35.220 --> 02:32:39.570
which is that 25% of acute hospital beds,

02:32:39.570 --> 02:32:42.030
1 in 4 hospital beds

02:32:42.030 --> 02:32:44.340
are occupied by people with dementia.

02:32:44.340 --> 02:32:46.680
And 44% of those people are in,

02:32:46.680 --> 02:32:48.840
because of a UTI and chest infection.

02:32:48.840 --> 02:32:52.163
Now, it may not be 100% of that 44%,

02:32:52.163 --> 02:32:54.000
I can see the numbers are
going to get away from me.

02:32:54.000 --> 02:32:57.060
100% of the 44% may still have to be in,

02:32:57.060 --> 02:32:58.677
but if they're in because of a UTI

02:32:58.677 --> 02:33:01.410
and chest infection,
they don't need to be.

02:33:01.410 --> 02:33:04.200
And we run our acute hospitals of what,

02:33:04.200 --> 02:33:07.230
94, 95% capacity all through the summer.

02:33:07.230 --> 02:33:11.160
Imagine if we could just
get and avoid the admission,

02:33:11.160 --> 02:33:12.780
the unnecessary admission

02:33:12.780 --> 02:33:14.944
of half of the half of the people

02:33:14.944 --> 02:33:17.850
who in with the UTI, chest
infection who have dementia.

02:33:17.850 --> 02:33:19.260
And of course they are in for.

02:33:19.260 --> 02:33:23.220
We also know for longer
periods partly because of age

02:33:23.220 --> 02:33:25.770
and partly because of the
nature of the condition.

02:33:25.770 --> 02:33:28.524
And it's a really interesting insight

02:33:28.524 --> 02:33:30.960
into actually how our capacity,

02:33:30.960 --> 02:33:33.116
our acute capacity is
actually being utilised,

02:33:33.116 --> 02:33:37.080
and focusing on that I think,
might create more capacity

02:33:37.080 --> 02:33:38.370
faster than our ability

02:33:38.370 --> 02:33:41.610
to build more physical
capacity or more beds.

02:33:41.610 --> 02:33:44.190
But anyway. It's a really
good paper, good conversation.

02:33:44.190 --> 02:33:47.310
So, let's bring the board to a close.

02:33:47.310 --> 02:33:49.170
I'm just going to quickly
look around the board.

02:33:49.170 --> 02:33:51.120
If anyone's got any other business,

02:33:51.120 --> 02:33:53.433
speak now, raise your hands.

02:33:55.645 --> 02:33:56.910
Otherwise,

02:33:56.910 --> 02:33:57.743
thank you very much.

02:33:57.743 --> 02:33:59.550
We've been going actually eight hours now

02:33:59.550 --> 02:34:00.510
with the two boards

02:34:00.510 --> 02:34:02.370
and you only had less than

02:34:02.370 --> 02:34:04.320
20 minutes of break.
- Yeah.

02:34:04.320 --> 02:34:06.270
- So, thank you for your commitment

02:34:06.270 --> 02:34:08.130
and continued enthusiasm.

02:34:08.130 --> 02:34:10.410
But it's been a really good
discussion, both boards.

02:34:10.410 --> 02:34:12.123
Thank you very much. Thank you.

